export const cases = [
  { id: "c218", name: "Apollo XI Limited v Nexedge Markets Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2240 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-08-28", time: "17:19", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/2240",
    summary: "Judge Cooper granted Nexedge's application to enforce Apollo's cross-undertaking in damages and ordered an inquiry into losses caused by a freezing injunction the court had earlier discharged for breach of the duty of full and frank disclosure, finding credible evidence across Nexedge's claimed loss categories (damage to its FCA permissions application, operational disruption, reputational harm, management time)." },
  { id: "c211", name: "LM1 Limited v Seacroft Film Investments Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2212 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-21", time: "21:24", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2212",
    summary: "HHJ Kelly, sitting in the Business and Property Courts in Leeds (Insolvency and Companies List), ruled on LM1 Limited's application to restrain Seacroft Film Investments Limited from advertising a winding-up petition based on a £20,000 statutory demand arising from a costs contribution under a settlement agreement. Although LM1 technically succeeded in restraining the petition, the judge found LM1's own lack of urgency in particularising its costs position undermined its case, and made no order as to costs, holding both parties' conduct warranted departure from the usual costs rule." },
  { id: "c212", name: "Sanaa El Debs & Ors v Craig Coughlan", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2160 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-20", time: "21:24", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2160",
    summary: "Master Clark, sitting in the Business and Property Courts, granted an application for pre-action disclosure brought by four members of the Debs family, who had invested around US$6m in Drecford Holdings Limited (an ADGM-incorporated company) via loan notes later converted to bonds, against respondent Craig Coughlan, a 50% shareholder/director of Drecford. The applicants allege fraudulent misrepresentation as to the investment's safety and an unlawful conspiracy involving Coughlan and principals of Purple Capital who failed to disclose conflicting interests, after Drecford stopped payments and went silent. The court found the claims sufficiently viable and disclosure of business plans, communications and trading records desirable to allow the applicants properly to particularise their allegations." },
  { id: "c213", name: "Alliance Petrochemical Investment (Singapore) Pte Ltd v Francesco Mazzagatti & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2178 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-08-14", time: "21:24", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/2178",
    summary: "Lionel Persey KC, sitting as a High Court Judge, ruled on multiple applications in API's claim that former CEO Francesco Mazzagatti and a co-defendant misappropriated roughly €143.8m from API via diverted customer payments, profits concealed through intermediaries and unauthorised bank-account withdrawals, allegedly used to help fund the defendants' acquisition of RockRose Energy using forged Abu Dhabi loan-facility documents. The judge dismissed strike-out applications directed at the forgery and Italian-criminal-proceedings allegations, allowed partial non-party disclosure from financial adviser H&P Advisory, but declined to grant the worldwide freezing and proprietary injunctions API sought, finding insufficient evidence of a real risk of asset dissipation." },
  { id: "c196", name: "Mackie Motors (Brechin) Limited v Renault UK Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2182 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-08-17", time: "17:22", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/2182",
    counsel: [],
    summary: "Lance Ashworth KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, dismissed an application by Renault UK, Nissan Motor (GB) and financing subsidiary RCI Financial Services to strike out fresh proceedings brought by former dealer Mackie Motors (Brechin) Limited (MMBL) as a Henderson v Henderson abuse of process, and refused summary judgment on causation. Applying the Johnson v Gore Wood 'broad, merits-based' framework, the judge held claims against Renault and Nissan (never parties to MMBL's unsuccessful 2021-23 proceedings) were not abusive, that a new inducement claim against RCI rested on evidence from a former RCI employee unavailable until after the earlier Court of Appeal ruling, and that a smaller Used Vehicle Stocking Agreement claim survived in the exercise of discretion; MMBL's claims, seeking around £18.6m in damages over RCI's 2021 termination of financing agreements and withdrawal of dealership system access, proceed to trial." },
  { id: "c193", name: "Balbinder Singh Battu v Reflect Canal House Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2106 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-13", time: "09:20", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2106",
    summary: "HHJ Paul Matthews, sitting as a High Court Judge in the Business List, dismissed an application to discharge a worldwide freezing order and continued the injunction against the respondents in a dispute over a residential property-development investment, finding the claimant had a good arguable case across multiple causes of action (breach of trust, fiduciary duty, fraud and conspiracy) and solid evidence of a real risk of asset dissipation. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; the claimant was represented by Justin Perring (instructed by Dutton Gregory LLP) and the first-to-fifth respondents by Marc Brittain (Direct Access)." },
  { id: "c192", name: "Developstate Limited v Alexander Luxury (Yorkshire) Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2170 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-14", time: "09:20", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2170",
    summary: "HHJ Richard Carter, sitting in the Business and Property Courts in Leeds (Insolvency and Companies List), dismissed a debtor company's application to restrain advertisement of a winding-up petition and strike out the underlying claim over unpaid construction-contract invoices totalling £27,341.80, finding no genuine dispute existed absent a Pay Less notice under the Construction Act and rejecting the company's defective-works cross-claim as unsupported by the contemporaneous documentation. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; the petitioning creditor was represented by Mr Lafferty (instructed by Arma) and the debtor company by Ms Parker (instructed by Hamlins)." },
  { id: "c191", name: "James & Anor v HSBC UK Bank Plc", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 88", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-02-17", time: "05:12", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/88",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal (Nugee LJ) dismissed an appeal by Mr and Mrs James against the strike-out of their claim against HSBC UK Bank Plc over an allegedly unauthorised £49,000 loan account opened in Mrs James's name in 2004 and around £25,000 of unauthorised transfers from their joint accounts, holding their fraud claim was statute-barred under the Limitation Act 1980." },
  { id: "c186", name: "Mercuria Energy Trading SA v Baltic Exchange Information Services Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1942 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-07-23", time: "21:12", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1942",
    summary: "Mr Justice Butcher gave case-management directions fast-tracking to an October 2026 trial Mercuria's claim that Baltic Exchange Information Services' TD3C crude-tanker benchmark no longer accurately reflects the underlying freight market following the effective closure of the Strait of Hormuz, with Mercuria alleging losses running into the hundreds of millions of dollars on freight and freight-derivative contracts referencing the route and the Baltic Exchange denying it breached any statutory, contractual or regulatory obligation in compiling the benchmark." },
  { id: "c176", name: "Sucden Financial Limited v TMT Metals AG & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 1080", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-08-10", time: "17:31", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/1080",
    counsel: ["Macfarlanes (Sucden Financial, counsel Jason Robinson KC and Charles Littlewood)"],
    summary: "The Court of Appeal (Sir Geoffrey Vos MR, Laing LJ and Foxton LJ) gave case-management directions in the continuing jurisdiction dispute between Sucden Financial and Mr Prateek Gupta arising from Sucden's fraud claim over an alleged nickel-cargo/margin-debt shortfall exceeding $6.6m. Foxton LJ ordered that Gupta must serve his defence within 28 days of the order being sealed notwithstanding his pending application to the Supreme Court challenging the dismissal of his jurisdiction appeal — his second such challenge — while extending the deadline for a further acknowledgment of service until 14 days after that application is finally resolved, on Sucden's undertaking not to treat compliance as submission to the jurisdiction and to indemnify Gupta's costs if his appeal ultimately succeeds. The ruling balances a defendant's ongoing appellate rights against the claimant's interest in avoiding indefinite delay to substantive proceedings that have already survived two jurisdictional challenges." },
  { id: "c171", name: "Cooke, Young & Keidan LLP v Laurence Howard Davis & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2093 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-05", time: "21:14", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2093",
    summary: "HHJ Johns KC, sitting as a High Court Judge in the Business List, struck out a counterclaim brought by the second defendant, an undischarged bankrupt, in a solicitors' fees dispute, holding the counterclaim had vested in the Official Receiver on his bankruptcy and rejecting arguments that it was instead a partnership asset shared with his co-defendant. The judge separately dismissed the claimant law firm's application for a preliminary issue and refused a 'warehousing' strike-out application against the first defendant, allowing his counterclaim to proceed to trial. The decision turns on the well-established principle that a bankrupt's causes of action vest automatically in their trustee/Official Receiver and cannot be pursued personally absent an assignment back. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; the claimant was represented by Helen Evans KC and Andrew Brown (instructed by Clyde & Co LLP) and the defendants by Francis Moraes (instructed by W Legal Limited)." },
  { id: "c170", name: "Augmentum Fintech Plc, Re", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1333 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-05-11", time: "21:14", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1333",
    summary: "Mr Justice Marcus Smith sanctioned a Part 26 Companies Act 2006 scheme of arrangement under which Augmentum Fintech plc — a London-listed fintech investment trust — would be acquired by Frontier Bidco Limited, a vehicle owned by Verdane Fund Manager AB, for 111 pence per ordinary share in cash. The judge confirmed the statutory requirements for a scheme were satisfied, that shareholders had been fairly represented at the court-ordered meeting, and that the cash price represented a reasonable premium, finding no impediment to sanction. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; the company was represented by Andrew Thornton KC." },
  { id: "c169", name: "Magic Investments SA v Ralph Thierry Broadbent & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 711", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-06-05", time: "21:14", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/711",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal (Newey, Asplin and May LJJ) reversed the lower courts' strike-out of Magic Investments' unfair-prejudice petition under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006 against the directors of The Greater Good Fresh Brewing Co Limited. The court held that a nomination agreement entitling Magic to appoint a director conferred a substantive board seat rather than a bare right to nominate, that an allegedly undervalued share issuance offered pro rata to all shareholders could still found unfair prejudice, and that the respondent's belated share-purchase offer (made almost eight months after the petition, without a costs component) was insufficient to dispose of the petition. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; Magic was represented by Tony Beswetherick KC and Adrian Pay (instructed by Mishcon de Reya LLP) and the first respondent by Nigel Dougherty and Olivia Tolson (instructed by DWF LLP)." },
  { id: "c168", name: "CIT Group Finance (Ireland) Unlimited Company v SpiceJet Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1277 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-06-03", time: "21:14", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1277",
    summary: "Simon Colton KC, sitting as a judge of the Commercial Court, granted aircraft lessor CIT summary judgment against SpiceJet on claims for unpaid rent and redelivery costs under leases of two Boeing 737-8 MAX aircraft. The judge held that clause 13.14 of the parties' early termination agreements entitled CIT to render those agreements void and revert to its original lease-based remedies once SpiceJet breached the renegotiated redelivery terms, rejecting SpiceJet's Braganza-duty and estoppel defences and holding that redelivery acceptance certificates issued under the termination agreements did not bar CIT's lease claims. The judgment gives guidance on 'snapback' clauses in aircraft leasing.",
    counsel: ["Clifford Chance (claimant/CIT)"] },
  { id: "c167", name: "Khurram Shahzad v Lars Skanvig Bramhelft (Re Khurram Shahzad)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1039 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-05-07", time: "21:14", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1039",
    summary: "ICC Judge Agnello KC dismissed Mr Shahzad's application to set aside an order recognising his Danish bankruptcy as foreign main proceedings under the Cross-Border Insolvency Regulations 2006, rejecting his argument that recognition amounted to an impermissible enforcement of Danish tax liabilities. The judge distinguished the case from authorities such as Buchanan and QRS1, where a foreign tax authority was the sole creditor, finding the Danish tax authority represented under 10% of total claims and that distributions would be made pari passu to all creditors. No evidence showed the Danish trustee acted at the tax authority's direction, so the public-policy exception for foreign revenue-law enforcement did not apply. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting." },
  { id: "c166", name: "The State Oil Company of the Republic of Azerbaijan & Ors v Mubariz Mansimov & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2102 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-08-07", time: "17:20", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/2102",
    summary: "Mr Justice Birt dismissed the defendants' application to set aside permission to serve SOCAR's claim out of the jurisdiction, in proceedings seeking to enforce roughly $240m of arbitration awards against Mubariz Mansimov and associated entities. The judge held there was a serious issue to be tried on SOCAR's claims under section 423 of the Insolvency Act 1986 (transactions defrauding creditors) and unlawful-means conspiracy, arising from asset restructurings alleged to have been designed to frustrate enforcement of the awards, but found no serious issue on a separate 'Marex tort' claim since no award existed at the time of the impugned restructuring. England was confirmed as the proper forum. Clifford Chance LLP (instructing Anthony Peto KC, Timothy Lau and Joshua Hillis) acted for the claimants, SOCAR; the defendants were represented by King & Spalding, not on the tracked list.",
    counsel: ["Clifford Chance (claimants/SOCAR)"] },
  { id: "c165", name: "Union Bancaire Privée, UBP SA v L Catterton Europe SAS", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2041 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-08-03", time: "17:20", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.brickcourt.co.uk/news/detail/commercial-court-refuses-pre-action-disclosure-in-investment-fraud-claim",
    summary: "Louise Hutton KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, refused UBP's application for pre-action disclosure ahead of an anticipated deceit claim alleging L Catterton Europe knew that 2023 valuation representations for its investment in clothing brand Jott had become false. The judge found the CPR 31.16 jurisdictional thresholds were met but declined disclosure as a matter of discretion, holding UBP's requests too broad and that ordinary post-issue disclosure would be a more proportionate route. Travers Smith LLP (instructing Tim Lord KC and Mohammud Jaamae Hafeez-Baig) acted for the claimant, UBP.",
    counsel: ["Travers Smith (claimant/UBP)"] },
  { id: "c164", name: "Mohammed Sarwar & Ors v Bilal Sarwar & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2104 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-07", time: "12:22", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2104",
    summary: "HHJ Stephen Davies, sitting as a High Court Judge in the Business & Property Courts in Manchester (Companies List), dismissed an unfair-prejudice petition under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006 brought by the parents and sister of Bilal Sarwar against him and Cambridge Hotel and Lodge Limited, a family hotel company. The petitioners alleged Bilal had wrongfully procured transfer of their combined 75% shareholding — 50% from his parents under an August 2025 'division agreement' and 25% from his sister transferred in 2022 on allegedly temporary terms — but the judge found the division agreement was genuinely agreed and granted declaratory relief confirming Bilal's title. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; the petitioners were represented by Hugo Groves (instructed by Mir Solicitors) and the first respondent by Paul Chaisty KC (instructed by Blacks Solicitors), neither on the tracked list." },
  { id: "c163", name: "Kession Capital Ltd, In the Matter Of", citation: "[2026] EWHC 785 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-04-07", time: "05:13", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/785.html",
    summary: "Deputy ICC Judge Curl KC handed down written reasons for an order made on 3 March 2026 winding up Kession Capital Ltd — an FCA-authorised regulatory host that had faced claims over its promotion of failed collective investment schemes — and replacing its administrators after finding the conduct of a creditors' meeting flawed. The administrators had permitted 25 of 26 unconnected creditors to vote for only nominal amounts while three connected creditors voted at face value, creating what the judge held was an artificial impasse justifying the unconnected creditors' loss of confidence. The court granted the unconnected creditors' application, ordering removal of the administrators and recovery of their costs. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; the applicant creditors were represented by Samuel Parsons, instructed by Acuity Law, neither on the tracked list." },
  { id: "c161", name: "Gladwin v RSM UK Restructuring Advisory LLP & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1043 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-05-07", time: "12:31", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/1043.html",
    summary: "A litigant in person, Mr Gladwin, pursued a professional negligence and fraud claim against the former administrators of Killean Estates Limited, a company he had incorporated to acquire and develop a Scottish estate. Having earlier been placed under an 'unless' order requiring him to file amended points of claim and seek the court's permission (under paragraph 75(6) of Schedule B1 to the Insolvency Act 1986) to bring proceedings against the former administrators, he failed to comply, and the court struck out the claim. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting." },
  { id: "c160", name: "TG Jones High Street Limited and TG Jones Retail Holdings Limited, Re (Sanction — Written Reasons)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2079 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-05", time: "17:26", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2079",
    summary: "Mr Justice Hildyard handed down his written reasons sanctioning the two inter-conditional Part 26A restructuring plans of TG Jones High Street Limited and TG Jones Retail Holdings Limited (the former WH Smith high-street business), confirming the sanction order made after the 1 July 2026 hearing. The plans compromised claims of secured lenders, landlords, suppliers and local authorities across the group's store estate, and the judge exercised cross-class cram-down powers, finding dissenting creditor classes would be no worse off than in the relevant alternative of insolvent administration.",
    counsel: ["Slaughter and May (for the plan companies, counsel Tom Smith KC, Ryan Perkins and Jon Colclough)"] },
  { id: "c141", name: "NW Publishing UK Ltd v Newsweek Publishing LLC", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1882 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-30", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1882",
    summary: "ICC Judge Agnello KC granted an injunction restraining presentation of a winding-up petition over a disputed debt of roughly £301,000 (a rent deposit plus a settlement sum), holding the debt was genuinely disputed on substantial grounds. The creditor was also ordered to pay indemnity costs, underlining the court's readiness to penalise use of the winding-up procedure to pressure a company over a bona fide dispute." },
  { id: "c142", name: "Next Generation Holdings Limited & Anor v Alec Finch & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 1015", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-31", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/1015",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal held that brokers who misappropriated around £3.51m of client money and concealed it through false accounts ahead of a 2017 share sale remained liable for the resulting client-money deficit and associated investigation costs. The court reversed the lower court's award of damages for trading losses, holding those losses did not flow directly from the breach of fiduciary duty." },
  { id: "c143", name: "Peter Waddell Holdco Limited v Bluebell Cars Holding Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2028 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-31", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2028",
    summary: "Mr Justice Marcus Smith heard conjoined claims arising from private-equity investor Freshstream's minority-protection rights over Big Motoring World, acquired via Bluebell Cars Holding in March 2022 alongside a call option and 'Material Default Event' investigation powers. The claims concern whether founder Peter Waddell's summary dismissal for alleged gross misconduct (31 incidents relied on) was lawful, whether Freshstream-appointed directors breached their duties in the MDE investigation and disciplinary process, and whether Step-In Rights notices were validly issued — with Waddell's holding company petitioning that the conduct was unfairly prejudicial under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006.",
    counsel: ["Quinn Emanuel Urquhart & Sullivan UK (for the claimant, Mr Waddell)"] },
  { id: "c144", name: "Quidpay Finance Limited v SettleGo Solutions Limited (t/a OpenPayd)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1991 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-30", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1991",
    summary: "Lance Ashworth KC, sitting as a deputy judge, held that clause 15 of payment-services provider OpenPayd's General Terms of Use — requiring customers to maintain a reserve account against potential liabilities — is a primary obligation that does not survive termination of the contract, so once OpenPayd terminated its relationship with customer QuidPay Finance under clause 22.6 it was obliged to repay QuidPay's reserved funds immediately rather than continuing to hold them. The decision turns on the contractual construction of reserve-account and termination provisions commonly used by e-money and payment-services firms.",
    counsel: ["A&O Shearman (for the defendant, OpenPayd)"] },
  { id: "c145", name: "FW Aviation (Holdings) 1 Limited v VietJet Aviation Joint Stock Company", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1996 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-07-31", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1996",
    summary: "Mr Justice Birt determined the remaining quantum issues in a third trial following the termination of Japanese Operating Lease with Call Option (JOLCO) aircraft-financing leases with VietJet, covering recovery of repair and maintenance costs to restore four aircraft to their required redelivery condition, lost rental income during delays in exporting the aircraft from Vietnam, enforcement costs, and whether FW Aviation could claim under indemnity provisions as successor to the financing parties rather than as aircraft owner. The judgment works through competing constructions of the lease and financing indemnities in a long-running aircraft-repossession dispute.", counsel: ["Quinn Emanuel Urquhart & Sullivan UK (for the claimant)", "King & Spalding International (for the defendant)"] },
  { id: "c138", name: "Conway & Ors v Plass & Ors (Re Argentex LLP)", citation: "[2025] EWHC 3125 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2025-11-27", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2025/3125",
    summary: "The High Court refused the special administrators' application for directions permitting them to close out FX-firm Argentex LLP's unhedged forward and option trading book before maturity, holding that the customer terms' 'own protection' close-out clause could not be construed to protect Argentex from the economic effect of its own insolvency or hedging losses, only from customer-side risk.",
    counsel: ["South Square — Richard Fisher KC (for the 'out of the money' representative party)", "South Square — Marcus Haywood (for the 'in the money' representative parties)"] },
  { id: "c118", name: "Quidpay Finance Ltd v Settlego Solutions Ltd (t/a OpenPayd)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1477 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-06-18", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1477",
    summary: "Preliminary-issue ruling on a payments-provider dispute after OpenPayd suspended QuidPay's accounts and withheld funds, citing alleged fraud linked to QuidPay's clients.",
    counsel: ["Allen Overy Shearman Sterling / A&O Shearman (for the defendant)"] },
  { id: "c120", name: "Christopher James Scanlon v Dane O'Hara & Ors (Nvayo Ltd, Re)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1927 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-27", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1927",
    summary: "Deputy ICC Judge Baister ruled on amendment applications by Nvayo Limited's former CEO and majority unsecured creditor seeking to widen his challenge to the special administrators' remuneration (£1.89m after one year) under the Payment and Electronic Money Institution Insolvency Regulations 2021, including a challenge to 'objective 1' costs brought in his capacity as a customer despite holding only 0.1% of customer claims." },
  { id: "c122", name: "California State Teachers' Retirement System & Ors v Boohoo Group Plc", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1944 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-07-14", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1944",
    summary: "Mr Justice Michael Green gave case-management directions in section 90A/Schedule 10A FSMA investor claims against Boohoo Group plc over alleged misleading statements about supply-chain conditions, selecting reliance-sample representative claimants, approving expert evidence on corporate broking and econometric 'market price reliance' analysis, and declining as premature the claimants' request for early disclosure of documents referenced in reliance forms.",
    counsel: ["Herbert Smith Freehills Kramer (for the defendant, Boohoo Group plc)"] },
  { id: "c123", name: "Importers Service Corporation & Anor v Mario Aliotta & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1969 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-29", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1969",
    summary: "Consequential-matters judgment following the court's earlier substantive ruling on the claimants' section 423 Insolvency Act 1986 application to reverse asset transfers made by Aliotta Holdings Limited; Mr Simon Gleeson found Mr Aliotta had backdated board minutes and fabricated evidence, and awarded the claimants full costs (indemnity costs against all defendants) despite their only partial success on the underlying applications." },
  { id: "c124", name: "Greig William Alexander Mitchell & Anor (joint liquidators of MBI International & Partners Inc) v Sheikh Mohamed Bin Issa Al Jaber & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1947 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-29", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1947",
    summary: "Deputy Master Holden dismissed the First Defendant's application to set aside an order for alternative service (by email to his solicitors) of a possession-and-sale claim brought by the joint liquidators of MBI International & Partners Inc to enforce a Supreme Court judgment and charging order against the defendant's London property, finding good reason for alternative service given the defendant's apparent tactical avoidance and prior failed service attempts at his Paris address." },
  { id: "c125", name: "Philip Gamett v Paul Hughes & Continental Clothing Company Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2004 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-30", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2004",
    summary: "Andrew de Mestre KC (Deputy Judge) refused permission under Companies Act 2006 Part 11 to continue a derivative claim over Continental Clothing Company Limited's alleged entitlement to shares/profits in a German subsidiary, holding the claimant's asserted 2002 oral agreement was contradicted by documentary evidence and that no reasonable director would pursue such a weak claim." },
  { id: "c126", name: "Gallagher Benefit Services Management Company Ltd v Peter Meagher", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1966 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)",
    date: "2026-07-29", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2026/1966",
    summary: "Mr Justice Ritchie heard a dispute over Gallagher's £12.375m acquisition of insurance broker Churchills from Mr Meagher plus a conditional earnout, with Gallagher alleging Meagher failed to disclose that the firm's largest client had given termination notice, inducing Gallagher to settle the earnout at £3.65m rather than roughly £1.8m; Meagher denies misrepresentation.",
    counsel: ["Mayer Brown (for the claimant, Gallagher)"] },
  { id: "c127", name: "Titanium Capital Investment Limited & Anor v Jonathan Hughes & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 976", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-28", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/976",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal (Newey, Snowden and Falk LJJ) affirmed that a 'business connexion' with a supplier constituted a partnership asset, so a partner who exploited that connection post-dissolution via his own companies may be liable to account for profits under section 29 of the Partnership Act 1890, leaving quantum to be determined at trial." },
  { id: "c128", name: "Paul Morgan Evans & Anor v Swansea Building Society", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 978", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-28", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/978",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal (Zacaroli, Miles and Dove LJJ) allowed an appeal on a narrow procedural point — the wrongful refusal of an oral reconsideration hearing on permission to appeal — in a residential-mortgage possession dispute, while dismissing the appellants' underlying challenges to the validity of the charge and to interim relief against sale." },
  { id: "c131", name: "JKD & Anor v IKC", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2031 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-07-31", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/2031",
    summary: "Mrs Justice Dias dismissed a section 68 Arbitration Act 1996 challenge to an arbitral tribunal's procedural order refusing interim relief in a power-plant EPC dispute, where the claimants had sought to restrain calls on performance and advance-payment bonds issued by Santander following a force-majeure termination, holding the order was procedural and unchallengeable.",
    counsel: ["White & Case (for defendant IKC)"] },
  { id: "c134", name: "John Palmer & Anor v P1 Pit Stop Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1924 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-31", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1924",
    summary: "Deputy ICC Judge Parfitt rectified P1 Pit Stop Limited's register of members — which the company had never properly maintained since its 2014 incorporation — restoring an equal 50/50 shareholding between the Palmer and Forland sides (250, 200 and 50 shares of £0.10 nominal value respectively) after disputes over share allotments, a 2018 confirmation statement and the enforceability of alleged trust arrangements." },
  { id: "c137", name: "AXA Insurance UK Plc & Anor v Commissioners of Inland Revenue & Anor", citation: "[2026] UKSC 24", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2026-07-27", area: "funds", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/uksc/2026/24",
    summary: "The Supreme Court held that a Group Litigation Order test-case decision (recognising a restitutionary claim for interest during a tax 'prematurity period') need not bind follower claims once later authority showed the test-case reasoning was wrong in law, confirming courts' CPR 19.23(1)(a) discretion to disapply a superseded GLO test case and that the follower claims' limitation issue must be decided afresh." },
  { id: "c01", name: "Kireeva v Bedzhamov", citation: "[2024] UKSC 39", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2024-12-18", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2024/39.html",
    summary: "Cross-border insolvency and the immovables rule: a foreign bankruptcy trustee cannot reach English land of the bankrupt." },
  { id: "c02", name: "Centrica Overseas Holdings Ltd v HMRC", citation: "[2024] UKSC 25", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2024-07-16", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2024/25.html",
    summary: "Management-expenses deductibility: expenses of a capital disposal were not deductible 'expenses of management'." },
  { id: "c03", name: "RTI Ltd v MUR Shipping BV", citation: "[2024] UKSC 18", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2024-05-15", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2024/18.html",
    summary: "Force majeure and 'reasonable endeavours': a party need not accept non-contractual performance to overcome force majeure." },
  { id: "c04", name: "Lifestyle Equities CV v Ahmed", citation: "[2024] UKSC 17", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2024-05-15", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2024/17.html",
    summary: "Directors' accessory liability: a director is not jointly liable for the company's IP infringement without knowledge of the essential facts." },
  { id: "c05", name: "Wright v Chappell (Re BHS Group Ltd)", citation: "[2024] EWHC 1417 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2024-06-11", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2024/1417.html",
    summary: "Wrongful trading and a novel 'misfeasant trading' measure of loss; applies the Sequana creditor duty at trial." },
  { id: "c06", name: "Re AGPS Bondco Plc (Adler)", citation: "[2024] EWCA Civ 24", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2024-01-23", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWCA/Civ/2024/24.html",
    summary: "First appellate guidance on Part 26A restructuring plans and the cross-class cram down; sets the fair-allocation framework." },
  { id: "c07", name: "Vermilion Holdings Ltd v HMRC", citation: "[2023] UKSC 37", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2023-10-25", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2023/37.html",
    summary: "Employment-related securities: a 'deeming' provision applied so an option was an employment-related security." },
  { id: "c08", name: "R (PACCAR Inc) v Competition Appeal Tribunal", citation: "[2023] UKSC 28", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2023-07-26", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2023/28.html",
    summary: "Litigation funding agreements that take a share of damages are damages-based agreements — unenforceable unless compliant." },
  { id: "c09", name: "Philipp v Barclays Bank UK plc", citation: "[2023] UKSC 25", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2023-07-12", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2023/25.html",
    summary: "No Quincecare duty where a customer personally authorises the payment; reshapes APP-fraud claims against banks." },
  { id: "c10", name: "Republic of Mozambique v Privinvest Shipbuilding SAL", citation: "[2023] UKSC 32", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2023-09-20", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2023/32.html",
    summary: "Scope of arbitration agreements: the bribery/illegality claims fell outside the arbitration clauses." },
  { id: "c11", name: "News Corp UK & Ireland Ltd v HMRC", citation: "[2023] UKSC 7", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2023-02-22", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2023/7.html",
    summary: "VAT zero-rating: digital editions of newspapers were not 'newspapers' for the historic zero-rate." },
  { id: "c12", name: "BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA", citation: "[2022] UKSC 25", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2022-10-05", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2022/25.html",
    summary: "Confirms the directors' 'creditor duty', engaged when insolvency is probable; a sliding scale, not an on/off switch." },
  { id: "c13", name: "Hopcraft v Close Brothers; Johnson v FirstRand Bank; Wrench v FirstRand Bank", citation: "[2025] UKSC 33", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2025-08-01", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2025/33.html",
    summary: "Motor-finance dealers owe no fiduciary or 'disinterested' duty, so bribery and secret-commission claims fail — but an unfair-relationship claim under s.140A Consumer Credit Act can still succeed (as for Mr Johnson)." },
  { id: "c14", name: "Stanford International Bank Ltd v HSBC Bank plc", citation: "[2022] UKSC 34", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2022-11-02", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2022/34.html",
    summary: "An insolvent customer suffered no recoverable loss from a Quincecare breach where payments only reduced its net debt." },
  { id: "c15", name: "Sevilleja v Marex Financial Ltd", citation: "[2020] UKSC 31", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2020-07-15", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2020/31.html",
    summary: "Restates and narrows the reflective-loss rule to claims by shareholders qua shareholders; creditors are not barred." },
  { id: "c16", name: "ClientEarth v Shell plc", citation: "[2023] EWHC 1897 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2023-07-24", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2023/1897.html",
    summary: "Derivative claim against directors over climate strategy refused; courts reluctant to second-guess board judgement." },
  { id: "c17", name: "Lloyd v Google LLC", citation: "[2021] UKSC 50", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2021-11-10", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2021/50.html",
    summary: "Representative 'opt-out' data-protection class action could not proceed without proof of individual damage." },
  { id: "c18", name: "R (KBR Inc) v Director of the Serious Fraud Office", citation: "[2021] UKSC 2", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2021-02-05", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2021/2.html",
    summary: "SFO section 2 notices do not have extraterritorial effect to compel documents held abroad by a foreign company." },
  { id: "c19", name: "Re DeepOcean 1 UK Ltd", citation: "[2021] EWHC 138 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2021-01-28", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2021/138.html",
    summary: "First use of the Part 26A cross-class cram down to bind dissenting creditors to a restructuring plan." },
  { id: "c20", name: "Re Virgin Active Holdings Ltd", citation: "[2021] EWHC 1246 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2021-05-12", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2021/1246.html",
    summary: "Cram down of dissenting landlords sanctioned; court focused on the 'relevant alternative' of administration." },
  { id: "c21", name: "Re Hurricane Energy plc", citation: "[2021] EWHC 1759 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2021-06-28", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2021/1759.html",
    summary: "Plan refused: court would not cram down shareholders where the debt-for-equity swap's 'relevant alternative' was unproven." },
  { id: "c22", name: "Re Houst Ltd", citation: "[2022] EWHC 1941 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2022-07-22", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2022/1941.html",
    summary: "First SME restructuring plan to cram down HMRC as a dissenting preferential creditor." },
  { id: "c23", name: "Re Avanti Communications Ltd", citation: "[2023] EWHC 940 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2023-04-25", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2023/940.html",
    summary: "Assets were subject to a fixed (not floating) charge despite limited control — reopens the characterisation debate." },
  { id: "c24", name: "Invest Bank PSC v El-Husseiny", citation: "[2025] UKSC 2", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2025-02-26", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2025/2.html",
    summary: "Section 423 can reach a transaction dealing with assets owned by a company the debtor controls, not just the debtor's own assets." },
  { id: "c25", name: "Hargreaves Property Holdings Ltd v HMRC", citation: "[2024] EWCA Civ 365", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2024-04-12", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWCA/Civ/2024/365.html",
    summary: "Withholding tax on 'yearly interest': arrangements failed to avoid the obligation; UK-source and short-interest points clarified." },
  { id: "c26", name: "HMRC v Professional Game Match Officials Ltd", citation: "[2024] UKSC 29", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2024-09-16", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2024/29.html",
    summary: "Employment-status test: mutuality of obligation and control within individual engagements can create employment for tax." },
  { id: "c28", name: "Test Claimants in the FII Group Litigation v HMRC", citation: "[2021] UKSC 31", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2021-07-23", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2021/31.html",
    summary: "Long-running cross-border dividend-taxation litigation; limitation and mistake-of-law principles for tax restitution claims." },
  { id: "c29", name: "HMRC v HFFX LLP; Atkins v HMRC", citation: "[2026] UKSC 17", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2026-06-17", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2026/17.html",
    summary: "LLP deferred-remuneration / 'special capital' scheme: trading profits routed through a corporate member were taxable as the individual members' income; marks the limits of purposive tax interpretation." },
  { id: "c30", name: "Saipem SpA v Petrofac Ltd; Re Petrofac Ltd", citation: "[2025] EWCA Civ 821", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2025-07-01", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWCA/Civ/2025/821.html",
    summary: "Court of Appeal overturned the sanction of Petrofac's Part 26A plans: the 'no worse off' test is necessary but not sufficient — the restructuring surplus must be fairly allocated among classes." },
  { id: "c31", name: "Re Thames Water Utilities Holdings Ltd", citation: "[2025] EWCA Civ 475", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2025-03-17", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWCA/Civ/2025/475.html",
    summary: "Court of Appeal upheld the sanction of Thames Water's Part 26A plan, rejecting the argument that 'public interest' favoured special administration over a plan that met the statutory tests." },
  { id: "c32", name: "Saxon Woods Investments Ltd v Costa", citation: "[2025] EWCA Civ 708", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2025-06-04", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWCA/Civ/2025/708.html",
    summary: "Director breached the s.172 duty to promote the company's success by concealing that a contractually-required sale/exit was not being pursued; guidance on good faith and honesty in board conduct." },
  { id: "c33", name: "Re Sino-Ocean Group Holding Ltd", citation: "[2025] EWHC 205 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2025-02-07", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2025/205.html",
    summary: "English Part 26A plan used to compromise Hong Kong-law-governed debt; applies the cross-class cram down where over 75% of a class approved and the relevant alternative was liquidation." },
  { id: "c34", name: "Re Waldorf Production UK plc", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1014 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-05-05", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/1014.html",
    summary: "2026 restructuring-plan sanction applying the post-Petrofac fairness framework to the allocation of the restructuring surplus and the treatment of dissenting creditors." },
  { id: "c35", name: "Byers v Saudi National Bank", citation: "[2023] UKSC 51", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2023-12-20", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKSC/2023/51.html",
    summary: "No liability in knowing receipt where the claimant's equitable proprietary interest was extinguished on transfer of the shares under the governing (Saudi) law." },
  { id: "c36", name: "Dexia SA v Comune di Torino", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1401 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-06-18", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Comm/2026/1401.html",
    summary: "Commercial Court upholds €400m interest rate swap portfolio with the City of Turin, rejecting Italian-law validity challenges at a trial the municipality chose not to attend; confirms ISDA Master Agreement protections and English-court primacy in cross-border swap disputes." },
  { id: "c37", name: "Iconic Sports Eagle Investment, LLC v John Textor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1498 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-06-19", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1498",
    summary: "Commercial Court applies the Snell Equity principle that a party seeking specific performance of a put option for shares need only demonstrate readiness and willingness to perform at the date of the order — not at the contractual completion date — where the defendant has repudiated the contract." },
  { id: "c38", name: "Hipgnosis Music Limited v Merck Mercuriadis", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1500 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-06-23", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1500",
    summary: "Former director diverted a corporate opportunity (music royalty catalogue acquisition business) to newly formed vehicles for personal gain; High Court held he breached the s.175 CA 2006 duty to avoid conflicts and that the successor vehicle and its manager were accessories jointly liable to account for profits." },
  { id: "c39", name: "Bank of India v Firestar Diamond FZE & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1565 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-06-23", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1565",
    summary: "Commercial Court upholds a personal guarantee given by Nirav Modi to Bank of India over obligations of Firestar Diamond FZE, rejecting arguments that Indian foreign-exchange regulations (FEMA) rendered the guarantee void; confirms SOFR replaces synthetic LIBOR as the applicable contractual interest rate after September 2024." },
  { id: "c40", name: "Deutsche Glasfaser Group GmbH, Re (Scheme Sanction)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1563 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-06-24", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1563",
    summary: "Mr Justice Adam Johnson sanctions a creditor scheme of arrangement under s.899 CA 2006 for German fibre-optic group Deutsche Glasfaser (~€7bn debt), bifurcating debt into opco/holdco tranches and injecting €400m super-senior capital; creditors voted 100% in favour; a companion judgment to the convening hearing [2026] EWHC 1467 (Ch) and confirms English court jurisdiction for large cross-border German restructurings." },
  { id: "c41", name: "Musst Holdings Ltd v Astra Asset Management UK Ltd & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1599 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-06-26", area: "funds", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/1599.html",
    summary: "Further judgment in the long-running Musst/Astra litigation (introduction-fee dispute against an AIFM); addresses whether litigation costs incurred in earlier related proceedings can be claimed as a head of damages (costs as damages)." },
  { id: "c42", name: "Gordian Holdings Ltd v Sofroniou", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1602 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)",
    date: "2026-06-26", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/KB/2026/1602.html",
    summary: "Further proceedings in a dispute involving allegations of transactions defrauding creditors under s.423 Insolvency Act 1986 and guarantee enforcement; connected to Bank of Cyprus lending and earlier proceedings at [2021] EWHC 235 (Comm)." },
  { id: "c43", name: "Songa Shipping SE v Gardsea Ltd", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1559 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-06-24", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Comm/2026/1559.html",
    summary: "Commercial Court determines that 'Banking Days' in a cross-border loan agreement must be construed by reference to the time zone of the place of performance of the relevant payment obligation, rejecting the borrower's argument that a 'Banking Day' spans from the opening of business in the first relevant financial centre to the close of business in the last, which would create a notional 37–38-hour day straddling time zones." },
  { id: "c44", name: "Barclays Bank UK plc v Financial Ombudsman Service Ltd", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1555 (Admin)", court: "High Court (Admin)",
    date: "2026-06-23", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Admin/2026/1555.html",
    summary: "Administrative Court grants judicial review of a Financial Ombudsman Service determination that it had jurisdiction over a retail complaint brought more than six months after the firm's final response, holding that the DISP 2.8.2 six-month time-bar is a mandatory jurisdictional precondition the FOS cannot waive otherwise than in accordance with the express terms of DISP." },
  { id: "c45", name: "JSC Commercial Bank Privatbank v Kolomoisky & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1617 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-06-29", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1617",
    summary: "Mr Justice Trower ruled that once the Court of Appeal dismissed the Kolomoisky defendants' appeal against the ~US$3bn PrivatBank judgment, the contractual ring-fencing protecting certain funds from enforcement terminated completely, including for legal fees incurred but unpaid before that date." },
  { id: "c46", name: "Ocorian Trustee (UK) Ltd & Anor v Chandarana & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1635 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-06-30", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1635",
    summary: "The Commercial Court largely discharged proprietary tracing injunctions over ~£9m in misapplied bridging-loan funds, finding most recipients were bona fide purchasers who had given value through accord and satisfaction, save for one defendant with proximity to the lender." },
  { id: "c47", name: "Re Cross Transport Ltd (In Administration)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1636 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-06-30", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1636",
    summary: "ICC Judge Jones held that administrators may pay litigation funder and administration expenses ahead of 'protected moratorium creditors' under the CIGA 2020 moratorium regime, since the statutory 'super-priority' duty does not require absolute pre-payment." },
  { id: "c48", name: "Various Claimants v Entain Plc", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1622 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)",
    date: "2026-06-30", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2026/1622",
    summary: "In a £1.6bn+ FSMA securities-fraud claim over alleged undisclosed bribery in Entain's Turkish operations, Mr Justice Trower (Financial List) ordered a split trial separating defendant-side liability issues from claimant-side reliance, causation and quantum issues." },
  { id: "c49", name: "Black Horse Ltd v Angel & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 831", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-06-30", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/831",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal upheld the use of multi-claimant claim forms for over 5,000 motor finance discretionary commission mis-selling claims against eight lenders, confirming such consolidated case management is a permissible 'convenient disposal' under CPR 7.3." },
  { id: "c50", name: "Commissioners for HMRC v BlueCrest Capital Management (UK) LLP", citation: "[2026] UKSC 18", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2026-07-01", time: "16:32", area: "funds", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/uksc/2026/18",
    summary: "The Supreme Court held that 'significant influence over the affairs of the partnership' under the salaried-members Condition B (Finance Act 2014) must derive from legally enforceable rights under the LLP agreement and statutory framework, not from informal or de facto arrangements, remitting HMRC's ~£142m PAYE/~£55.3m NIC dispute with BlueCrest's LLP members to the tribunal below." },
  { id: "c51", name: "Skatteforvaltningen v MCML Ltd", citation: "[2026] UKSC 19", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2026-07-01", time: "12:18", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/uksc/2026/19",
    summary: "In the long-running cum-ex litigation, the Supreme Court unanimously held that the dismissal of SKAT's 2018 negligence claim (on Revenue Rule grounds) did not give rise to issue estoppel barring its later 2022 fraud claim against the same brokerage over the same tax vouchers, since the two claims involved fundamentally different factual and legal bases." },
  { id: "c52", name: "Houssein & Ors v London Credit Ltd & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 830", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-01", time: "05:20", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/830",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal dismissed an appeal by borrowers against secured lender London Credit Limited arising from a £1.881m loan secured on residential property, holding that the borrowers' settlement offers did not amount to valid tenders capable of stopping interest running, and that the lender's 4%-per-month default interest rate was not an unenforceable penalty given the lender's legitimate refinancing-risk interest." },
  { id: "c53", name: "Culina Group Limited & Ors v Thomas Van Mourik & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1661 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-07-01", time: "05:25", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1661",
    summary: "In a case management conference in litigation brought by Culina Group against former chief executive Thomas van Mourik and other former directors/executives, Andrew Baker J ruled on contested pleading amendments, refusing to found fiduciary duties on a service company but permitting amendments alleging liability to account for an increase in shareholding value as an unsettled point of law for trial." },
  { id: "c54", name: "Gable Insurance AG v William Dewsall & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 851", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-03", time: "12:14", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/851",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal dismissed an insurer (in liquidation)'s appeal against a finding that its former CEO/director's admitted breaches of duty in authorising payments were negligent rather than dishonest, holding the trial judge was entitled to draw a rational distinction between payments diverted from trust accounts (dishonest) and other payments made in the belief they were board-authorised and disclosed to the auditor (not dishonest)." },
  { id: "c55", name: "The Wine Enterprises Investments Scheme Limited (in liquidation) v Crowe U.K. LLP", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1662 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-03", time: "20:05", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1662",
    summary: "Consequential costs judgment following the liquidators' professional-negligence claim against the company's former auditors, in which they recovered only ~£102,000 (1.6%) of their £8.4m claim; the court held the auditors were nonetheless the successful party and awarded them 85% of pre-offer costs and full post-offer costs on the standard basis, refusing indemnity costs." },
  { id: "c56", name: "Philip Henry Cole v Scott William Masterton", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1615 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-07-01", time: "12:25", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1615",
    summary: "Hancock J refused to lift a debarring order against a defendant who had persistently failed to engage with proceedings and, proceeding on the documentary evidence alone, upheld the claimant's claim for £10m under a share purchase agreement the defendant alleged was a sham." },
  { id: "c57", name: "Frontiers Capital I Limited Partnership v Thomas Flohr", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1656 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-06", time: "05:25", area: "funds", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1656",
    summary: "Master Brightwell granted a non-party costs order under s.51 Senior Courts Act 1981 against Timothy Horlick, liquidator of a dissolved venture capital fund's general partner, holding he was the 'real party' who controlled and funded revived litigation against Thomas Flohr for his own benefit." },
  { id: "c58", name: "Stor RB One Ltd v Kanthasamy", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1696 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-07-07", time: "12:20", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1696",
    summary: "Judge Kramer KC declined to strike out a defendant's claim that a personal-guarantee judgment against him should be set aside for fraud (a forged guarantee deed and a fabricated legal-advice certificate produced by a co-defendant relative), finding a real issue for trial, but adjourned the harder question of whether fraud by a co-defendant (rather than the judgment creditor) can unravel a judgment pending Court of Appeal guidance in Lindsay's Estate v Outlook Finance Ltd." },
  { id: "c59", name: "UniCredit Bank GmbH, London Branch v Celestial Aviation Services Ltd", citation: "[2026] UKSC 10", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2026-03-25", area: "banking", url: "https://supremecourt.uk/cases/uksc-2024-0103",
    summary: "The Supreme Court unanimously held that UK Russia sanctions prohibited a confirming bank from paying under standby letters of credit securing aircraft leases to Russian airlines, even though the leases and LCs predated sanctions, adopting a broad 'factual connection' test for the sanctions regime's reach." },
  { id: "c60", name: "THG Plc v Zedra Trust Company (Jersey) Limited", citation: "[2026] UKSC 6", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2026-02-25", area: "corporate", url: "https://supremecourt.uk/cases/uksc-2024-0047",
    summary: "The Supreme Court gave authoritative guidance on whether, and what, statutory limitation period applies to amendments to unfair-prejudice petitions under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006." },
  { id: "c61", name: "DBLP Sea Cow Limited v Lars Stefensen", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1735 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-09", time: "21:14", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1735",
    summary: "Mr Justice Mellor committed Lars Stefensen to two years' imprisonment for contempt of four court orders (a proprietary injunction, an information order, a debarring order and a post-judgment injunction) after he sold 2.5 million Rezolve AI plc shares he held on trust pending payment, without authorisation, and refused to disclose the proceeds' whereabouts." },
  { id: "c62", name: "Hamad Al Haroun v Ahmad Al Fahad Al Ahmad Al Sabah (Kuwait Finance House PLC)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1669 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)",
    date: "2026-07-08", time: "21:14", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2026/1669",
    summary: "David Quest KC (sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge) dismissed applications by Kuwait Finance House PLC to set aside a contempt order and default judgment, finding that although the claimant had manipulated a delivery-confirmation document, genuine service of enforcement documents on KFH had nonetheless occurred through multiple courier/postal deliveries which the bank failed to process internally." },
  { id: "c63", name: "Illiquidx Limited v Altana Wealth & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 874", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-10", time: "12:18", area: "funds", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/874",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal (Arnold, Zacaroli and Miles LJJ) dismissed an appeal against findings that Altana Wealth misused confidential information IlliquidX had disclosed under NDA about a strategy to launch a sanctions-compliant fund investing in distressed Venezuelan sovereign debt, rejecting the appellants' argument that the strategy was in the public domain." },
  { id: "c64", name: "Loudmila Bourlakova & Ors v Semen Anufriev & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1747 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-10", time: "21:25", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1747",
    summary: "The High Court ruled on an application to lift legal privilege under the 'iniquity exception' in a $3bn+ dispute over the late Oleg Bourlakov's business interests, a significant asset-tracing/fraud dispute involving complex corporate structures; trial is listed for October 2027." },
  { id: "c65", name: "Tether Investments, S.A. de C.V. & Anor v Electric Solidus, Inc. (t/a Swan Bitcoin)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1652 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-06-26", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1652",
    summary: "The Commercial Court refused anti-suit relief sought to restrain a BVI derivative action, holding that a litigation-restriction clause in a shareholders' agreement does not bar derivative claims brought on behalf of the company — relevant to governance disputes in crypto/digital-asset joint ventures." },
  { id: "c66", name: "JJH Enterprises Ltd (t/a ValueLicensing) v Microsoft Corporation & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 872", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-07", time: "21:18", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/872",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal dismissed Microsoft's appeal in a Competition Appeal Tribunal follow-on damages claim over the secondary market for used software licences, holding the CAT has jurisdiction to determine copyright questions arising within a competition claim, that the Software Directive's exhaustion principle extends to non-program works incidental to software, and that bulk licences can lawfully be subdivided and resold." },
  { id: "c67", name: "Jeremy Robert Webb & Anor v Eversholt Rail Limited & Anor (Re Eversholt Rail (365) Limited)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 101 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-01-22", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/101",
    summary: "Sir Anthony Mann dismissed liquidators' appeal seeking 'everything forever' disclosure from a sister company under sections 235-236 of the Insolvency Act 1986, holding that reconstituting corporate knowledge does not by itself justify unlimited, unparticularised document requests." },
  { id: "c68", name: "Re Equity for Growth (Securities) Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 696 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-03-25", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/696",
    summary: "ICC Judge Agnello KC wound up a financial-services firm on a rare contested FCA petition, holding that its combative stance to c.£2.7m of consumer redress claims independently satisfied the 'just and equitable' ground under section 367(3) FSMA 2000, alongside insolvency." },
  { id: "c69", name: "BCNO Limited v Cooke", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1263 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-06-02", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1263",
    summary: "HHJ Paul Matthews held that section 117 of the Companies Act 2006 gives a company no jurisdiction to seek a 'no-access' order where the underlying section 116 request for its register of members was itself non-compliant, and queried his own earlier reasoning in Sir Henry Royce Memorial Foundation v Hardy." },
  { id: "c70", name: "Van Rooyen & Ors v The Respondents Listed in the Schedule of Respondents (Re Mirror Trading International (Pty) Ltd)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1286 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-05-29", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1286",
    summary: "Mr Justice Leech dismissed a strike-out application against South African liquidators' section 426 transaction-avoidance claims arising from the Mirror Trading International cryptocurrency Ponzi scheme, holding that South African limitation defences raised disputed foreign-law questions unsuitable for summary disposal." },
  { id: "c71", name: "Lyons v Bridging Finance Inc", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1388 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-06-09", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1388",
    summary: "Mr Justice Rajah upheld a bankruptcy order against a Bahamas-resident debtor, holding that a single substantial property development and letting in London sufficed to constitute 'carrying on business' in England under section 265 of the Insolvency Act 1986." },
  { id: "c72", name: "CC/Devas (Mauritius) Ltd & Ors v The Republic of India", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 797", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-06-24", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/797",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal held that a state's ratification of the New York Convention 1958 does not, by itself, waive sovereign immunity from enforcement, rejecting an attempt to enforce €195m in arbitral awards against India in England." },
  { id: "c73", name: "Nexans France SAS & Anor v London Array Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 887", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-10", time: "05:21", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/887",
    summary: "Lord Justice Green refused Nexans permission to appeal the Competition Appeal Tribunal's ruling that London Array's offshore-windfarm joint venture was overcharged for submarine power cables supplied during a 2008–09 tender, as part of the EU Commission's submarine-cable cartel." },
  { id: "c74", name: "ICG Manager Limited & Ors v Colliers International Valuation UK LLP", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1749 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-13", time: "17:24", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1749",
    summary: "Sitting as a deputy judge, David Halpern KC permitted amendments to a negligence claim by lender-side claimants against valuer Colliers International Valuation UK LLP over an allegedly negligent January 2018 property valuation that the claimants say prevented them identifying a breach of a loan-to-value covenant and averting subsequent losses, allowing pleaded amendments that Colliers negligently revised its valuation from £22.41m to £23.75m after borrower contact while rejecting amendments alleging intentional wrongdoing." },
  { id: "c75", name: "Malcolm Cohen & Anor v Co-Operative Group Limited & Ors (Re The Food Retailer Operations Limited)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1228 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-05-21", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1228",
    summary: "Mr Justice Cawson dismissed a c.£205m claim brought by the joint liquidators of The Food Retailer Operations Limited (formerly Somerfield Stores Limited) against Co-operative Group Limited and associated entities, holding that the 2015 'Project Chicago' restructuring — under which Co-op withdrew roughly £478m of share capital from Somerfield after transferring its profitable stores to Co-op subsidiaries — did not constitute a transaction at an undervalue or a preference under sections 238/239 of the Insolvency Act 1986, since the share capital withdrawal was supported by consideration from the shares' original subscription." },
  {"id":"c80","name":"Ron Hoffman & Anor v Finalto Group Limited & Anor","citation":"[2026] EWHC 1702 (Comm)","court":"High Court (Comm)","date":"2026-07-06","area":"corporate","url":"https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1702","summary":"Mr Justice Butcher resolved consequential matters following an earlier trial judgment on breached management-equity claims valued at roughly GBP22.8m, awarding pre-judgment interest at base+4%, splitting costs to reflect a Part 36 offer, and refusing permission to appeal."},
  { id: "c81", name: "Saxon Woods Investments Limited & Ors v Francesco Costa", citation: "[2026] UKSC 21", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2026-07-14", time: "17:25", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/uksc/2026/21",
    summary: "The Supreme Court unanimously dismissed an appeal by Francesco Costa, chairman of Spring Media Investments Ltd, holding that the section 172(1) Companies Act 2006 duty of good faith governs a director's conduct, not just his subjective belief, so a director cannot secretly defy a collective board decision through concealment or deception. It upheld the Court of Appeal's order requiring Costa to buy out Saxon Woods' shareholding at its undiscounted December 2019 valuation." },
  { id: "c82", name: "West One Loan Ltd v Okroyan", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1428 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-06-11", time: "05:19", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/1428.html", summary: "Edwin Johnson J held that a mortgage lender, West One Loan Ltd, was entitled to possession of a Virginia Water property despite the borrower's designation as a UK-sanctioned person under the Russia (Sanctions) (EU Exit) Regulations 2019. The borrower argued that because sanctions law made it unlawful for her to repay the debt, her repayment obligation — and any breach founding possession — was suspended. The court accepted the argument's intellectual coherence but declined to extend it to possession proceedings, holding the lender could enforce its security provided it acted under its OFSI licence." },
  { id: "c83", name: "Breeze & Ors v TSB Bank PLC", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 32", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-01-30", time: "05:19", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWCA/Civ/2026/32.html", summary: "The Court of Appeal (Newey, Baker and Arnold LJJ) dismissed an appeal by 392 former Northern Rock 'Whistletree' mortgage customers in group litigation against TSB, upholding two preliminary rulings in TSB's favour. It held that, on the true construction of the mortgage terms, TSB's acquisition of the Northern Rock loan portfolio did not oblige it to charge interest by reference to one of its own pre-existing standard variable rates rather than the Whistletree SVR, and that section 140A(5) of the Consumer Credit Act 1974 precluded certain unfair-relationship claims tied to a separate credit agreement." },
  { id: "c84", name: "IBP Markets Ltd, Re (Investment Bank Special Administration Regulations 2011)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1790 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-15", time: "17:19", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1790", summary: "In the special administration of IBP Markets Ltd, an FCA-regulated broker, Mr Justice Hildyard refused a creditor's (All Blue Investments North Star 1 Ltd, in liquidation) application for permission to adduce expert evidence on trading-data patterns supporting its claim to £21.2m as client money under the FCA's CASS rules, which the joint special administrators dispute as having been transferred under a title transfer collateral arrangement. He held the evidence had not been shown to be 'reasonably necessary' under CPR 35.1 given the dispute turns on contractual construction and fact-finding, but left open a renewed application supported by a draft expert report by 15 September 2026.", counsel: ["Freshfields (for All Blue Investments North Star 1 Limited)"] },
  { id: "c85", name: "ICG Manager Ltd & Ors v Colliers International Valuation UK LLP (Re Consequential Matters)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1788 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-15", time: "17:19", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1788", summary: "Follow-on ruling to [2026] EWHC 1749 (Ch) (c74) on consequential matters arising from the court's permission to amend the Particulars of Claim. Mr David Halpern KC, sitting as a deputy High Court judge, fixed the timetable for amending the Defence and Reply at a forthcoming Pre-Trial Review rather than immediately, and — while the claimants had obtained most of the amendments sought — awarded them only half their costs because multiple drafting iterations had unnecessarily increased both parties' costs, with the defendant recovering its own costs of dealing with the amendments." },
  { id: "c86", name: "Emerging Media Ventures Ltd & Anor v Kundra & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1803 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)", date: "2026-07-16", time: "17:20", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2026/1803", summary: "Griffiths J granted summary judgment for the claimants in a dispute over an 11.7% shareholding in Sporting Holdings, owner of the Rajasthan Royals IPL cricket franchise. Following a 2015 finding by India's Supreme Court that Ripu Sudan Kundra ('Raj Kundra') had committed IPL betting offences, he transferred his shares under a 2015 Share Transfer Agreement and a 2019 Settlement Agreement under which he received payments in exchange for undertakings never to claim franchise interests or sue the shareholders. The claimants terminated the settlement in July 2025 alleging Kundra breached its non-disparagement terms, and the court granted declarations on the validity of both agreements, ordered repayment of US$4,937,887 plus interest, and granted injunctions enforcing the non-disparagement/non-interference clauses together with a permanent anti-suit injunction against related Indian proceedings." },
  { id: "c87", name: "Windhorst v ADS Securities LLC", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 905", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-07-17", time: "12:10", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/905", summary: "The Court of Appeal (Fraser and Newey LJJ) dismissed Lars Windhorst's appeal against an 18-month suspended committal for contempt of court, upholding findings that his repeated failure to attend CPR Part 71 examination hearings — ordered to enforce a judgment debt of around €27.2m owed by insolvent Dutch holding company Tennor Holding BV, of which he was controlling shareholder and CEO — breached both a court order and his own undertaking. The court confirmed the sanction was proportionate and that a judge has power to impose a positive document-production condition as a term of a suspended committal sentence." },
  { id: "c88", name: "Together Commercial Finance Limited & Ors v Matthew Stuart Slack & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1732 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-17", time: "17:20", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1732", summary: "HHJ Klein (sitting as a High Court judge) held that a Debt Respite Scheme mental-health crisis moratorium obtained by a guarantor did not prevent receivers, appointed by a secured lender on default of a corporate loan, from proceeding to sell the guarantor's charged property, because the moratorium protects only 'qualifying debts' owed personally by the debtor and not the underlying corporate debt the charge secures." },
  { id: "c89", name: "Cedar Mundi (Holding) SAL v Attieh & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1820 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-17", time: "21:04", area: "funds", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1820", summary: "Mr Justice Bryan found in favour of a Lebanese venture-capital fund vehicle in a dispute over the transfer of substantially all of its investment portfolio, first to a related party and then, under a 2021 English-law sale-and-purchase agreement, to a further fund vehicle, holding the transfers breached the former director's fiduciary duties and were not properly authorised." },
  { id: "c90", name: "Yieldpoint Stable Value Fund, LP v Quintar Kimura Special Credit Fund Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1558 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-01", time: "12:03", area: "funds", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1558", summary: "Rosalind Phelps KC, sitting as a judge of the Commercial Court, determined the fair market value of a matured loan-participation redemption owed by Quintar Kimura Special Credit Fund Limited to Yieldpoint Stable Value Fund, LP following the underlying Chilean mining borrower's default, fixing recovery at US$3.115m against a US$5m principal by reference to the fund's own contemporaneous credit and recovery assessments." },
  { id: "c91", name: "Elevate Estates Management Ltd v Fine Star Ltd", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1831 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-13", time: "17:11", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1831", summary: "Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs dismissed applications by residential property manager Elevate Estates Management Ltd for a validation order under section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986 and to restrain advertisement of a winding-up petition presented by its landlord, Fine Star Ltd, after rent arrears on 51 leases reached £1,120,586.87 by 7 July 2026. The judge found no genuine or substantial dispute as to the debt and rejected Elevate's unsubstantiated cross-claim for repair costs, ordering Elevate to pay indemnity costs." },
  { id: "c92", name: "Glas Sas (London Branch) v European Topsoho S.a r.l. & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1759 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-14", time: "21:06", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1759", summary: "Peter MacDonald Eggers KC held that a purported 2018 security agreement and related 2021 transfer notices, relied on to justify moving unpledged SMCP shares worth roughly €85m out of insolvent Luxembourg bond issuer European Topsoho for a nominal €1, were not genuine contemporaneous documents but were fabricated after the event to dress up an unauthorised disposal of the bond trustee's collateral." },
  { id: "c93", name: "Moorwand Ltd v Hamblin & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 942", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-07-21", time: "12:13", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/942", summary: "The Court of Appeal allowed an appeal by electronic-money institution Moorwand Ltd against a ruling that it must restore £160,000 to the account of RND Global Ltd, a shell company used by fraudsters to trick the Hamblins into an authorised-push-payment transfer, holding the trial judge's finding that Moorwand was not put on inquiry about the payment instructions was reasonable and met the high threshold for appellate interference with a Quincecare-duty assessment." },
  { id: "c94", name: "Three R Tourism and Hospitality Limited & Ors v Orrick Herrington & Sutcliffe (UK) LLP", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1844 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)", date: "2026-07-21", time: "12:13", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2026/1844", summary: "Mr Justice Cotter dismissed a law firm's strike-out and summary-judgment application in a professional-negligence claim over advice on Carlyle Group's investment transaction acquiring shares in Wakanow.com Limited, holding that actionable loss arose when the relevant agreements were signed in November 2018 rather than on later conversion-notice service, leaving the claimants needing to succeed on constructive-knowledge arguments under section 14A of the Limitation Act 1980 to avoid being time-barred.", counsel: ["Herbert Smith Freehills Kramer (defendant)"] },
  { id: "c95", name: "TAQA Bratani Ltd & Ors v Fujairah Oil and Gas UK LLC & Ors", citation: "[2025] EWCA Civ 1669", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2025-12-19", time: "12:13", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2025/1669", summary: "The Court of Appeal (Falk, Newey LJJ and Sir Geoffrey Vos MR) held that a c.US$84m dividend paid by an insolvent oil company shortly before its US$1 sale was itself the relevant 'transaction' for section 238 Insolvency Act 1986 purposes - not the wider disposal of which it formed part - and that the section 238(5)(b) defence (reasonable grounds for believing the transaction would benefit the company) must be assessed from that company's own perspective, not its parent's or shareholders', tightening the defence available to insolvent groups structuring pre-sale dividends." },
  { id: "c96", name: "Salinas Pliego & Anor v Astor Asset Management 3 Ltd & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 940", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-07-21", time: "17:08", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/940", summary: "In the long-running stock-lending dispute over ~US$115m of loans secured on ~US$415m of Grupo Elektra shares, the Court of Appeal (Males, Phillips and Foxton LJJ) upheld a finding that the claimants' use of private investigators (Black Cube) to run a covert sting on the defendants' solicitor, obtaining privileged litigation-strategy information by deception, was a serious abuse of process, and dismissed their appeal against the striking-out of their summary judgment application while allowing the defendants' cross-appeal discharging all freezing orders." },
  { id: "c97", name: "Ciright Inc v Centili Group Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1865 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-22", time: "12:00", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1865", summary: "Mr David Rees KC, sitting as a deputy High Court judge, continued an interim proprietary injunction protecting c.US$3.4m Ciright transferred into Centili's bank account in January 2026, which Ciright says is held on bare trust and Centili says was a loan advance under a facility agreement; despite finding material non-disclosure by Ciright on the application, the judge preserved the injunction pending trial and imposed penal costs sanctions instead of discharging it." },
  { id: "c98", name: "China Triumph International Engineering Co Ltd v WeLink Global Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1872 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-22", time: "12:00", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1872", summary: "Paul Stanley KC, sitting as a deputy High Court judge, allowed most of the defendants' proposed amendments in a c.£17.2m dispute over debts said to have been assumed under April 2019 loan agreements financing three UK solar-farm projects, but refused to join two further companies as additional defendants to advance related counterclaims, holding they should pursue any claims independently rather than complicate the existing proceedings." },
  { id: "c100", name: "BS Enterprises Limited, Re (Saini v Pandher)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1851 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-22", time: "21:05", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1851", summary: "ICC Judge Greenwood upheld an unfair-prejudice petition under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006 brought by a 50% shareholder of a property-holding company, finding the respondent director breached her fiduciary duties by letting the company's pub premises to her own connected companies at undervalue without formal agreements, creating undisclosed conflicts of interest, and excluding the petitioner from management, and ordered a share buyout." },
  { id: "c101", name: "Re Argo Blockchain plc", citation: "[2025] EWHC 3395 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2025-12-23", time: "12:13", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2025/3395", summary: "Hildyard J sanctioned the Part 26A restructuring plan of Argo Blockchain plc — the first English restructuring plan used for a Nasdaq-listed company and the first for a crypto-mining business — restructuring US$40m of senior unsecured notes via a 'Growler' equity-for-debt structure, applying cross-class cram-down and addressing US Chapter 15 recognition and allocation of restructuring surplus to out-of-the-money shareholders.", counsel: ["South Square (Plan Company, instructed by Fladgate)"] },
  { id: "c102", name: "Health and Home Ltd & Anor v Elite Property Holdings Ltd & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 933", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-07-23", time: "17:11", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/933", summary: "The Court of Appeal (King, Arnold and May LJJ) upheld a refusal to grant relief from sanctions to a respondent who served witness evidence eight days late in an insolvency application, holding that the final hearing of an insolvency application — with its full disclosure, pleadings, witness statements and cross-examination — qualifies as a 'trial' for CPR 32.10 purposes, so strict compliance with directions is required.", counsel: ["Simmons & Simmons (respondents/liquidators)"] },
  { id: "c103", name: "Patrick Hughes v Martin Bellamy & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1871 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-23", time: "21:13", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1871", summary: "Mr Justice Trower refused a 50% shareholder in Mulberry Ltd permission to bring a double-derivative claim on behalf of AI Pathfinder Inc over a January 2026 £17.7m sale of its assets to a management-buyout vehicle, holding the claimant failed to show a prima facie undervalue, that any defendant profited dishonestly, or that the alleged wrongdoers controlled AIP in the manner needed to satisfy the Foss v Harbottle exceptions." },
  { id: "c104", name: "Al Othman Holding Company v Al Rajhi Holding WLL", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 949", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-07-23", time: "21:13", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/949", summary: "The Court of Appeal (Males, Phillips and Lewison LJJ) allowed an appeal on whether a November 2018 email acknowledging 'the amount due' under a US$5m interest-free loan was a valid acknowledgment of the debt under the Limitation Act 1980, holding a reasonable recipient would read it as acknowledging liability despite an ambiguous reference to a share sale that never occurred." },
  { id: "c105", name: "Al-Bitar & Anor v Byblos Bank SAL", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1807 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)", date: "2026-07-17", time: "09:17", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2026/1807", summary: "Deputy High Court Judge O'Mahony held that an account the claimants used to receive rental income from their property-letting business was opened and operated for business purposes, not personal consumption, so the consumer-contract jurisdictional gateway in the Civil Jurisdiction and Judgments Act 1982 did not apply, and the Lebanese bank's exclusive-jurisdiction clause in favour of the Lebanese courts stood." },
  { id: "c106", name: "De Menezes v Alves & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1906 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-24", time: "12:04", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1906", summary: "Mr Justice Fancourt granted a former co-director permission to continue a derivative claim on behalf of Oficina Inglesa Limited, a Chelsea Harbour luxury-furniture business, against his former business partner for breach of the no-conflict duty under section 175 of the Companies Act 2006, holding that operating a competing furniture venture from the same showroom after their relationship broke down created an obvious conflict, that a real risk of substantial harm to the company was established even absent quantifiable loss, and that no valid board authorisation or ratification had occurred via informal WhatsApp exchanges." },
  { id: "c107", name: "Float Capital Ltd, Re", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1891 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-24", time: "12:04", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1891", summary: "Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs approved the joint liquidators' remuneration on Float Capital Limited's transition from administration to creditors' voluntary liquidation, fixing one liquidator's fees on the time-costs basis under rule 18.23 despite procedural irregularities (creditor approval being unavailable due to conflict of interest), while holding the other liquidator's basis was already deemed fixed as continuing office-holder under rule 18.20(4) so his increased remuneration fell to be approved under rules 18.24/18.28 instead, and imposing estimate-based safeguards and continued SIP 9 reporting." },
  { id: "c108", name: "Soprim Construction SARL v Republic of Djibouti & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1850 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-24", time: "12:04", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1850", summary: "Mr Justice Picken held that Doraleh Container Terminal SA held approximately US$41-42m in Standard Chartered Bank London accounts on bare trust for the Republic of Djibouti, following an agreement with the Republic's court-appointed administrator, enabling judgment-creditor Soprim Construction to pursue charging-order enforcement against those funds in partial satisfaction of arbitration awards exceeding US$100m against Djibouti arising out of the Doraleh port dispute." },
  { id: "c109", name: "Smithers & Anor v Persons Unknown Category 1 & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1907 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-24", time: "17:10", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1907", summary: "Mr Justice Bright granted summary judgment for deceit to two claimants who had invested roughly £10.5m in what they believed was a legitimate London-run cryptoasset trading business (Raliplen/Servelius) but which was in fact a fraudulent scheme, ordering the unrepresented 'persons unknown' defendants controlling the receiving cryptoasset addresses to return misappropriated Bitcoin in specie and to pay compensation for fungible USD Coin and Ethereum, plus indemnity costs of £230,000." },
  { id: "c110", name: "Rice v Napril Holding Ltd", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1897 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)", date: "2026-07-24", time: "17:10", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2026/1897", summary: "Mr Justice Sweeting granted the claimant summary judgment in a share purchase agreement earn-out dispute following the sale of Core Management Group Limited (an umbrella payroll company), holding that the SPA's definition of 'New Revenue' for calculating 'Stretch Value Payments' extended to all revenue from ordinary business activity, including renewals by existing clients, and that the buyer's narrower construction excluding renewal revenue had no realistic prospect of success at trial." },
  { id: "c111", name: "Xiong Wei & Anor v Wang Junhong", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1892 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-24", time: "21:22", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1892", summary: "Mr Andrew Hochhauser KC enforced a US$2.13m Chinese judgment against the defendant in a shareholder dispute over entitlement to shares in Syner Ltd, holding the judgment was final and conclusive despite a pending Chinese supervisory retrial application and that no breach of natural justice arose from the defendant's strategic withholding of auditor evidence." },
  { id: "c112", name: "E J W Builders Ltd & Anor v Marshall & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 911", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-07-20", time: "17:17", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/911", summary: "The Court of Appeal (Falk, Warby and Miles LJJ) dismissed an appeal against HHJ Paul Matthews' dismissal of a claim that a partnership, or alternatively a contractual joint venture, existed between a building contractor and hotel owner-developers over the redevelopment of the former Hilbury Court Hotel in Trowbridge, applying appellate-deference principles to an ex tempore trial judgment." },
  { id: "c113", name: "Kession Capital Ltd (in Liquidation) v KVB Consultants Ltd and others", citation: "[2026] UKSC 11", court: "Supreme Court", date: "2026-04-01", time: "09:20", area: "banking", url: "https://supremecourt.uk/cases/press-summary/uksc-2024-0114", summary: "The Supreme Court unanimously allowed Kession Capital's appeal, holding that a principal firm's liability under section 39(3) FSMA 2000 for the acts of its appointed representative does not extend to a distinct 'part' of business the AR was expressly barred from conducting. Kession's appointed-representative agreement restricted its AR, Jacob Hopkins McKenzie, to professional clients only, so Kession was not responsible for JHM's unauthorised dealings with retail clients. The decision overturns the Court of Appeal majority and gives principal firms a clearer basis for contractually limiting section 39 exposure by client category." },
  { id: "c114", name: "Webster & Anor v ESMS Global Ltd & Ors", citation: "[2025] EWHC 3107 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2025-11-25", time: "21:10", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2025/3107", summary: "HHJ Cadwallader, sitting as a judge of the High Court, held that shareholders have an enforceable right under sections 292-293 of the Companies Act 2006 to apply to court for an order compelling a deadlocked company to circulate a members' proposed written resolution — including one seeking to appoint an additional director — where the board declines or fails to do so, granting the claimants' application in a case where two equal 47.6%-shareholder factions were unable to agree." },
  { id: "c116", name: "Secretary of State for Business and Trade v Greensill", citation: "[2026] EWHC 639 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-03-18", time: "09:09", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/639.html", summary: "Mr Justice Trower dismissed an application by Alexander David Greensill to strike out director-disqualification proceedings brought against him over the insolvencies of Greensill Capital (UK) Limited and Greensill Limited, holding that the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 does not require the Secretary of State to prove as a threshold issue that the alleged misconduct caused the companies' insolvency, and rejecting Mr Greensill's fair-process challenge to how the case had been prepared for trial." },
  { id: "c117", name: "Gardner Aerospace Holdings Ltd v Upton", citation: "[2026] EWHC 555 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-03-20", time: "09:16", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/555.html", summary: "Mr Justice Richard Smith held that Gardner Aerospace's former interim CEO and CFO, Mr Upton, breached his statutory duties under sections 171, 172 and 175 of the Companies Act 2006 by unilaterally lobbying MPs to subject a board-approved debt-for-equity restructuring transaction — increasing a Chinese state-owned investor's stake in the group's parent — to closer governmental scrutiny, without board authority and in pursuit of his own agenda for investment and job guarantees." },
  { id: "c149", name: "Sucden Financial Limited v TMT Metals AG & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 986", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-07-31", time: "21:20", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWCA/Civ/2026/986.html", summary: "The Court of Appeal upheld jurisdiction over Sucden's fraud/deceit claim that it was induced to forbear enforcing a debt over an allegedly fraudulent nickel-cathode bill of lading, applying the Tort Damage Gateway per Dolphin Maritime v Swedish Club on the basis the debt was payable — and the loss therefore sustained — in England.", counsel: ["Macfarlanes (claimant/appellant)"] },
  { id: "c153", name: "Clifford Stewart Lay & Anor v Independent Vetcare Limited", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 1027", court: "Court of Appeal", date: "2026-07-31", time: "05:15", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/1027", summary: "The Court of Appeal allowed the sellers' appeal and struck out Independent Vetcare's counterclaim for breach of warranties in a share purchase agreement, holding that IVL had engaged in impermissible 'approbation and reprobation' by taking the opposite position on the same FCA-authorisation issue in concurrent employment tribunal proceedings — an abuse of the court's process." },
  { id: "c154", name: "IPJSC United Company Rusal v Whiteleave Holdings Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1982 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)", date: "2026-07-28", time: "05:15", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1982", summary: "Mr Justice Bright refused to approve, as drafted, two letters of request seeking witness evidence from Cyprus and Germany in Rusal's long-running shareholder dispute with Whiteleave Holdings, Vladimir Potanin and others over control of PJSC MMC Norilsk Nickel, requiring removal of proposed English-counsel examination and full translation before the requests could proceed." },
  { id: "c155", name: "Paul Andrew Whittaker v Bertha UK Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2029 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-31", time: "05:15", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2029", summary: "Mr Justice Leech granted a minority shareholder permission to appeal, on two of six grounds, against an order for sale of his shares in Dogwoof Ltd to satisfy charging orders securing costs owed to a fellow shareholder, limited to challenges over the valuation methodology applied to the shares and the relevance of his whistleblowing allegations to that valuation.", counsel: ["Jonathan Cohen KC (for the respondent, instructed by Greenwoods Legal Services)"] },
  { id: "c156", name: "Providence Gate Group Holdings Ltd v Crossbaron Ltd", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1322 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-01-13", time: "09:15", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/1322.html", summary: "HHJ Stephen Davies dismissed Providence Gate Group Holdings Ltd's application to restrain presentation of a winding-up petition over a statutory demand exceeding £7m served by Crossbaron Ltd, rejecting arguments that the debt was genuinely disputed on substantial grounds or that presentation of a petition would be an abuse of process." },
  { id: "c157", name: "Stanford v Klotho Brands Ltd & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1917 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-07-27", time: "09:15", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/1917.html", summary: "Amanda Hardy KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, struck out Kevin Stanford's 2025 claim seeking to reopen a 2022 declaration that Klotho Brands Ltd held good title to shares in All Saints Retail Ltd, holding the claim remained a challenge to property rights that had vested in the Official Receiver on Stanford's 2023 bankruptcy, and imposed an extended civil restraint order." },
  { id: "c158", name: "Float Capital Limited, Re", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2077 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)", date: "2026-08-05", time: "12:28", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2077", summary: "Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs revisited the joint liquidators' remuneration in Float Capital's liquidation (previously approved in [2026] EWHC 1891 (Ch)) after the Court of Appeal's intervening decision in Frost v The Good Box Co Labs Limited [2025] EWCA Civ 252 narrowly construed Insolvency Rule 18.24, precluding that route for increasing remuneration already fixed on a time-costs basis; the judge instead used the court's supervisory jurisdiction under section 112 of the Insolvency Act 1986 to discharge the earlier order and re-confirm the liquidators' fees on that basis, given that Rule 18.30's creditor-approval machinery could not operate effectively." },
  { id: "c172", name: "Cheyne European Special Situations Fund Investments SCA & Ors v TMF Trustee Limited & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2091 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-06", time: "09:20", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2091",
    summary: "Mr Justice Leech considered an application by the security-agent defendants to stay English proceedings brought by five investment funds holding €71.3m of senior secured notes, who challenge enforcement action taken following a debt 'up-tiering' transaction, pending resolution of parallel New York litigation raising substantially identical validity issues. The court weighed the effect of an exclusive English jurisdiction clause in the governing intercreditor agreement against the overlap with the New York proceedings, applying the 'interests of justice' test and distinguishing the position under and outside Brussels I Recast. The claimant funds were represented by Lord Wolfson KC and Abra Bompas (instructed by Pallas Partners LLP) and the defendants by David Allison KC and Ryan Perkins (instructed by Kobre & Kim (UK) LLP); no tracked firm or chambers acted.",
    counsel: ["Kobre & Kim UK LLP (defendants)", "Pallas Partners LLP (claimants)"] },
  { id: "c173", name: "The Estate of Euan Mcintyre Lindsay & Anor v Outlook Finance Limited (In Liquidation) & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 1005", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-31", time: "09:20", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/1005",
    summary: "The Court of Appeal (Singh, Arnold and Zacaroli LJJ) upheld the setting-aside of a 2014 judgment that had dismissed Scottish dairy farmers' claims against their lender and its appointed receiver, after a later Scottish court found the lender's owner had fabricated documents and misrepresented loan terms so as to procure the original judgment by fraud. The court held a judgment obtained on fraudulent evidence can be set aside even against a non-fraudulent party — here the receiver — where that party's case was parasitic on, and adopted, the fraudster's dishonest evidence, and rejected a laches defence given the protracted litigation needed to uncover the fraud. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; the appellant receiver was represented by John Wardell KC and Henry Bankes-Jones (instructed by Kennedys Law LLP) and the respondents by Jeffrey Bacon (instructed by Gunnercooke LLP)." },
  { id: "c175", name: "Trafigura PTE Limited v Société Nationale de Raffinage", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1914 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-07-30", time: "12:26", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1914",
    summary: "Mr Justice Michael Green granted commodities trader Trafigura a final anti-suit injunction restraining Cameroon's majority state-owned oil refiner, SONARA, from pursuing proceedings in the Limbe court seeking to suspend payment under an irrevocable letter of credit over a rejected gasoline cargo, holding the Cameroonian claim breached the exclusive English jurisdiction clause in the parties' sale contract and did not fall within the narrow exception for interim protective measures, since SONARA's real aim was to suspend payment and unwind the agreed allocation of risk rather than obtain ancillary protection." },
  { id: "c178", name: "HSJ Consultancy Limited (in liquidation), Re", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1135 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-05-13", time: "17:41", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1135",
    summary: "ICC Judge Mullen held that former directors Jackson and Hughes breached their duties under section 172 of the Companies Act 2006 by causing HSJ Consultancy Limited to enter an employee benefit trust and an employer-financed retirement benefits scheme in 2010, extracting loans of roughly £436,500 that were never repaid while the company was, or was rendered, insolvent. The court rejected the directors' defences — that the schemes were lawful and supported by tax counsel opinions, that any breach was ratified by shareholders under the Duomatic principle, and that relief should be granted under section 1157 of the Companies Act 2006 — finding the creditor duty was engaged and that no independent advice had been taken nor adequate reserves maintained against the risk the schemes would fail. The liquidators, represented by Georgia Purnell (instructed by Wedlake Bell LLP), succeeded in their claim for recovery of the extracted sums plus interest; the respondents were represented by Martin Budworth (instructed by Neil Davies & Partners), neither a tracked firm." },
  { id: "c179", name: "Spec 1 Limited & Ors v The Export-Import Bank of China", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1162 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-05-15", time: "17:41", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/1162",
    counsel: ["Reed Smith (claimants, counsel Jawdat Khurshid KC and Anna Hoffmann)", "Stephenson Harwood (defendant, counsel David Lewis KC and Joshua Folkard)"],
    summary: "Mr Justice Bright ruled on competing applications in a dispute arising from ship-finance arrangements, in which three shipping-company claimants alleged fraudulent misrepresentation against Chinese lender the Export-Import Bank of China. The lender sought a stay of the English proceedings in favour of parallel Singapore litigation, while the claimants sought anti-suit relief restraining the Singapore proceedings; both applications turned on the construction of an asymmetric exclusive jurisdiction clause in the parties' Novated, Amended and Restated Secured Loan Agreement, which required the borrowers to sue only in England while preserving the lender's right to sue in other competent jurisdictions, including concurrently. The court held the clause, properly construed by reference to its commercial purpose of facilitating the lender's enforcement against the borrowers' assets, expressly contemplated parallel proceedings, and dismissed both applications — allowing the English and Singapore claims to continue simultaneously and urging the parties to cooperate pragmatically in resolving the dispute across the two forums." },
  { id: "c180", name: "Veranova Bidco LP v Johnson Matthey PLC & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1021 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-05-01", time: "21:32", area: "corporate", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Comm/2026/1021.html",
    counsel: ["Linklaters (claimant, Veranova Bidco LP; counsel Tony Singla KC, Michael Bolding and Jessie Ingle)", "Herbert Smith Freehills Kramer (defendants, Johnson Matthey PLC and affiliates; counsel Simon Rainey KC, David Caplan and Ruihan Liu)"],
    summary: "Mrs Justice Dias ruled on a hard-fought, high-stakes fraud claim brought by Veranova Bidco LP (an Altaris-backed acquisition vehicle) against Johnson Matthey PLC arising from the sale of Johnson Matthey's pharmaceutical business, centred on the claimant's complaint that it was not told before completion that a major customer had triggered a price-review mechanism. The judge held the claimant established breach of warranty, but the wider fraud claim failed because the sale and purchase agreement restricted recovery for breach of warranty absent fraud, and fraud could not be made out on the pleaded facts. Mrs Justice Dias confirmed that a corporate defendant's dishonesty cannot be assembled by aggregating the separately innocent states of mind of different executives — one who knew the underlying facts and another who knew the warranty wording — reinforcing the high evidentiary bar for pleading corporate fraud in SPA warranty litigation." },
  { id: "c181", name: "Aerotron Ltd v Hermes Aviation Ltd", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2111 (KB)", court: "High Court (KB)",
    date: "2026-08-07", time: "21:32", area: "banking", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/KB/2026/2111.html",
    summary: "This ruling concerns Aerotron's application for a stay of enforcement pending its appeal against the substantive judgment in the same dispute, Aerotron Ltd v Hermes Aviation Ltd [2026] EWHC 1879 (KB) (23 July 2026), which had dismissed Aerotron's application to refuse recognition and enforcement of two Maltese judgments (totalling US$541,209.84 plus interest, covering a security deposit, flight-cancellation compensation and maintenance-provider costs) on the ground that recognition was manifestly contrary to public policy under Article 45(1)(a) of the Brussels I Recast Regulation. Having lodged an appeal against that dismissal on 30 July 2026, Aerotron argued a stay was needed because, absent one, it was unlikely to recover sums paid or enforced from Hermes if the appeal succeeded. No tracked firm or chambers is recorded as acting; Hermes' English solicitors were W Legal, not a firm on Meridian's tracked list." },
  { id: "c187", name: "SQN Global Limited v Marcus Hughes & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2056 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-10", time: "12:14", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2056",
    summary: "SQN Global Limited, a hire-purchase creditor of insolvent haulage operator Genesis (2014) UK Ltd, brought fraudulent-trading claims under section 213 of the Insolvency Act 1986 against the company's directors after obtaining a worldwide freezing order in May 2021. ICC Judge Mullen found that de facto/de jure directors Marcus Hughes, Tracy Greening and David Hughes knowingly diverted around 112 trailers and over £411,000 in cash payments to an associated company, CRVS, in breach of the freezing order and with intent to defraud creditors, holding each liable for the full loss; a transport manager was held liable only for assets and revenue channelled through CRVS from June 2021, while claims against an accountant and a resigned director were dismissed for lack of evidence of knowing participation.",
    counsel: [] },
  { id: "c189", name: "Steven Orton v Barclays Bank UK Plc", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 1025", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-31", time: "12:14", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/1025",
    summary: "Steven Orton, who had received partial PPI redress from Barclays in 2019, pursued a further consumer-credit claim over undisclosed commissions (59.7%-84.6% of premiums) worth around £2,750, then discontinued twelve days before trial after Barclays' correspondence made clear the costs of proceeding would exceed any recovery; the County Court at Middlesbrough ordered him to pay Barclays' costs as unreasonable conduct under CPR 27.14(2)(g). The Court of Appeal allowed Orton's appeal, holding that the small-claims track's costs-neutral regime must heavily inform any unreasonableness finding, that discontinuing for commercial reasons is not itself unreasonable, and that Barclays' pre-trial letters were ultimata rather than genuine settlement offers.",
    counsel: ["Simmons & Simmons (for the respondent, Barclays Bank UK PLC)"] },
  { id: "c190", name: "Glint Pay Ltd & Ors v Jason Daniel Baker & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 1023", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-31", time: "12:14", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/1023",
    summary: "Glint Pay Ltd, which had granted Brahma Finance a debenture over its assets to secure a £1.65m loan later acquired by Niven Alpha, sued more than four years after Niven appointed administrators in September 2019 following a disputed event of default, alleging the appointment was invalid because made for the improper collateral purpose of enabling the administrators' appointor to acquire the business. The Court of Appeal allowed Glint's appeal against strike-out, holding that the debenture's information covenant extended to the financial data Niven had requested (so an arguable breach existed), that a realistic prospect existed the appointment was improperly motivated (which could render it void), and that estoppel and abuse-of-process doctrines could not shut out the claim at the strike-out stage.",
    counsel: ["Reynolds Porter Chamberlain (for the respondent administrators)"] },
  { id: "c195", name: "Quidpay Finance Limited v Settlego Solutions Limited (t/a OpenPayd)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2199 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-19", time: "12:19", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2199",
    summary: "Lance Ashworth KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, handed down a further judgment dealing with costs and consequential matters following his substantive ruling in the QuidPay/OpenPayd reserve-account dispute ([2026] EWHC 1991 (Ch)), ordering OpenPayd to pay QuidPay €2,453,717.89 and £7,000,000 held in the disputed reserve account together with interest at 1% above the Bank of England base rate (4.75%) from 27 May 2026. The judge awarded QuidPay 65% of the costs of its earlier injunction application — reduced to reflect unsustainable allegations QuidPay had made about the beneficial ownership of the reserve funds — and QuidPay's costs of the preliminary-issue trial in full.",
    counsel: [] },
  { id: "c197", name: "Alliance Petrochemical Investment (Singapore) Pte Ltd v Francesco Mazzagatti & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2178 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-08-14", time: "21:21", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/2178",
    summary: "Alliance Petrochemical Investment (Singapore) Pte Ltd (API) alleged the defendants misappropriated approximately €143.8m from it — diverting customer payments, misappropriating profits through intermediaries and making unauthorised withdrawals from its bank accounts — and sought worldwide freezing and proprietary injunctions plus third-party disclosure from financial adviser H&P Advisory Limited. Lionel Persey KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, dismissed the strike-out application (holding API's allegations concerning an allegedly forged Abu Dhabi loan facility and related Italian proceedings remained properly pleaded and relevant), ordered third-party disclosure of RockRose-acquisition due-diligence materials from H&P Advisory, but refused the worldwide freezing order for delay and insufficient fresh evidence of dissipation risk and dismissed the proprietary injunction application.",
    counsel: [] },
  { id: "c198", name: "Sanaa El Debs & Ors v Craig Coughlan", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2160 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-20", time: "21:21", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2160",
    summary: "The applicants sought pre-action disclosure under CPR 31.16 against Craig Coughlan in connection with an anticipated claim for fraudulent misrepresentation and unlawful means conspiracy arising from a failed investment in Drecford Holdings Limited. Master Clark granted disclosure with specified limitations, rejecting the respondent's arguments that ADGM was a more appropriate forum, that the claims were too speculative, and that the requested documents were outside his control, finding the claims sufficiently articulated and that disclosure was desirable to help fairly dispose of anticipated proceedings, resolve the dispute without litigation, and save costs.",
    counsel: [] },
  { id: "c199", name: "Richard Graham Doubtfire v Timothy Michael Horrell", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2173 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-21", time: "12:20", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2173",
    summary: "Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs dismissed an application to set aside a statutory demand for approximately £430,000 served under a personal guarantee for a £250,000 loan, after the guarantor's own company, Silversword Developments Limited, defaulted and entered liquidation. The applicant's duress/undue-influence case failed for want of evidence and was contradicted by his own contemporaneous acknowledgment that the guarantee terms were 'fair'; his proposed cross-claims in defamation and unlawful means conspiracy, arising from a separate development venture whose economics changed after planning permission for fewer dwellings than expected was granted, were held unarguable for lack of evidence of reputational harm, causation or quantifiable loss.",
    counsel: ["Cripps LLP (respondent)"] },
  { id: "c200", name: "EMJ Plastics Limited v Mekina Industries Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2163 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-17", time: "12:20", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2163",
    summary: "Recorder Douglas Campbell KC found that two former managers of glass-reinforced-plastic formwork manufacturer EMJ Plastics, who left in 2022 to set up rival Mekina Industries, had copied thousands of confidential EMJ documents — including CAD drawings, calculation sheets and a customer prospects list — without authorisation and used them to launch and pitch the competing venture to investors. The court held the departing employees and their new company liable for breach of contract, breach of confidence, breach of fiduciary duty and unlawful means conspiracy (dismissing only a copyright claim for insufficient particularity), with additional damages available under Article 13 of the IP Enforcement Directive.",
    counsel: [] },
  { id: "c201", name: "Valeriy Ernestovich Drelle v Servis-Terminal LLC (In Liquidation in the Russian Federation)", citation: "[2026] UKSC 29", court: "Supreme Court",
    date: "2026-07-27", time: "17:20", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/uksc/2026/29",
    summary: "Servis-Terminal LLC, a Russian company in liquidation, sought to found a bankruptcy petition against its former director Valeriy Drelle on an unrecognised, unregistrable Russian money judgment for RUB 2bn, after the Court of Appeal held that only a recognised or registered foreign judgment could satisfy the 'debt' requirement under section 267 of the Insolvency Act 1986. The Supreme Court (Lord Briggs and Lord Hamblen giving the lead judgment, Lords Sales, Stephens and Doherty agreeing) unanimously overturned that ruling, holding that a final and conclusive foreign money judgment creates an immediate common-law 'obligation principle' debt regardless of recognition or registration, and that 'no direct operation' language in earlier authority merely denies direct execution rather than legal effect. The Court rejected an analogy with the revenue rule (which bars enforcement of foreign sovereign claims, not private judgment debts) and remitted to the Court of Appeal the separate question whether the underlying debt is genuinely disputed on substantial grounds.",
    counsel: [] },
  { id: "c204", name: "Garden House Software Ltd v Marsh & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2184 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-18", time: "09:09", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/2184.html",
    counsel: ["Jamie Riley KC and Calum Mulderrig instructed by Addleshaw Goddard LLP (claimant)", "Cadence Solicitors LLP / JPP Law LLP (defendants)"],
    summary: "Mr Justice Fancourt gave a quantum judgment against the former directors of an insolvent company over a 2017 assignment of intellectual property, which the court had found to be a transaction at an undervalue and a transaction defrauding creditors under sections 238 and 423 of the Insolvency Act 1986. The judgment orders payment of just over £2.03m, on top of the earlier liability findings and alongside a breach-of-directors'-duties claim. It is a Business and Property Courts (Business List, ChD) decision, brought by the claimant company via Addleshaw Goddard LLP against defendants represented by Cadence Solicitors LLP and JPP Law LLP; no tracked firm acted." },
  { id: "c206", name: "LM1 Ltd v Seacroft Film Investments Ltd", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2212 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-21", time: "09:09", area: "ri", url: "https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Ch/2026/2212.html",
    counsel: ["Best Solicitors (applicant)", "Walker Morris LLP (respondent)"],
    summary: "HHJ Kelly, sitting in the Insolvency and Companies List of the Business and Property Courts in Leeds, granted an application restraining presentation of a winding-up petition that had been threatened on the strength of a statutory demand, but made no order as to costs. The ruling illustrates the court's willingness to intervene pre-emptively against a disputed winding-up petition where the underlying debt is genuinely contested. Neither side was represented by a tracked firm — the applicant by Best Solicitors, the respondent by Walker Morris LLP." },
  { id: "c207", name: "Stuart William Evans & Ors v JNP Group Consulting Engineers Limited", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2175 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-08-14", time: "05:17", area: "corporate", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/2175",
    counsel: [],
    summary: "HHJ Charman, sitting in the Circuit Commercial Court of the Business and Property Courts in Birmingham, held that a remuneration agreement reached among the members of two limited liability partnerships that merged into JNP Group Consulting Engineers Limited in 2016 did not bind the newly-incorporated company under section 51 of the Companies Act 2006, since the company did not yet exist when the agreement was reached, and that the claimants' subsequent retirement letters were a full and final settlement of their claims over remuneration, director loan accounts and shareholdings in any event. Neither side was represented by a tracked firm." },
  { id: "c208", name: "FH Holding Moscow Limited v AO Unicredit Bank & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 468", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-04-17", time: "09:18", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/468",
    counsel: ["A&O Shearman (respondent banks)"],
    summary: "The Court of Appeal (Lewison, Males and Popplewell LJJ) dismissed an appeal by FH Holding Moscow Limited (FHM), a Cyprus company operating in Russia, against the Commercial Court's refusal of an anti-suit injunction to restrain Moscow foreclosure proceedings brought by AO UniCredit Bank. FHM had borrowed under an English-law Facility Agreement with a VIAC (Vienna) arbitration clause but granted security under a separate Russian-law Mortgage Agreement conferring jurisdiction on the Moscow Commercial Court; following sanctions-related payment disruption linked to the Russia-Ukraine conflict, UniCredit commenced foreclosure under the Mortgage Agreement, which FHM said breached the Facility Agreement's arbitration clause. The Court of Appeal held that disputes over whether an Event of Default occurred fell within the Moscow court's jurisdiction under the Russian-law Mortgage Agreement, not exclusively within the Vienna arbitration clause." },
  { id: "c209", name: "Cheyne European Special Situations Fund Investments SCA & Ors v TMF Trustee Ltd & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2091 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-06", time: "17:20", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2091",
    counsel: ["Kobre & Kim (UK) LLP (defendants)"],
    summary: "Minority senior secured noteholders of Hunkemöller International BV (including Cheyne, Contrarian and Man funds) sued TMF Trustee Ltd and Hunkemöller over a 2024 'up-tiering'/distressed-disposal liability-management exercise that restructured Hunkemöller's revolving credit facility, bridge facility and €272.5m senior secured notes. Mr Justice Leech, sitting in the Financial List, granted the defendants' application to stay the English claim in favour of materially parallel, more advanced New York Supreme Court proceedings concerning the same 2024 transaction and the same underlying facts. The ruling is a live test case on how English courts manage cross-border liability-management disputes running in parallel with US litigation over the same up-tier transaction." },
  { id: "c210", name: "Competition and Markets Authority v Emma Sleep (Emma International GmbH)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 1995 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-07-30", time: "17:20", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/1995",
    counsel: [],
    summary: "The CMA sought a court enforcement order requiring mattress retailer Emma Sleep to comply with a fixed 1:2 volume ratio when using reference/'was now' pricing in discount advertising — i.e. that for every two units sold at the discounted price, at least one must have been sold at the higher reference price. The High Court found Emma Sleep had breached consumer-protection law on narrower, admitted grounds, but held the CMA had not established that its blanket fixed-volume-ratio requirement was the correct test for whether a reference-price discount is misleading, and declined to impose it, inviting the parties to agree revised order terms. The judgment is a significant check on the CMA's approach to reference-pricing enforcement across retail generally, with a further hearing possible later in 2026 if terms cannot be agreed." },
  { id: "c214", name: "Mackie Motors (Brechin) Limited v Renault UK Limited & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2182 (Comm)", court: "High Court (Comm)",
    date: "2026-08-17", time: "12:34", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/comm/2026/2182",
    counsel: [],
    summary: "Mackie Motors (Brechin) Limited, a former Renault/Nissan/Dacia franchised dealer in northeast Scotland, sued Renault UK, Nissan Motor (GB) and RCI Financial Services (Renault's finance subsidiary) over the November 2021 withdrawal of dealer-system access and termination of stocking finance that followed a suspicious-activity report RCI filed alleging money-laundering concerns about MMBL's director — losses MMBL says forced the sale of its dealership in February 2022. Deputy Judge Lance Ashworth KC held that MMBL's new claim (repudiatory breach of implied terms in the Dealership Agreements themselves, plus RCI's tortious inducement of Renault/Nissan's breach) was not a Henderson v Henderson abuse of process, since neither Renault nor Nissan had been defendants to MMBL's earlier, struck-out claim on an implied umbrella agreement, and evidence of RCI's alleged coercion only emerged after that claim was dismissed. Summary judgment on causation was refused as fact-sensitive, so the claim proceeds to trial." },
  { id: "c215", name: "QuidPay Finance Limited v SettleGo Solutions Limited (t/a OpenPayd) (Re Costs and Consequentials)", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2199 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-19", time: "12:34", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2199",
    counsel: [],
    summary: "Following an earlier preliminary-issues judgment that SettleGo Solutions Limited (t/a OpenPayd), an e-money and payment-services provider, had no continuing contractual right after termination to retain a multi-million-pound reserve of client funds it held for QuidPay Finance Limited, Deputy Judge Lance Ashworth KC handed down this follow-on ruling on costs and consequential matters. QuidPay's recoverable costs of its injunction application were cut by around a third, to roughly US$400,000, because QuidPay had advanced an unsustainable beneficial-ownership argument over the funds and threatened to report OpenPayd to the FCA; OpenPayd recovered its costs of the preliminary-issue applications, while QuidPay recovered its costs of the preliminary-issue trial itself. The ruling illustrates the costs consequences of overreaching tactical arguments even for an otherwise successful commercial claimant." },
  { id: "c216", name: "Sucden Financial Limited v TMT Metals AG & Ors", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 1080", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-08-10", time: "17:26", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/1080",
    counsel: ["Macfarlanes (claimant)"],
    summary: "Sucden Financial brought a commercial-finance dispute against TMT Metals AG and others; this ruling by Foxton LJ addressed the procedural timing for filing a defence while a related jurisdiction appeal was pending before the Supreme Court. The Court of Appeal gave directions on how the underlying claim should proceed in the interim, balancing the claimant's interest in progressing the case against the practical consequences of the outstanding jurisdiction challenge. Jason Robinson KC and Charles Littlewood, instructed by Macfarlanes, appeared for the claimant." },
  { id: "c217", name: "Glint Pay Ltd & Ors v Baker & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWCA Civ 1023", court: "Court of Appeal",
    date: "2026-07-31", time: "17:26", area: "banking", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2026/1023",
    counsel: [],
    summary: "Glint Pay and related appellants challenged the validity of an out-of-court administrator appointment, alleging it had been made for an improper purpose. The Court of Appeal allowed the appeal, finding a triable issue on the propriety of the appointment and declining to treat the matter as suitable for summary disposal. The judgment underscores the scope for challenging out-of-court administrator appointments in security-enforcement contexts where improper purpose is credibly alleged." },
  { id: "c219", name: "Cavendish IP Solutions Ltd v On and On Consultants Ltd & Anor", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2247 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-28", time: "09:10", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2247",
    counsel: [],
    summary: "A litigation funder, as assignee of a liquidator's claims, sued to recover payments made by an insolvent company to a defendant-controlled entity in the two years before a November 2015 liquidation. Mr Justice Fancourt found the individual defendant was a de facto/shadow director despite no formal appointment, and that substantial payments to the corporate defendant were unlawful preferences and/or breaches of directors' duties. The court also held that continuing to trade a business whose costs consistently exceeded revenue amounted to a fraudulent breach of fiduciary duty, causing creditor losses exceeding £1 million." },
  { id: "c220", name: "Richard Graham Doubtfire v Timothy Michael Horrell", citation: "[2026] EWHC 2173 (Ch)", court: "High Court (Ch)",
    date: "2026-08-21", time: "09:10", area: "ri", url: "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/ch/2026/2173",
    counsel: [],
    summary: "Doubtfire, a director of Silversword Developments Limited, applied to set aside a £399,772 statutory demand served by Horrell under a personal guarantee for loans to Silversword after it entered creditors' voluntary liquidation, arguing the guarantee was procured by duress/undue influence and that he held counterclaims in defamation and unlawful-means conspiracy arising from separate dealings over ACM-47 Guildford Road Limited. Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs dismissed the application, finding the duress claim unparticularised and contradicted by contemporaneous evidence (independent legal advice; Doubtfire's own email calling the terms 'fair'), and holding the counterclaims lacked serious harm, quantification and a sufficient causal link to equal or exceed the demanded sum." },
];

// ---- Lightweight lookups ----------------------------------------------------
export const firmById = Object.fromEntries(firms.map((f) => [f.id, f]));
export const areaById = Object.fromEntries(practiceAreas.map((a) => [a.id, a]));
export const typeById = Object.fromEntries(updateTypes.map((t) => [t.id, t]));
export const tierById = Object.fromEntries(tiers.map((t) => [t.id, t]));

// ---- AI-generated case summaries -------------------------------------------
// Longer, AI-written orientation summaries for each BAILII case (facts, holding,
// significance). AI-generated and not a substitute for the judgment — always
// read the case on BAILII before relying on it.
export const caseSummaries = {
  c220: "Richard Doubtfire, a director of Silversword Developments Limited, had personally guaranteed loans Timothy Horrell advanced to Silversword. When Silversword failed to repay and entered creditors' voluntary liquidation, Horrell served a statutory demand on Doubtfire for £399,772, and Doubtfire applied to set it aside on two grounds: that the guarantee was procured by duress or undue influence, and that he held counterclaims in defamation and unlawful-means conspiracy — arising from separate, later dealings concerning ACM-47 Guildford Road Limited and involving Horrell's co-director Nouri — sufficient to extinguish or exceed the demanded sum. Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs dismissed the application in full: the duress/undue-influence case amounted to bare assertion undermined by contemporaneous evidence that Doubtfire had taken independent legal advice and had himself described the guarantee's terms as 'fair', while the defamation claim lacked the requisite serious harm and the conspiracy claim failed on both agreement and causation, since it was the local planning authority's decision — not the defendants' conduct — that altered the underlying project's economics and it was administrators, not the defendants, who effected the relevant property sale. The judgment illustrates how exacting the evidential threshold is for setting aside a statutory demand on disputed cross-claims where the underlying commercial narrative does not withstand scrutiny.",
  c218: "In August 2024 Apollo XI, a BVI investment vehicle, advanced Nexedge — a regulated FCA introducing broker — a US$10m loan intended partly to fund an upgrade of Nexedge's FCA licence. The relationship deteriorated by April 2025, and Apollo obtained a without-notice freezing injunction on 15 April 2025 based substantially on a covertly recorded conversation; the injunction was later discharged (17 June 2025) after the court found Apollo had materially misled it as to how the recording was obtained and its contents, and had no good arguable case. In this 28 August 2026 judgment, Judge Cooper permitted Nexedge to enforce Apollo's cross-undertaking in damages and ordered an inquiry into quantifiable loss, rejecting Apollo's arguments that Nexedge's claimed categories of loss (harm to its regulatory-permissions application, business disruption, reputational damage, diverted management resources) were too speculative to proceed.",
  c219: "A litigation funder, having taken an assignment of a liquidator's claims, sued the sole director of an insolvent company and a corporate defendant it controlled, seeking to recover payments made to the corporate defendant in the two years before the company entered liquidation in November 2015. The individual defendant argued he was never formally appointed a director and so owed no directors' duties; Mr Justice Fancourt rejected that, finding him a de facto or shadow director on the facts. The court held that a series of payments to the corporate defendant were unlawful preferences and/or breaches of duty, and separately found that continuing to trade the business after it was plain that costs exceeded revenue amounted to a fraudulent breach of fiduciary duty causing creditor losses of more than £1 million. The judgment is a reminder that informal, undocumented control of a company's affairs can still found director-duty and preference liability years after the fact.",
  c211: "LM1 Limited faced a statutory demand for £20,000 from Seacroft Film Investments Limited over an alleged unpaid costs contribution due under a settlement agreement between the parties, and applied to restrain Seacroft from advertising a winding-up petition based on that demand, arguing the sum was genuinely disputed. HHJ Kelly, sitting in the Insolvency and Companies List of the Business and Property Courts in Leeds, found LM1 technically succeeded on the substance of its application, so the petition was restrained, but held that LM1's own delay in redrawing its bill of costs and providing meaningful information about anticipated costs was itself unsatisfactory conduct. Weighing both parties' conduct, the judge declined to make the usual costs order in the successful applicant's favour and instead made no order as to costs. The ruling is a reminder that even a technically successful applicant to restrain a winding-up petition can be denied its costs where its own conduct of the dispute has been unsatisfactory.",
  c212: "Four members of the Debs family say they invested around US$6m in Drecford Holdings Limited, an ADGM-incorporated (Abu Dhabi Global Market) company, through loan notes that were later converted into bonds, on the strength of representations that the investment was safe and low-risk. After Drecford stopped making payments and went silent, the applicants say they uncovered evidence of fraudulent misrepresentation and an unlawful conspiracy involving respondent Craig Coughlan — a director and 50% shareholder of Drecford — and principals of Purple Capital, who allegedly failed to disclose conflicting interests in the investment structure. Master Clark granted the applicants' request for pre-action disclosure of Drecford's business plans, internal communications and trading records, holding their prospective claims in fraud and conspiracy were sufficiently viable and that the disclosure sought was reasonably necessary to allow them to plead their case with proper particularity. The decision illustrates the court's willingness to order pre-action disclosure against an individual respondent, rather than only the corporate vehicle, where a viable fraud claim is credibly asserted against them personally.",
  c213: "Alliance Petrochemical Investment (Singapore) Pte Ltd (API) sued its former CEO Francesco Mazzagatti and a co-defendant, alleging they misappropriated roughly €143.8m from the company through three routes — diverting customer payments, concealing profits via intermediary entities, and making unauthorised withdrawals from API's own bank accounts — and that the proceeds helped fund the defendants' subsequent acquisition of RockRose Energy, allegedly financed in part using forged Abu Dhabi loan-facility documentation. Ruling on a cluster of applications, Lionel Persey KC dismissed the defendants' attempts to strike out the forgery and related Italian-criminal-proceedings allegations, and allowed API partial non-party disclosure from financial adviser H&P Advisory Ltd, but refused API's application for worldwide freezing and proprietary injunctions, finding the evidence of a real risk that the defendants would dissipate assets was not strong enough to justify such relief at this stage. The ruling is a useful illustration of the high evidential bar for freezing relief even where a claimant's underlying fraud and forgery allegations are permitted to proceed to trial.",
  c214: "Mackie Motors (Brechin) Limited (MMBL) was a franchised motor dealer for Renault, Nissan and Dacia in northeast Scotland until, in November 2021, Renault and Nissan withdrew its system access and RCI Financial Services (Renault's UK finance subsidiary) terminated its stocking-finance facilities, after RCI filed a suspicious-activity report alleging money-laundering concerns about MMBL's director; MMBL says the fallout forced it to sell its dealership to Parks of Hamilton in February 2022. An earlier claim against RCI alone, framed around an implied 'umbrella agreement', was struck out and the strike-out upheld by the Court of Appeal in May 2023; MMBL brought this fresh claim in July 2025 against all three companies, alleging repudiatory breach of implied terms within the Dealership Agreements themselves and tortious inducement by RCI, supported by a witness statement from a former Renault/Nissan employee, Kenneth Banks, said to reveal RCI's coercion of the manufacturers. Deputy Judge Lance Ashworth KC refused to strike out the claims against Renault and Nissan as Henderson v Henderson abuse, since neither had been a defendant to the earlier claim and the implied-term breaches now alleged had never been adjudicated, and found the inducement claim against RCI was not abusive either because the Banks evidence was genuinely new. The court also declined summary judgment on causation, leaving that issue — and the substantive claims — to go to trial.",
  c215: "QuidPay Finance Limited, an e-money customer of payment-services provider SettleGo Solutions Limited (trading as OpenPayd), had earlier won a preliminary-issues trial establishing that OpenPayd's contractual entitlement to hold a reserve against a suspected fraud claim did not survive termination of their agreement, so OpenPayd was ordered to release a multi-million-pound reserve of QuidPay's client funds; QuidPay had initially also argued the funds were held on trust for its own customers but abandoned that argument before trial. In this follow-on judgment on costs and consequential matters, Deputy Judge Lance Ashworth KC reduced QuidPay's recoverable costs of its injunction application by around a third, to roughly US$400,000, because QuidPay had pursued an ultimately unsustainable beneficial-ownership case and had threatened to report OpenPayd to the FCA during the dispute. OpenPayd was awarded its costs of the preliminary-issue applications, while QuidPay recovered its costs of the preliminary-issue trial that determined the reserve point in its favour. The decision is a reminder that tactical overreach — even by an otherwise successful claimant — carries a real costs price in English commercial litigation.",
  c216: "This is a further ruling in Sucden Financial's long-running commercial-finance dispute with TMT Metals, distinct from the earlier jurisdiction-gateway decision at [2026] EWCA Civ 986 (see c149). Foxton LJ addressed the procedural question of how and when TMT Metals should file its defence to the substantive claim while a related jurisdictional challenge remained pending before the Supreme Court. The Court of Appeal gave case-management directions designed to let the underlying commercial-finance claim progress without prejudicing the outcome of the pending Supreme Court appeal. Macfarlanes, instructing Jason Robinson KC and Charles Littlewood, continued to act for Sucden as claimant.",
  c217: "Glint Pay and related appellant companies challenged the validity of an administrator's out-of-court appointment over one of the group companies, arguing the appointment had been made for an improper purpose rather than a genuine insolvency-related one. The Court of Appeal considered whether the appointment was open to challenge on a summary basis or whether the improper-purpose allegation raised a triable issue requiring fuller investigation. It allowed the appeal, holding that a triable issue on impropriety had been shown and that the matter was not suitable for summary disposal in the administrator's favour. The decision is a useful marker for the scope to challenge out-of-court administrator appointments in security-enforcement disputes where an improper purpose is credibly alleged.",
  c210: "Emma Sleep (Emma International GmbH), the German mattress-and-furniture retailer, was already the subject of CMA enforcement action over its use of reference ('was now') pricing when advertising discounts. The CMA asked the High Court to impose a fixed volume requirement (FVR) — a rigid 1:2 ratio under which, for every two units sold at a discounted price, Emma would need to have sold at least one unit at the higher reference price for the discount claim to be non-misleading. The court found that Emma Sleep had breached consumer-protection law on narrower grounds Emma itself admitted, but was not persuaded that the CMA's blanket 1:2 volume test was the right legal standard for assessing whether a reference-price discount misleads consumers generally, and declined to write that fixed ratio into the enforcement order. The parties were invited to agree revised order terms, with the prospect of a further hearing later in 2026 if they cannot. The decision is being read across the retail sector as a check on the CMA's ambition to impose bright-line volume tests for discount pricing, leaving the question more fact-specific than the regulator had hoped.",
  c209: "Minority senior secured noteholders of Hunkemöller International BV — funds managed by Cheyne Capital, Contrarian Capital and Man Group among others — brought an English claim against bond trustee TMF Trustee Ltd and Hunkemöller itself, challenging a 2024 'up-tiering' liability-management exercise that restructured the group's revolving credit facility, a bridge facility and €272.5m of senior secured notes in a way the claimants say disadvantaged them relative to noteholders who participated in the new money. Materially the same transaction and factual matrix were already the subject of more advanced proceedings in the New York Supreme Court. Mr Justice Leech, sitting in the Financial List, granted the defendants' application for a case-management stay of the English claim pending resolution of the New York litigation, to avoid the risk of duplicative proceedings and inconsistent findings on the same up-tier transaction. The ruling is being watched as an early English test case on how the courts will manage transatlantic disputes over increasingly common European liability-management/up-tier exercises structured with parallel US law elements.",
  c208: "FH Holding Moscow Limited (FHM), a Cyprus-incorporated company operating in Russia, borrowed under a Facility Agreement governed by English law and containing a VIAC (Vienna) arbitration clause, but granted security to the lenders under a separate, Russian-law Mortgage Agreement that conferred jurisdiction on the Moscow Commercial Court. After payment disruption linked to sanctions arising from the Russia-Ukraine conflict, AO UniCredit Bank commenced foreclosure proceedings in Moscow under the Mortgage Agreement; FHM applied for permission to serve an anti-suit injunction application out of the jurisdiction, arguing the Moscow proceedings breached the Facility Agreement's arbitration clause, but the Commercial Court refused permission. The Court of Appeal (Lewison, Males and Popplewell LJJ) dismissed FHM's appeal, holding on construction of the two related but separate finance documents that the Moscow foreclosure proceedings — concerning whether an Event of Default had occurred — fell within the Russian-law Mortgage Agreement's own jurisdiction clause rather than being caught by the Facility Agreement's Vienna arbitration clause. The case is a useful illustration of the risk that overlapping or competing dispute-resolution clauses across related finance documents, governed by different laws, can each be given effect according to their own terms rather than treated as subject to a single overarching forum — a live issue for lenders and borrowers with Russia-linked security structures split across parallel English-law facility and Russian-law security documents.",
  c207: "Three former members of two limited liability partnerships that merged in February 2016 to form JNP Group Consulting Engineers Limited sued the company over remuneration packages they said had been agreed at the time of the merger, over deductions from their director loan accounts for uncollected aged debts, and over related shareholding issues. HHJ Charman, sitting in the Circuit Commercial Court of the Business and Property Courts in Birmingham, held that no binding remuneration agreement existed between the claimants and the company itself, because the company had not yet been incorporated when the alleged February 2016 agreement was reached among the LLP members — any such agreement bound only the LLPs, and section 51 of the Companies Act 2006 (pre-incorporation contracts) did not operate to transfer that liability to the newly-formed company. In any event, the court found the claimants' retirement letters constituted a full and final settlement of all disputes over employment, director loan accounts and shareholdings, and that deductions from their loan accounts for uncollected aged debts had been validly agreed at a September 2017 shareholders' meeting, so the counterclaim was also compromised by the retirement letters. The judgment is a useful illustration of the strict application of section 51 to deny pre-incorporation arrangements binding effect on a newly-formed company absent clear novation or adoption.",
  c201: "Servis-Terminal LLC, a Russian company now in liquidation, obtained a Russian court judgment for RUB 2 billion (roughly £17m) against its former director general and shareholder, Valeriy Drelle, over a loan the company said he had caused it to advance improperly. Because Russia is not a party to any UK reciprocal-enforcement regime and the judgment had neither been recognised at common law nor registered under the Foreign Judgments (Reciprocal Enforcement) Act 1933, Servis-Terminal instead presented a bankruptcy petition against Drelle in England, relying on the judgment itself as the underlying 'debt' for the purposes of section 267 of the Insolvency Act 1986. The Court of Appeal held that an unrecognised and unregistered foreign judgment could not found a bankruptcy petition, reasoning that such a judgment has 'no direct operation' in England until recognised, but the Supreme Court unanimously reversed: Lord Briggs and Lord Hamblen (Lords Sales, Stephens and Doherty agreeing) held that the 'obligation principle' — under which a final, conclusive foreign money judgment gives rise to an immediate debt enforceable as a simple contract debt at common law — applies regardless of formal recognition or registration, and that 'no direct operation' means only that the foreign judgment cannot itself be executed in England, not that it lacks legal effect. The Court also rejected Drelle's argument that the revenue-rule analogy (which bars indirect enforcement of foreign tax and penal claims) should be extended to foreign judgments generally, since that rule protects sovereign, not private, claims, and remitted to the Court of Appeal the question whether the underlying debt is genuinely disputed on substantial grounds, which would in any event bar the petition. The ruling substantially widens the practical toolkit available to creditors holding foreign judgments against UK-linked debtors, particularly from jurisdictions such as Russia that have no reciprocal-enforcement treaty with England.",
  c200: "EMJ Plastics Limited makes glass-reinforced plastic permanent formwork panels for construction, and two of its senior staff — Operations Manager Ian Baggaley and Production Manager Mark Johnson — left in 2022 to set up a direct competitor, Mekina Industries. During his notice period Baggaley copied 11,579 EMJ documents onto a personal drive; EMJ said this was done without authorisation and for competitive purposes, while the defendants argued a director had sanctioned the copying. Recorder Douglas Campbell KC, sitting in the Intellectual Property List, found that Mekina's CAD drawings and engineering calculations incorporated copied EMJ material (evidenced by identical layer-naming conventions and near-identical text), that Johnson had obtained and used EMJ's confidential customer 'hot list' to plan and pitch the new venture to outside investors, and that Baggaley's senior operational role gave rise to fiduciary duties he breached by concealing his own wrongdoing. The court held the defendants liable for breach of contract, breach of confidence, breach of fiduciary duty and unlawful means conspiracy — with Johnson liable via common design — while dismissing only the copyright claim for insufficient particularity, and confirmed additional damages were available under Article 13 of the IP Enforcement Directive. The judgment is a useful illustration of how English courts piece together forensic and documentary evidence (file-copying logs, drawing metadata, textual similarity) to hold departing employees and their new venture to account for misuse of a former employer's confidential technical and commercial information.",
  c199: "Richard Doubtfire had personally guaranteed a £250,000 loan that Timothy Horrell advanced to Silversword Developments Limited, a company of which Doubtfire was director; when Silversword defaulted and went into liquidation, Horrell served a statutory demand on Doubtfire for around £430,000 under the guarantee. Doubtfire applied to set the demand aside on three grounds: that the guarantee itself had been procured by duress or undue influence; that he held cross-claims in defamation and unlawful means conspiracy, arising from a separate property venture (ACM-47 Guildford Road Limited) whose economics were undermined when planning permission was granted for four dwellings rather than the six originally anticipated, that exceeded the debt; and residual discretionary grounds. Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs dismissed the application in full, finding the duress case rested on bare, unevidenced assertions that were contradicted by Doubtfire's own contemporaneous acknowledgment that the guarantee's terms were 'fair', that the defamation claim lacked any evidence of the serious reputational harm required under the Defamation Act 2013, and that the conspiracy claim failed for want of evidence of any unlawful combination between Horrell and third parties, causation, or quantifiable loss — the change in project economics stemming from the planning authority's own decision rather than any wrongdoing by Horrell. The ruling illustrates how exacting the evidential bar is for a guarantor seeking to resist a statutory demand by pointing to disputed cross-claims that have not been properly evidenced or pleaded.",
  c196: "Mackie Motors (Brechin) Limited (MMBL) had held Renault and Nissan car-dealership franchises in north-east Scotland since the 1970s and 1990s respectively, financed through RCI Financial Services, Renault's UK financing subsidiary. After RCI grew concerned in late 2021 about MMBL's involvement in substantial international loan transactions and filed a suspicious-activity report with the National Crime Agency, Renault and Nissan restricted MMBL's access to their ordering systems and RCI terminated all financing agreements; MMBL's first claim against RCI, including competition-law and unfair-contract-terms allegations, was struck out in 2022 and its appeal dismissed by the Court of Appeal in 2023. MMBL brought fresh proceedings in July 2025 against Renault, Nissan and RCI, this time alleging repudiatory breach of implied terms requiring system access, tortious inducement of that breach by RCI, and a separate claim under a Used Vehicle Stocking Agreement, seeking roughly £18.6m in damages; the defendants applied to strike out the whole claim as a Henderson v Henderson abuse of process, or for summary judgment on causation. Lance Ashworth KC held it was not abusive to sue Renault and Nissan for the first time, since neither had been a party to the earlier proceedings; that the inducement claim against RCI relied on evidence from a former RCI employee, Kenneth Banks, that only emerged after the Court of Appeal's 2023 ruling and so could not have been raised earlier; and that the smaller stocking-agreement claim should proceed alongside the rest for reasons of proportionality, while rejecting summary judgment on causation as turning on disputed evidence unsuitable for determination without cross-examination. The ruling is a useful illustration of how English courts distinguish genuinely new evidence-based claims from repackaged litigation when applying the modern, discretionary Henderson v Henderson abuse-of-process doctrine.",
  c193: "Mr Battu says he invested around £463,000 in a residential property-development scheme in Southampton run through Reflect Canal House Limited and a web of related respondents, on the strength of promises that he would receive equity in the venture and be appointed a director — neither of which materialised. After uncovering what he says is evidence of fund misappropriation, false accounting records and the closure of accounts to hide the money trail, he obtained a without-notice worldwide freezing order and the respondents applied to discharge it, arguing the order should not have been granted because of alleged material non-disclosure at the original hearing. HHJ Paul Matthews rejected the discharge application and continued the freezing injunction, holding that Mr Battu had shown a good arguable case across his claims for breach of trust, breach of fiduciary duty, fraud and conspiracy, and that the documented pattern of transfers, false records and account closures gave solid evidential grounds for a real risk that assets would otherwise be dissipated before any judgment could be enforced. The ruling is a useful illustration of how English courts assess an alleged fraudster's dissipation risk from a paper trail of concealment rather than direct evidence of intent to evade a judgment.",
  c192: "Alexander Luxury (Yorkshire) Limited faced a winding-up petition from Developstate Limited over unpaid invoices, totalling £27,341.80, for work under a construction contract, and applied to restrain advertisement of the petition and strike out the underlying debt claim, arguing the sums were genuinely disputed and that it had a defective-works cross-claim that outweighed what it owed. Under the Construction Act's payment regime, a paying party who wishes to withhold sums otherwise due on an interim application must serve a timely Pay Less notice, and Alexander Luxury had not done so. HHJ Richard Carter, sitting in the Business and Property Courts in Leeds, held there was accordingly no genuine dispute about the invoiced sums themselves, and separately rejected the defective-works cross-claim as unsupported and contradicted by the contemporaneous documentary record, dismissing the company's application and clearing the way for the petition to proceed. The decision is a reminder that the well-established rule against using winding-up petitions to resolve bona fide payment disputes gives no shelter to a debtor who has failed to follow the Construction Act's own notice procedure for withholding payment.",
  c191: "Mr and Mrs James sued HSBC UK Bank Plc over an allegedly unauthorised £49,000 loan account opened in Mrs James's name in September 2004 and roughly £25,000 transferred from their joint accounts to that loan account between 2005 and 2008, which they said they only discovered in 2010 when attempting to remortgage their property. HSBC successfully struck out the claim below on the basis that the Jameses had sufficient knowledge to plead fraud by 2013 — over nine years before they issued their claim in October 2022 — making it statute-barred under section 32 of the Limitation Act 1980's 'late discovery' provisions. Lord Justice Nugee, giving the unanimous judgment of the Court of Appeal, rejected arguments that the lower court had misread a July 2013 letter from HSBC or wrongly conflated knowledge of the loan account with knowledge of fraud, holding that a contemporaneous record of fictitious repayment arrangements in Mrs James's name, combined with the unauthorised account opening and transfers, was sufficient to infer fraud had been discoverable by 2013. The appeal was dismissed and the strike-out upheld; HSBC was represented by Eversheds Sutherland (International) LLP, neither a firm tracked by Meridian.",
  c186: "Mercuria, a major commodity trading house, sued Baltic Exchange Information Services — the body that compiles the TD3C benchmark measuring the cost of shipping crude oil by tanker from the Middle East Gulf to China — alleging that after the effective closure of the Strait of Hormuz during 2026's Iran-related tensions, the Baltic Exchange kept publishing TD3C assessments that no longer accurately or reliably represented the market the benchmark is supposed to measure, distorting freight and freight-derivative contracts referencing the route. Mercuria says it has suffered, or will suffer, losses running into the hundreds of millions of dollars on positions linked to TD3C, though it is not seeking to recover that sum in these proceedings; the Baltic Exchange denies breaching any statutory, contractual or regulatory obligation in compiling the index and says Mercuria's claim has itself added to market confusion. Given the wider market's reliance on the benchmark and the billions of dollars in freight contracts and financial instruments referencing it, Mr Justice Butcher (sitting in the Financial List) fast-tracked the dispute to a fifteen-day trial listed for October 2026, rejecting the Baltic Exchange's opposition to expedition and to early determination of preliminary issues. The case is a significant early test of how English courts will police the integrity of a benchmark administrator's compliance obligations under the retained UK Benchmarks Regulation where a geopolitical shock is said to have broken the link between a published rate and the market it tracks.",
  c181: "Aerotron, an aircraft lessor/operator, lost a substantive High Court claim on 23 July 2026 ([2026] EWHC 1879 (KB)) in which it had tried to block recognition and enforcement in England of two Maltese judgments obtained by Hermes Aviation, together worth just over US$541,000, covering a security deposit, flight-cancellation compensation and maintenance costs. Aerotron's public-policy argument under the Brussels I Recast Regulation failed, so it appealed and, pending that appeal, applied for a stay of enforcement, warning that if the appeal succeeded it might struggle to recover from Hermes any sums it had already paid or had enforced against it in the meantime. This 7 August 2026 ruling ([2026] EWHC 2111 (KB)) decides that stay application, illustrating how English courts weigh a losing party's risk of unrecoverable payment against a winning party's entitlement to enforce a foreign money judgment while an appeal is pending. Neither party was represented by a firm on Meridian's tracked list; Hermes' English solicitors were W Legal.",
  c180: "Veranova Bidco LP, an acquisition vehicle backed by private equity firm Altaris, bought Johnson Matthey's pharmaceutical business and later complained that it had not been told, before signing, that one of the business's customers had triggered a contractual price-review mechanism affecting a key product. Pursuing the claim with what the judge called 'relentless aggression', Veranova argued this omission amounted to fraud, which mattered because the sale agreement otherwise barred recovery for breach of warranty absent fraud. Mrs Justice Dias found that Veranova had proved a breach of warranty on the facts, but held the fraud case failed: English law will not treat a company as dishonest merely because one employee knew the underlying facts and a different, entirely innocent employee separately knew the relevant warranty wording — the individual states of mind cannot be combined to manufacture corporate dishonesty. The result left Veranova with a proven breach but no remedy, given the SPA's fraud-only carve-out from the warranty limitations, and the case is now a leading authority on the limits of attributing knowledge to establish corporate fraud in M&A disputes. Linklaters acted for Veranova Bidco (counsel Tony Singla KC, Michael Bolding and Jessie Ingle); Herbert Smith Freehills Kramer acted for Johnson Matthey (counsel Simon Rainey KC, David Caplan and Ruihan Liu).",
  c176: "The Court of Appeal gave further case-management directions in the continuing jurisdiction dispute between Sucden Financial and Mr Prateek Gupta, arising from Sucden's fraud claim over an alleged nickel-cargo/margin-debt shortfall exceeding $6.6m, following the July 2026 ruling (c149, [2026] EWCA Civ 986) that upheld the English court's jurisdiction over him. Foxton LJ held that Gupta must serve his defence within 28 days notwithstanding a pending application to the Supreme Court challenging that jurisdiction ruling — his second such challenge — while extending the deadline for a further acknowledgment of service until 14 days after that application is finally resolved. Sucden gave undertakings not to treat compliance with the directions as submission to the jurisdiction and to indemnify Gupta's costs if his Supreme Court application ultimately succeeds. The ruling balances a defendant's ongoing appellate rights against a claimant's interest in avoiding indefinite delay to proceedings that have already survived two jurisdictional challenges.",
  c175: "Trafigura sold a cargo of gasoline to Société Nationale de Raffinage (SONARA), Cameroon's majority state-owned oil refiner, with payment secured by an irrevocable letter of credit; after SONARA rejected the cargo, it turned to the Limbe court in Cameroon seeking to suspend its payment obligation under the credit rather than pay first and dispute later. Trafigura argued this breached clause 23G of the sale contract, which gave the English High Court exclusive jurisdiction, and applied to the Commercial Court for a final anti-suit injunction restraining the Cameroonian proceedings. Mr Justice Michael Green agreed, holding that SONARA's real aim was to suspend payment and unwind the parties' agreed allocation of risk under the letter of credit, not to obtain ancillary interim protection, so the narrow exception in the jurisdiction clause for protective measures did not apply. The decision reinforces English courts' willingness to enforce exclusive jurisdiction clauses in commodity sale contracts against attempts to sidestep letter-of-credit payment obligations through parallel foreign proceedings.",
  c172: "Five funds in the Cheyne European Special Situations group hold €71.3 million of senior secured notes and say a debt 'up-tiering' transaction that benefited Redwood Capital, and the security agent's subsequent enforcement steps, were invalid. Because near-identical validity challenges are already being litigated in New York, the security-agent defendants asked the English court to stay these proceedings pending the US outcome, notwithstanding an exclusive English jurisdiction clause in the governing intercreditor agreement. Mr Justice Leech reserved judgment on the stay application, with the case turning on how far an exclusive jurisdiction clause should yield to case-management concerns about parallel litigation of the same up-tiering dispute, and on the different tests that apply depending on whether Brussels I Recast governs the question. The ruling will be a useful data point on English courts' willingness to stay proceedings under an English-law intercreditor agreement in favour of parallel US litigation over a liability-management transaction.",
  c173: "Scottish dairy farmers borrowed from a lender that, a Scottish court later found, was run by an owner who fabricated loan documents and misrepresented lending terms on an industrial scale. Relying on that fraud, the farmers had lost a 2014 English judgment dismissing their claims against both the lender and the receiver it had appointed over their farms, and later sought to set that judgment aside — not just against the fraudulent lender, but against the receiver, who had not personally lied but whose defence of the 2014 claim depended on and adopted the lender's fabricated evidence. The Court of Appeal held that a judgment procured by fraud can be set aside even against an 'innocent' co-defendant where their case was parasitic on the fraudster's dishonesty and they took the benefit of it, and rejected the receiver's argument that the farmers had delayed too long given how long the underlying fraud took to uncover. The decision is a useful illustration of how a judgment tainted by one party's fraud can be unwound even against a co-defendant not personally implicated in the deception.",
  c171: "Cooke, Young & Keidan LLP (CYK), a firm of solicitors, sued its former clients Laurence Davis and John Baker for unpaid fees arising from CYK's representation of them in earlier commercial litigation. Both defendants raised a counterclaim alleging CYK had acted negligently in that earlier litigation, but Baker had in the meantime become bankrupt, raising the threshold question of whether his half of the counterclaim was even his to bring. HHJ Johns KC held that a bankrupt's causes of action — including a professional-negligence counterclaim of this kind — vest automatically in the Official Receiver on bankruptcy under section 306 of the Insolvency Act 1986, so Baker had no standing to pursue it personally, and rejected an argument that the counterclaim was instead a partnership asset he and Davis held jointly (which might have avoided the vesting problem). The judge also refused CYK's applications for a preliminary issue and to strike out Davis's separate counterclaim as an abuse akin to 'warehousing' a claim, meaning Davis's counterclaim survives to trial even though Baker's does not. The ruling is a useful illustration of how bankruptcy can silently extinguish a litigant's ability to pursue even a well-founded counterclaim unless the trustee or Official Receiver formally assigns it back.",
  c170: "Augmentum Fintech plc, a London-listed investment trust focused on early- and growth-stage fintech companies, agreed to be taken private by Frontier Bidco Limited, a vehicle owned by Swedish private equity house Verdane's fund manager, for 111 pence in cash per ordinary share. Because the transaction was structured as a Part 26 Companies Act 2006 scheme of arrangement rather than a contractual offer, it required the sanction of the court following a meeting of the company's shareholders at which the requisite statutory majorities voted in favour. Mr Justice Marcus Smith reviewed the scheme against the well-established checklist for court sanction — proper compliance with the statutory meeting and voting requirements, fair representation of the affected class at the meeting, and whether an intelligent and honest shareholder acting in their own interest might reasonably approve the scheme — and found no obstacle to approval, noting the cash price represented a reasonable premium to the pre-announcement share price. The judgment sanctioned the scheme, clearing the way for Augmentum's take-private and delisting.",
  c169: "Magic Investments SA, a minority investor in The Greater Good Fresh Brewing Co Limited, held a nomination agreement it said entitled it to a seat on the company's board, and complained that the majority shareholder, Ralph Thierry Broadbent, had frozen it out — including via a share issuance at an undervalue offered pro rata to all shareholders — in a way that unfairly prejudiced its interests within the meaning of section 994 of the Companies Act 2006. The lower courts struck out Magic's unfair-prejudice petition, reasoning that the nomination agreement gave only a right to nominate rather than a guaranteed board seat, that a pro rata rights-style issuance offered equally to all shareholders could not found unfair prejudice, and that Mr Broadbent's later offer to buy Magic's shares disposed of any live grievance. The Court of Appeal (Newey, Asplin and May LJJ) disagreed on all three points: the nomination agreement, properly construed, conferred a substantive right to a board seat; an allegedly undervalued issuance can still be unfairly prejudicial even where offered pro rata, if it dilutes a minority who cannot afford to take up their entitlement; and a buy-out offer made almost eight months after the petition, without provision for the petitioner's costs, was not enough to dispose of the case. The petition was reinstated and remitted for full hearing.",
  c168: "Aircraft lessor CIT Group Finance (Ireland) leased two Boeing 737-8 MAX aircraft to Indian carrier SpiceJet, which fell into rent arrears it attributed partly to the pandemic. The parties agreed early termination agreements (ETAs) allowing SpiceJet to redeliver the aircraft on a less onerous redelivery condition than the original leases required, but the ETAs gave CIT the right to treat them as void — and revert to enforcing the original, stricter lease terms — if SpiceJet failed to comply. When SpiceJet did not meet the renegotiated redelivery condition, CIT invoked that reversion right and sought summary judgment for unpaid rent and redelivery costs under the original leases; SpiceJet resisted, arguing CIT's decision to revert breached the Braganza duty of rational, good-faith contractual decision-making and that redelivery acceptance certificates it had received estopped CIT from pursuing the lease-based claims. Simon Colton KC, sitting as a judge of the Commercial Court, rejected both defences and granted CIT summary judgment, holding clause 13.14 of the ETAs operated exactly as its plain wording provided and that the acceptance certificates did not extend to barring claims arising from SpiceJet's own antecedent breaches of the original leases. The decision is a significant data point on how English courts will construe 'snapback' provisions increasingly used in aviation-finance workout agreements. Clifford Chance acted for CIT; SpiceJet was represented by King & Spalding, not a firm on Meridian's tracked list.",
  c167: "Mr Shahzad was adjudicated bankrupt in Denmark, and an English court had previously recognised that Danish bankruptcy as 'foreign main proceedings' under the Cross-Border Insolvency Regulations 2006 (CBIR), enabling the Danish trustee to act in relation to the debtor's English assets. Mr Shahzad applied to set aside that recognition order on public-policy grounds, arguing the Danish bankruptcy was, in substance, being used to enforce Danish tax liabilities against him — something English public policy has long refused to permit foreign states to do indirectly through insolvency recognition (drawing on older authorities such as Buchanan and QRS1 concerning the non-enforcement of foreign revenue claims). ICC Judge Agnello KC examined the composition of the Danish bankruptcy's creditor body and found the Danish tax authority held under 10% of the total admitted claims, so it was not — unlike in Buchanan or QRS1 — the estate's sole or dominant creditor, and there was no evidence the Danish trustee was acting under the tax authority's direction rather than independently for the collective benefit of all creditors, who would share pari passu in any distribution. On that basis the judge held the recognition order was not, in substance, an impermissible enforcement of foreign revenue law, and dismissed Mr Shahzad's set-aside application, leaving the Danish trustee's recognised status intact.",
  c166: "SOCAR, Azerbaijan's state oil company, is pursuing English proceedings to enforce roughly $240m of arbitration awards against businessman Mubariz Mansimov and a network of associated entities, alleging he restructured his assets in ways designed to frustrate enforcement. Having obtained permission to serve the claim out of the jurisdiction, SOCAR faced an application from the defendants to set that permission aside on the grounds that England was not the proper forum and that the claims lacked merit. Mr Justice Birt held there was a serious issue to be tried on SOCAR's claims that the restructurings amounted to transactions defrauding creditors under section 423 of the Insolvency Act 1986 and to an unlawful-means conspiracy, but found no serious issue on a further 'Marex tort' claim because no arbitration award existed at the time the impugned restructuring took place, so there was no accrued right capable of being interfered with. Concluding England was the appropriate forum for the surviving claims, the judge dismissed the defendants' set-aside application, allowing SOCAR's enforcement-focused fraud claims to proceed. Clifford Chance acted for SOCAR; the defendants were represented by King & Spalding, not a firm on Meridian's tracked list.",
  c165: "Union Bancaire Privée (UBP) sought pre-action disclosure from L Catterton Europe ahead of an anticipated deceit claim, alleging the private-equity firm knew that valuation representations it made in 2023 about its investment in the clothing brand Jott had already become false by the time they were given. Before issuing a claim, UBP asked the court to order L Catterton to disclose documents under CPR 31.16, arguing early disclosure would help it plead a properly particularised fraud case and might assist in disposing of the dispute without full litigation. Louise Hutton KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, found that the jurisdictional thresholds for pre-action disclosure were satisfied — a prospective claim, an anticipated proceeding, and disclosure that would be within the scope of standard disclosure once proceedings began — but declined to exercise her discretion in UBP's favour. She held UBP's requested categories of documents were too broad for the pre-action stage, and that the more proportionate course was for disclosure to proceed in the ordinary way after a claim was actually issued and pleaded. Travers Smith acted for UBP.",
  c164: "Mohammed Sarwar, Robina Kausar and Shahelah Ali — the father, mother and sister of Bilal Sarwar — petitioned under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006 alleging that Bilal had unfairly conducted the affairs of Cambridge Hotel and Lodge Limited, the family company running a hotel business, to strip them of their shareholdings. The parents said they never genuinely agreed to transfer their 50% stake to Bilal under an August 2025 'division agreement', while the sister said her 25% stake, transferred to Bilal in 2022, was only ever meant to be held on a temporary basis pending a later re-transfer. HHJ Stephen Davies, sitting as a High Court Judge in the Business and Property Courts in Manchester, heard extensive factual evidence about the family's dealings and found on the balance of probabilities that the parents did genuinely intend to transfer their shares under the division agreement and that no temporary arrangement was agreed for the sister's shares. He therefore dismissed the unfair-prejudice petition in its entirety and granted Bilal declaratory relief confirming his title to the disputed shareholding. The judgment is a reminder that family shareholder disputes turn heavily on contemporaneous documentary evidence and credibility findings rather than on generalised assertions of unfairness.",
  c163: "Kession Capital Ltd, an FCA-authorised firm that provided regulatory hosting services to appointed representatives, entered administration after facing claims from investors who had lost money in collective investment schemes it had promoted or approved financial promotions for. When the administrators put their proposals to a creditors' decision procedure, they classified three creditors as 'connected' (entitled to vote at the face value of their claims) and treated the remaining 25 unconnected creditors' claims as worth only a nominal £1 each pending further investigation, producing a result the unconnected creditors said unfairly entrenched the connected creditors' control. Deputy ICC Judge Curl KC agreed the valuation approach had created an artificial impasse and that the unconnected creditors had justifiable and objectively well-founded reasons to lose confidence in the administrators' independence and conduct. He therefore made an order (with written reasons following on 7 April 2026) removing the administrators, replacing them, converting the company into liquidation, and ordering that the outgoing administrators bear the costs of the application.",
  c161: "Mr Gladwin, acting as a litigant in person, sought to bring a professional negligence and fraud claim against the former administrators of Killean Estates Limited — a company he incorporated in 2015 to acquire and develop the Killean Estate in Scotland, of which he was sole director and shareholder. Because the claim targeted former administrators, he first needed the court's permission under paragraph 75(6) of Schedule B1 to the Insolvency Act 1986 to bring proceedings against them, and had been made subject to an 'unless' order requiring him to file amended points of claim, an application to amend the claim form and join the correct defendants, and the necessary permission application by a set deadline. When he failed to comply with the unless order, the court struck out the claim, rejecting his attempt to run the case without observing the procedural gateway insolvency law imposes for claims against former office-holders. The decision reiterates that litigants in person are not excused from complying with unless orders or from seeking the statutory permission required before suing a company's former administrators, however sympathetically the underlying grievance might otherwise be viewed.",
  c160: "TG Jones High Street Limited and TG Jones Retail Holdings Limited — the former WH Smith high-street chain, acquired by Modella Capital from WH Smith plc in 2025 — proposed two inter-conditional Part 26A restructuring plans to compromise claims of secured lenders, landlords, suppliers and local authorities across a c.451-store estate, backed by a c.£15m new-money injection from sponsor Modella Capital. Following a sanction hearing on 1 July 2026, at which cross-class cram-down was used against a dissenting secured-creditor class after opposition raised at the earlier convening hearing was not maintained, Mr Justice Hildyard sanctioned both plans; his written reasons were handed down over a month later, on 5 August 2026. The judgment records that the plan companies were represented by Tom Smith KC, Ryan Perkins and Jon Colclough (instructed by Slaughter and May), the BL landlord class by Ben Shaw KC (instructed by Hogan Lovells International LLP), and St Albans City and District Council by David Adams. Hildyard J's reasons flagged concerns about Modella Capital retaining its equity and the 'high level of execution risk' in the turnaround, describing the plans as 'the lesser of two evils' compared with the alternative of insolvent administration.",
  c141: "A landlord and a former tenant, Newsweek Publishing LLC, were in dispute over a rent deposit and a separate settlement sum said to total around £301,000, which NW Publishing UK Ltd (the landlord entity) claimed as an unpaid debt. Rather than suing for the sum, the creditor threatened to present a winding-up petition against Newsweek Publishing, prompting Newsweek to apply for an injunction restraining presentation of any such petition on the grounds that the debt was genuinely disputed on substantial grounds — the well-established rule that winding-up petitions should not be used to resolve bona fide disputes about whether money is owed at all. ICC Judge Agnello KC agreed, finding the alleged debt was indeed genuinely disputed (covering issues around the application of the rent deposit and the scope of the settlement), and granted the injunction restraining presentation of a petition. The judge also ordered the creditor to pay costs on the indemnity basis, reflecting the court's disapproval of using insolvency process as a debt-collection tool where a real dispute exists — a reminder that the threat of a winding-up petition is not a substitute for litigating a contested debt claim.",
  c142: "Brokers acting for Next Generation Holdings and a related claimant misappropriated around £3.51m of client money over a period before a 2017 share sale, concealing the shortfall through false accounting entries that masked the true financial position of the business being sold. After the fraud came to light, the claimants brought proceedings against those responsible (Alec Finch and others) for breach of fiduciary duty, seeking recovery of the misappropriated client money, the costs of investigating the fraud, and additional damages for trading losses said to flow from the concealment. The Court of Appeal (Snowden, Peter Jackson and Bean LJJ) upheld the defendants' liability for the client-money shortfall itself and for the reasonable costs of investigating the fraud, holding those losses were a direct consequence of the breach of fiduciary duty. However, the court reversed the lower court's separate award of damages for trading losses, holding those losses were too remote and did not flow directly from the fiduciaries' breach — narrowing the scope of recoverable consequential loss in fiduciary-breach claims arising from concealed financial misconduct.",
  c143: "Private-equity investor Freshstream acquired an indirect minority stake in Big Motoring World — a used-car dealership network built by founder Peter Waddell — through Bluebell Cars Holding Limited in March 2022, taking protective minority rights including a call option to buy a further 35% stake and powers to investigate 'Material Default Events' such as discriminatory or harassing employee conduct. Freshstream-appointed directors used those powers to investigate Waddell, suspending him in March 2024 and summarily dismissing him in April 2024 for gross misconduct across 31 alleged incidents; Waddell's holding company petitioned that the process and the exercise of Step-In Rights were unfairly prejudicial under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006, while also disputing the lawfulness of his dismissal. Mr Justice Marcus Smith heard the conjoined wrongful-dismissal claim and unfair-prejudice petition together, working through the Employee Handbook's disciplinary procedure and the validity of the Step-In Rights and Material Default Event notices. Quinn Emanuel Urquhart & Sullivan acted for Mr Waddell, with Willkie Farr & Gallagher for Freshstream, Stephenson Harwood for BIG and Kennedys for the other director defendant.",
  c144: "QuidPay Finance, a customer of payment-services provider OpenPayd (operated by SettleGo Solutions), was required under clause 15 of OpenPayd's General Terms of Use to maintain a reserve account funded from its own money as security against potential liabilities to OpenPayd. After OpenPayd terminated the relationship under clause 22.6, a dispute arose over whether clause 15's reserve-account obligation survived termination — entitling OpenPayd to continue holding QuidPay's reserved funds — or was a primary obligation that fell away once the contract ended. Lance Ashworth KC, sitting as a deputy judge of the Chancery Division, held that the reserve-account obligation does not survive termination: it is a primary obligation tied to the ongoing relationship, not an accrued right capable of outlasting it, so OpenPayd was required to repay QuidPay's reserved funds immediately on termination. OpenPayd was represented by counsel instructed by A&O Shearman.",
  c145: "FW Aviation and VietJet had already been through two trials arising from VietJet's termination of Japanese Operating Lease with Call Option (JOLCO) leases over aircraft, with this third trial before Mr Justice Birt resolving the remaining quantum issues. The court considered claims for the cost of repairing and maintaining four aircraft to the condition required for redelivery, lost rental income attributable to delays in exporting the aircraft from Vietnam, enforcement costs, and a dispute over whether FW Aviation — as successor to the original financing parties rather than as aircraft owner — could recover under the leases' indemnity provisions at all. The judgment turns on detailed construction of the JOLCO lease and financing documents governing each head of loss. FW Aviation was represented by Quinn Emanuel Urquhart & Sullivan and VietJet by King & Spalding.",
  c138: "Specialist FX and payments firm Argentex LLP entered special administration in July 2025 with an unhedged trading book of forward and option contracts that, at the moment of appointment, was net 'in the money' — meaning an immediate close-out would crystallise a profit for creditors, but at the expense of customers who were themselves 'out of the money' on their individual contracts and would rather let them run to maturity. The joint special administrators sought directions from the court as to whether Argentex's standard customer terms — which allowed it to close out contracts where it 'reasonably consider[ed] it necessary for its own protection' — entitled them to close out the whole book early, addressing both Argentex's own insolvency and the economic effect of its unhedged position. Representative parties argued the clause on each side: Richard Fisher KC (South Square) for customers who were 'out of the money' and opposed early close-out, and Marcus Haywood (South Square) for those 'in the money'. The court held that, properly construed, the 'own protection' clause could not be used to protect Argentex from the consequences of its own insolvency or from the hedging losses inherent in running an unhedged book — those were risks Argentex bore itself, distinct from the customer-side risks (such as a customer's own default) the clause was designed to address — and accordingly refused the administrators' request for directions permitting early close-out.",
  c118: "QuidPay Finance Limited, an online payments company, sued digital-banking and payments provider SettleGo Solutions Limited (trading as OpenPayd) after OpenPayd suspended QuidPay's accounts and withheld funds, citing alleged fraud linked to QuidPay's clients and unspecified regulatory and legal obligations. The court ruled on competing preliminary-issue applications: it held that the words 'or cannot' should remain in the framing of the second preliminary issue, allowing OpenPayd to rely on regulatory or legal constraints as a defence to payment, and adjourned QuidPay's application for disclosure of documents — including a Suspicious Activity Report — to trial rather than resolving it immediately. The claimant was represented by counsel instructed by Lewis Silkin LLP; the defendant by counsel instructed by Allen Overy Shearman Sterling LLP (A&O Shearman). The decision illustrates the courts' caution about ordering early disclosure of SAR-related material in payments disputes where a provider raises AML/regulatory justifications for withholding funds.",
  c120: "Nvayo Limited, an FCA-regulated electronic money institution, entered special administration under the Payment and Electronic Money Institution Insolvency Regulations 2021. Christopher Scanlon — Nvayo's former CEO, a majority unsecured creditor and (in a small part) a customer — applied to amend his existing challenge to the special administrators' remuneration, which had reached £1.89m after the administration's first year, seeking to rely on additional statutory gateways and to bring a separate challenge to the administrators' 'objective 1' costs in his capacity as a customer, despite holding only about 0.1% of customer claims. Deputy ICC Judge Baister ruled on the scope of the permitted amendments. The administrators were represented by counsel instructed by Trowers & Hamlins LLP; Mr Scanlon by counsel instructed by Kingsley Napley LLP. The case illustrates the procedural latitude — and limits — afforded to creditors challenging office-holder remuneration in the still-developing special administration regime for failed payments and e-money firms.",
  c122: "US and other institutional investors, led by the California State Teachers' Retirement System, brought claims against Boohoo Group plc under section 90A and Schedule 10A of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, alleging the fast-fashion retailer published misleading annual reports, market announcements and modern-slavery statements between January 2017 and September 2020 that concealed poor working conditions at a Leicester supplier factory, before media reports triggered a roughly 42% share-price fall in July 2020. At a case-management conference, Mr Justice Michael Green (Financial List) resolved several procedural questions ahead of trial: which claimants would serve as 'reliance sample' representatives for the trial of individual investor reliance, what expert evidence on corporate broking practice and econometric 'market price reliance' would be permitted, and — declining as premature — the claimants' request for early disclosure of documents underlying the reliance forms. Boohoo was represented by counsel instructed by Herbert Smith Freehills Kramer; the claimants by counsel instructed by Fox Williams LLP. The ruling is a significant case-management staging post in one of the largest live s.90A securities actions against a UK-listed company, with trial on reliance issues not expected before October 2027.",
  c123: "Importers Service Corporation and a co-claimant applied under section 423 of the Insolvency Act 1986 to reverse asset transfers made by Aliotta Holdings Limited, alleging the transfers were structured to put assets beyond creditors' reach. Following the court's earlier substantive ruling, Mr Simon Gleeson determined the consequential matters — costs and the scope of relief — finding that Mr Aliotta had backdated board minutes and fabricated evidence during the proceedings, and that the defendants had made common cause in defending the impugned transfers. Although the claimants succeeded on one application and failed on another, the judge awarded them their costs in full, and indemnity costs against all defendants, given the seriousness of the concealment and fabrication found against them. The decision is a reminder that fabricating evidence in section 423 proceedings carries a heavy costs price even where a claimant's own applications only partially succeed.",
  c124: "The joint liquidators of MBI International & Partners Inc sought to enforce a Supreme Court judgment and a charging order against a London property owned by Sheikh Mohamed Bin Issa Al Jaber via a possession-and-sale claim, serving the claim on his solicitors by email after service attempts at his Paris address failed. The First Defendant applied to set aside the order permitting that alternative service. Deputy Master Holden dismissed the application, holding there was good reason for alternative service given the defendant's apparent tactical avoidance of service and the liquidators' legitimate need to progress enforcement of a Supreme Court judgment without further delay. The ruling illustrates the courts' willingness to sanction alternative service against defendants who appear to be evading formal service in cross-border insolvency enforcement.",
  c125: "A shareholder of Continental Clothing Company Limited brought a derivative claim seeking the court's permission under Part 11 of the Companies Act 2006 to pursue a claim on the company's behalf over its alleged entitlement to shares or profits in a German subsidiary, based on an oral agreement he said was made in 2002. Andrew de Mestre KC, sitting as a Deputy Judge, refused permission, holding that the claimant's account of the oral agreement was contradicted by contemporaneous documentary evidence — correspondence, company accounts and a history of payments made to him inconsistent with the claimed entitlement — and that no director acting for the good of the company would reasonably continue such a weak claim. The judge also noted the claimant remained free to pursue the underlying dispute personally, without exposing the company itself to litigation costs risk.",
  c126: "Gallagher Benefit Services Management Company Ltd, which had acquired insurance broker Churchills from Peter Meagher for £12.375m plus a conditional earnout, brought proceedings alleging Mr Meagher failed to disclose before completion that the firm's largest client — worth roughly £419,000 in annual revenue — had given notice to terminate its relationship, and that this non-disclosure induced Gallagher to settle the earnout calculation at £3.65m rather than a figure closer to £1.8m reflecting the lost client. Mr Meagher denies misrepresentation, contending the termination notice was never effective and that the client relationship in fact continued through extended renegotiation. Mr Justice Ritchie's judgment addresses the parties' competing accounts of the disclosure and earnout-negotiation history; Gallagher was represented by counsel instructed by Mayer Brown.",
  c127: "A dispute between former business partners turned on whether a 'business connexion' with a key supplier — Acon, a supplier of COVID-19 lateral-flow tests — was itself a partnership asset. After the partnership dissolved, one partner continued to exploit that supplier relationship through his own, separate companies. The Court of Appeal (Newey and Snowden LJJ, Falk LJ agreeing) upheld the first-instance finding that the business connexion was a partnership asset, so the partner who exploited it post-dissolution is liable to account to his former partners for the profits made, under section 29 of the Partnership Act 1890 (a partner's duty to account for profits from use of partnership property after the partnership ends). Quantification of those profits was left to be determined at a further trial.",
  c128: "Residential mortgage borrowers appealed against orders in a mortgage possession claim brought by Swansea Building Society, challenging both the validity of the underlying charge and the grant of interim relief permitting a sale of the property pending final resolution. The Court of Appeal (Zacaroli, Miles and Dove LJJ) allowed the appeal only on a narrow procedural ground — that the appellants had been wrongly refused an oral hearing to reconsider the initial refusal of permission to appeal — but dismissed their substantive challenges to the charge's validity and to the interim relief permitting sale, meaning the underlying possession and sale process was otherwise unaffected.",
  c131: "In a power-plant EPC dispute, the claimants sought to restrain calls on performance and advance-payment bonds issued by Santander after the underlying contract was terminated for force majeure, and applied to an arbitral tribunal for interim relief to that effect; the tribunal refused. The claimants then challenged that refusal under section 68 of the Arbitration Act 1996, alleging serious irregularity. Mrs Justice Dias dismissed the challenge, holding that a tribunal's procedural order refusing interim relief is not an 'award' capable of section 68 challenge, and that in any event no binding agreement existed between the parties on the legal test the tribunal should have applied to the interim-relief application. The defendant, IKC, was represented by counsel instructed by White & Case.",
  c134: "P1 Pit Stop Limited, incorporated in 2014 by John Palmer and Howard Forland on an intended equal footing, had never properly maintained a statutory register of members, leaving its true shareholding in doubt after a disputed 2018 confirmation statement purported to record share allotments and transfers, including shares said to be held by Magna Secretaries Limited on trust arrangements the parties disputed. Palmer and Magna applied under section 125 of the Companies Act 2006 to rectify the register; Mr Forland and his wife Elaina Forland were co-defendants, along with a further individual, Myles Beerman. Deputy ICC Judge Parfitt, hearing Mr Palmer and Mr Forland in person (Magna did not appear), reconstructed the register from scratch and held that the evidence supported an equal 50/50 split between the Palmer and Forland sides, rectifying the register to show Mr Palmer holding 250 ordinary shares and Mr and Mrs Forland holding 200 and 50 shares respectively, each of £0.10 nominal value. The case is a practical illustration of the court's section 125 jurisdiction to reconstruct a company's ownership history where a young company never kept a proper register and its filed confirmation statements cannot be taken at face value.",
  c137: "A Group Litigation Order test case had earlier established, as a common GLO issue, that the common law recognised a restitutionary claim for interest accruing during the 'prematurity period' — the period between a taxpayer paying tax and that tax later being offset or repaid. After the test case was decided, later appellate authority established that no such restitutionary claim in fact existed at common law, leaving 'follower' claimants who had relied on the (now-wrong) test case ruling in a position where their claims were technically still bound by a legally incorrect precedent. AXA Insurance and a co-appellant, follower claimants in the GLO, argued the test-case decision should no longer bind their claims, and separately that a limitation-period question had not actually been decided as a common GLO issue and so remained open. The Supreme Court held that courts have discretion under CPR 19.23(1)(a) to order that a test-case decision should not bind follower claims where circumstances warrant it, that requiring the follower claims to proceed on a basis 'now known to be wrong in law' would conflict with fundamental judicial principles, and that the limitation issue must therefore be decided under current law rather than treated as concluded by the test case. The decision confirms the flexibility available to manage GLOs where intervening case law overtakes an earlier test-case ruling, of practical relevance to any large tax (or other) group litigation run through lead/follower claims.",
  c106: "Marcos De Menezes and Eduardo Alves co-founded and co-directed Oficina Inglesa Limited (OIL), a Chelsea Harbour luxury-furniture business, in 2008. After their personal relationship broke down in 2022, Alves incorporated a new company, Cobogo Gallery Limited, selling contemporary Brazilian furniture from the same showroom, and De Menezes sought permission to bring a derivative claim on OIL's behalf alleging Alves breached the no-conflict duty in section 175 of the Companies Act 2006. Mr Justice Fancourt granted permission, holding that 'riding two horses' in directly competing markets from the same premises created an obvious conflict of interest, that a real risk of substantial harm to OIL was made out even without a currently quantifiable loss, that informal WhatsApp exchanges did not amount to valid board authorisation or ratification, and that the alternative unfair-prejudice remedy under section 994 was not obviously preferable to a derivative claim. The case is a reminder that co-director conflicts arising from a broken personal relationship are assessed by ordinary section 175 principles, not treated more leniently because the underlying dispute is personal rather than purely commercial.",
  c107: "Float Capital Limited moved from administration into creditors' voluntary liquidation, with Geoffrey Bouchier of Kroll Advisory and Ben Woodthorpe of S&W Partners appointed as joint liquidators, and the two sought the court's approval of their remuneration in circumstances where seeking creditor approval would itself have been compromised by a conflict of interest. Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs fixed Mr Bouchier's remuneration on the time-costs basis under rule 18.23 of the Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 at just over £363,000, despite procedural irregularities in how approval was sought, because the alternative of a creditors' vote was itself conflicted. Mr Woodthorpe's basis, by contrast, was treated as already fixed by the deeming provision in rule 18.20(4) carrying over his basis of remuneration as continuing office-holder, so his increased figure of just over £1.27m fell to be approved under rules 18.24/18.28 rather than rule 18.23. The judge imposed safeguards going forward, including estimates to cap further draws, continued SIP 9 reporting and creditor notification rights, illustrating the courts' willingness to police liquidators' fees even on an unopposed application.",
  c108: "Soprim Construction SARL holds arbitration awards exceeding US$100m against the Republic of Djibouti arising from the long-running Doraleh Container Terminal dispute and sought to enforce them by way of a charging order over funds held in London bank accounts in the name of Doraleh Container Terminal SA (DCT), a company Djibouti had expropriated and placed under a court-appointed Djiboutian administrator. The central question was whether the roughly US$41-42m held at Standard Chartered Bank in London belonged beneficially to DCT or to the Republic itself, since only the Republic's assets could satisfy Soprim's awards against it. Mr Justice Picken held that, following an agreement between DCT and its administrator, the funds were held by DCT on bare trust for the Republic of Djibouti, meaning they were in substance the Republic's assets notwithstanding being nominally held in DCT's name. The ruling clears the way for Soprim to pursue charging-order enforcement against the frozen London funds in partial satisfaction of its awards, and illustrates the English courts' willingness to look through corporate and administrator arrangements to identify a state's real beneficial assets for sovereign-debt enforcement purposes.",
  c105: "The claimants, a couple who let out UK property, held an account with Byblos Bank SAL, a Lebanese bank, into which their rental income was paid; they sought to enforce a £1.3m transfer instruction against the bank in the English courts, relying on the consumer-contract jurisdictional gateway in the Civil Jurisdiction and Judgments Act 1982 to displace the account's exclusive-jurisdiction clause in favour of Lebanon. Byblos Bank argued the account was opened and operated for the claimants' property-letting business, not personal consumption, so the consumer gateway did not apply. Deputy High Court Judge O'Mahony agreed with the bank, finding on the evidence that the account was a business account used to receive rental income from the claimants' property business, and accordingly the Lebanese exclusive-jurisdiction clause governed and the claim could not proceed in England. The decision is a useful illustration of how courts distinguish 'business' from 'consumer' banking relationships when a claimant seeks to invoke consumer-protective jurisdictional gateways.",
  c103: "A 50% shareholder in Mulberry Ltd sought permission to bring a double-derivative claim on behalf of AI Pathfinder Inc (AIP), a US subsidiary, challenging a January 2026 £17.7m sale of AIP's assets to a management-buyout vehicle (SAI) as a transaction at an undervalue involving breach of fiduciary duty by AIP's directors and shadow directors. The claimant pointed to AIP's earlier US$500m seed-round valuation as evidence the sale price was too low. Mr Justice Trower refused permission, holding that AIP's subsequent financial distress made the seed-round valuation irrelevant to assessing the January 2026 sale price, that the claimant had not shown any defendant profited dishonestly from the transaction, and that the alleged wrongdoers did not control AIP in the way required to bring the claim within the exceptions to the rule in Foss v Harbottle. The decision is a reminder of the high bar for double-derivative claims and the need for contemporaneous, not historic, valuation evidence when alleging an undervalue disposal.",
  c104: "Al Othman had advanced a US$5m interest-free loan to Al Rajhi in November 2014, structured to let Al Othman exit an investment in Al Salam Energy Ltd without a formal share sale. Years later Al Othman sought to recover the loan, but Al Rajhi argued the claim was time-barred under the Limitation Act 1980. The dispute turned on whether a November 2018 email from Al Rajhi acknowledging 'the amount due' was a valid acknowledgment of the debt that would restart the limitation clock, given the email also contained an ambiguous reference to a 'sale' that in fact never completed. The Court of Appeal (Males, Phillips and Lewison LJJ) allowed Al Othman's appeal, holding that a reasonable recipient in Al Othman's position would have understood the email as acknowledging liability under the Loan Agreement notwithstanding the ambiguous wording. The decision is a useful illustration of how acknowledgment of a debt is assessed objectively from the reasonable recipient's perspective in cross-border lending disputes.",
  c102: "Elite Property Holdings and connected respondents faced an insolvency application from the liquidators of Health and Home Ltd; the respondents' witness evidence was filed eight days outside the court-ordered deadline, and the first-instance judge refused relief from sanctions under CPR 3.9, applying the strict Denton framework applicable to trials. On appeal the respondents argued the final hearing of an insolvency application was not a 'trial' within CPR 32.10 and so should attract a more lenient approach to late evidence. The Court of Appeal disagreed, holding that a substantive insolvency hearing involving disclosure, pleadings, witness statements and cross-examination has all the hallmarks of a trial and so is treated as one for case-management purposes, and dismissed the appeal. The decision confirms that litigants in insolvency proceedings cannot expect a softer approach to procedural compliance than in ordinary civil trials.",
  c01: "A Russian bankruptcy trustee sought to reach a London townhouse owned by the bankrupt. The Supreme Court held that the long-standing 'immovables rule' prevents a foreign insolvency from affecting English land, which is governed by the law of its situs and falls outside the foreign bankruptcy. The decision preserves an important territorial limit on cross-border insolvency assistance.",
  c02: "Centrica incurred professional fees on the disposal of a loss-making subsidiary and claimed them as deductible 'expenses of management' of its investment business. The Supreme Court held the fees were capital expenditure on a disposal and not deductible management expenses, clarifying the test. Significant for holding companies and the deductibility of deal costs.",
  c03: "A force majeure clause required the affected party to use reasonable endeavours to overcome the event; the question was whether that obliged MUR to accept payment in a non-contractual currency. The Supreme Court held reasonable endeavours do not require a party to accept non-contractual performance, so MUR could rely on force majeure. An important clarification of force-majeure and reasonable-endeavours wording in finance and commercial contracts.",
  c04: "Directors of a company that infringed trade marks were sued as joint tortfeasors and for an account of profits. The Supreme Court held a director is not liable as an accessory unless they knew the essential facts making the act wrongful, and is not personally liable to account for the company's profits. The decision limits personal director liability for company torts.",
  c05: "Liquidators of BHS sued former directors for wrongful trading and breach of duty after the retailer's collapse. The High Court found the directors liable, recognised a novel 'misfeasant trading' measure of loss and applied the Sequana creditor duty, ordering contributions exceeding £110m. A landmark warning for directors trading in the 'twilight zone' of insolvency.",
  c06: "The first appellate decision on Part 26A restructuring plans, concerning the Adler real-estate group. The Court of Appeal overturned the sanctioned plan, holding that the 'no worse off' test is not the whole story and that the restructuring surplus must be allocated fairly, with pari passu creditors generally treated equally. It set the framework for the cross-class cram down.",
  c07: "A share option was re-granted in connection with the holder's role; the issue was whether it was 'employment-related' under the statutory deeming rule. The Supreme Court held the deeming provision applied, so the option was an employment-related security taxed accordingly. Relevant to management equity and incentive structuring.",
  c08: "The question was whether litigation-funding agreements giving the funder a share of damages are 'damages-based agreements' (DBAs). The Supreme Court held they are, meaning many such agreements were unenforceable unless they complied with the DBA regime. The ruling disrupted the litigation-funding market and prompted ongoing reform efforts.",
  c09: "Mrs Philipp was tricked by fraudsters into instructing Barclays to transfer £700,000 (an authorised push-payment fraud). The Supreme Court held that a bank owes no Quincecare duty to refuse a customer's own validly authorised instruction; its primary duty is to execute instructions promptly. APP-fraud redress now runs through the PSR's mandatory reimbursement scheme rather than the common law.",
  c10: "Mozambique alleged it was the victim of bribery in the 'tuna bonds' scandal and sued; the defendants sought a stay in favour of arbitration. The Supreme Court held the bribery claims fell outside the scope of the arbitration agreements, so the dispute could proceed in court. The case gives guidance on construing the scope of arbitration clauses.",
  c11: "News Corp argued the digital editions of its newspapers should share the historic zero-rate of VAT for 'newspapers'. The Supreme Court held that, at the relevant time, the zero-rate applied only to physical newspapers, so the digital editions were standard-rated. An illustration of the 'always speaking' approach to statutory interpretation in VAT.",
  c12: "The Supreme Court confirmed the directors' 'creditor duty' — the duty to consider creditors' interests when a company is insolvent or bordering on insolvency, or an insolvent liquidation is probable. The weight given to creditors increases as the financial position worsens; it is a sliding scale rather than an on/off switch. The decision is foundational for directors of distressed companies.",
  c13: "Three customers challenged undisclosed commissions paid by lenders to the car dealers who arranged their finance. The Supreme Court held dealers owe no fiduciary or 'disinterested' duty, so bribery and secret-commission claims fail, but upheld Mr Johnson's claim that the relationship was unfair under s.140A of the Consumer Credit Act. The FCA is consulting on an industry redress scheme.",
  c14: "A Ponzi-scheme bank in liquidation alleged HSBC breached its Quincecare duty by executing payments before the fraud collapsed. The Supreme Court held that, because the payments only reduced the bank's net indebtedness, it suffered no recoverable loss. An important decision on loss and the Quincecare duty in an insolvency context.",
  c15: "Marex, a judgment creditor, sued the controller who stripped a company's assets to defeat enforcement; the issue was whether the reflective-loss rule barred the claim. The Supreme Court restated and confined the reflective-loss principle to claims by shareholders in their capacity as shareholders, so creditors are not barred. It is now the leading authority on reflective loss.",
  c16: "ClientEarth, a shareholder, sought permission to bring a derivative claim against Shell's directors over the company's climate strategy. The High Court refused permission, holding that the directors' management of competing considerations was a matter of business judgment for the board, not the court. A significant decision on directors' duties and climate litigation.",
  c17: "Mr Lloyd sought to bring an opt-out representative action for damages on behalf of millions of iPhone users over alleged data tracking. The Supreme Court held the claim could not proceed without proof of damage for each individual, blocking a US-style class action. The ruling shaped the landscape for mass data-protection claims.",
  c18: "The SFO issued a section 2 notice compelling a US parent company to produce documents held abroad. The Supreme Court held that section 2 of the Criminal Justice Act 1987 has no extraterritorial effect over foreign companies. The case is a key limit on the SFO's evidence-gathering powers in cross-border investigations.",
  c19: "One of the first restructuring plans under Part 26A, involving group companies in the subsea-engineering sector. The court exercised the new cross-class cram down for the first time to bind a dissenting class of creditors. A foundational decision for the restructuring-plan tool.",
  c20: "Virgin Active's gym business proposed a plan compromising landlord claims across different classes. The court sanctioned the plan and crammed down dissenting landlords, focusing on the 'relevant alternative' of administration in which they would recover little. An influential early decision for landlord compromises.",
  c21: "An oil company proposed a debt-for-equity plan that would heavily dilute its shareholders. The court refused to sanction it, declining to cram down shareholders where it was not shown they were 'out of the money' in the relevant alternative. The case showed the limits of the cross-class cram down.",
  c22: "A property-management SME proposed a restructuring plan opposed by HMRC as a preferential creditor. The court sanctioned the plan and crammed down HMRC — the first plan to do so — extending the tool to smaller companies. A milestone for SME restructurings.",
  c23: "The case concerned whether charges over a satellite operator's assets were fixed or floating despite limited chargee control. The court held that certain assets were subject to a fixed charge, taking a broader view than some had expected. It reopened the fixed/floating-charge debate that is central to lender priority.",
  c24: "A creditor invoked section 423 of the Insolvency Act (transactions defrauding creditors) where the debtor had caused a company he controlled to dispose of assets. The Supreme Court held section 423 can apply even though the assets were owned by the company rather than the debtor personally. The decision widens the reach of this anti-avoidance provision.",
  c25: "The taxpayer used short-term loan arrangements intended to avoid the obligation to withhold income tax on 'yearly interest'. The Court of Appeal held the interest was yearly interest with a UK source, so the withholding obligation applied. Important for the design of lending structures and withholding tax.",
  c26: "HMRC argued that part-time football referees were employees for tax, turning on mutuality of obligation and control. The Supreme Court held that mutuality and a sufficient framework of control can exist within individual engagements, and remitted the case. A leading modern authority on employment status.",
  c28: "Part of long-running group litigation over the UK's taxation of foreign dividends contrary to EU law. The Supreme Court addressed limitation periods and the availability of mistake-of-law restitution claims. Significant for tax-restitution claims and the running of limitation.",
  c29: "A foreign-exchange trading LLP used a scheme routing trading profits through a corporate member before paying them to individual members as deferred 'special capital'. The Supreme Court held the amounts were taxable as the individual members' income, confirming the courts below. A landmark on LLP remuneration structuring and the limits of purposive tax interpretation.",
  c30: "Petrofac's Part 26A plans were sanctioned at first instance over dissenting creditors. The Court of Appeal set the sanction aside, holding that meeting the 'no worse off' test is not enough — the restructuring surplus must be allocated fairly between classes. The decision raised the evidential bar for the cross-class cram down.",
  c31: "Thames Water's Part 26A plan provided emergency liquidity to the regulated water utility; objectors argued special administration better served the public interest. The Court of Appeal upheld the sanction, holding the plan met the statutory tests and that public interest had only a limited role in the discretion. A high-profile application of the plan to a regulated monopoly.",
  c32: "A shareholders' agreement required the company to work in good faith towards an exit; the chairman concealed from the board that no sale was being pursued. The Court of Appeal found this breached the s.172 duty to promote the company's success, even absent personal gain, and supported unfair-prejudice relief. A practical decision on directors' honesty with the board.",
  c33: "A Chinese property developer used an English restructuring plan to compromise debt governed partly by Hong Kong law. The court sanctioned the plan and applied the cross-class cram down where over 75% of a class had approved and liquidation was the relevant alternative. The case illustrates the plan's cross-border reach.",
  c34: "A 2026 restructuring-plan sanction in the energy-production sector. The court applied the post-Petrofac fairness framework, scrutinising the allocation of the restructuring surplus and the treatment of dissenting creditors before sanctioning the plan. A recent example of plan practice after the Court of Appeal's fairness guidance.",
  c35: "Shares held on trust were transferred in breach of trust to a Saudi bank, and the beneficiaries sued for knowing receipt. The Supreme Court held there was no liability because the claimant's equitable proprietary interest was extinguished on the transfer under the governing (Saudi) law. A leading authority on knowing receipt and cross-border property.",
  c36: "Between 2001 and 2006 Dexia entered into 11 interest rate swap transactions with the City of Turin totalling around €400m, hedging the municipality's variable-rate bond obligations. When interest rates fell for over a decade Turin sought to invalidate the swaps in Italian proceedings, arguing non-disclosure of mark-to-market values and implicit costs under the Cattolica doctrine, and constitutional and civil-code defects. Mr Justice Andrew Baker rejected each argument in full and granted Dexia comprehensive declaratory relief at a trial Turin chose not to attend. He held that Turin's swaps were straightforward hedges — not complex aleatory contracts requiring Cattolica-style disclosure — that express ISDA Master Agreement language ruling out an advisory role protected the bank, and that Turin as a professional investor could not invoke retail-client protections; Turin's non-attendance generated a substantial costs liability and final declarations deployable in Italian enforcement.",
  c37: "Iconic Sports Eagle Investment LLC held a put option over a substantial minority stake (aggregate value exceeding US$100 million) in Eagle Football Holdings Limited, which owns stakes in Olympique Lyonnais and Botafogo FR. After the defendant Textor repudiated the put option agreement, Iconic brought a claim for specific performance and the case raised two issues remitted by the Court of Appeal: whether Iconic was barred from advancing the 'Snell Point' (that a party need only show readiness and willingness to perform at the date of the specific performance order) and whether that point was correct in law. Deputy Judge Anderson KC held that Iconic was not estopped from advancing the argument and that the principle in Snell's Equity — permitting a claimant to demonstrate readiness at the judgment date rather than the contractual completion date when the counterparty has repudiated — correctly states English law. The decision provides practical authority for M&A and private equity practitioners whose clients face repudiation of share option or SPA obligations, confirming that inability to complete on the original timetable does not defeat a specific performance claim where the defendant caused the default.",
  c38: "Hipgnosis Music Limited alleged its former director, Merck Mercuriadis, diverted the company's business of acquiring music royalty catalogues by pursuing an essentially identical model through Hipgnosis Songs Fund Limited (HSFL2), which completed a successful £202 million IPO in July 2018 after the original vehicle failed. Adam Johnson J held that Mercuriadis breached the s.175 CA 2006 duty to avoid conflicts of interest: the corporate opportunity had not been approved or abandoned by the company, and he exploited it through different corporate structures for personal gain, engaging the continuing duties imposed by s.170(2)(a) CA 2006 on former directors. The successor vehicle HSFL2 and its associated management company were found to be accessories to the breach and jointly liable to account for profits. The decision is an important modern statement on the corporate opportunity doctrine and the reach of post-directorship fiduciary obligations under English law.",
  c39: "Bank of India sought to enforce a personal guarantee signed by Nirav Modi — the fugitive Indian diamantaire held at HMP Thameside — securing USD $4.1 million owed by his company Firestar Diamond FZE. The Commercial Court (Tinkler DJ) rejected Modi's argument that the guarantee was void under Indian foreign-exchange regulations (FEMA): the Reserve Bank of India may grant retrospective approval, and the obligation to obtain it rests on the guarantor, not the bank. The court also confirmed that SOFR-based rates replace synthetic LIBOR under the facility documentation following that benchmark's cessation in September 2024, and held that Modi had been validly served with the October 2025 demand notice. Judgment was entered for Bank of India in full.",
  c40: "Deutsche Glasfaser Group GmbH — Germany's leading FTTH fibre-optic infrastructure provider — faced €162m in interest due 30 June 2026 and proposed a creditor scheme of arrangement under s.899 CA 2006 bifurcating its ~€7bn of existing debt into structurally senior opco debt and structurally subordinated holdco debt, alongside a new €400m super-senior facility and an €845m equity injection from EQT and OMERS. Mr Justice Adam Johnson sanctioned the scheme on 24 June 2026, noting that 98 of 100 creditors attended the meeting and voted unanimously in favour, with scheme-scenario recovery (60.8–91.8 cents/€) materially superior to a distressed liquidation (34.2–48.8 cents/€). A last-minute German tax ruling received on 16 June 2026 introduced conditionality over the implementation mechanism, but the court held this did not prevent sanction; the scheme remains binding pending resolution of that point by 24 August 2026. This is a companion to [2026] EWHC 1467 (Ch) (the convening hearing) and confirms the continuing utility of English Part 26 schemes — distinct from Part 26A restructuring plans — for cross-border restructurings of German-incorporated groups with English law-governed facilities.",
  c41: "Musst Holdings Ltd brought a long-running introduction-fee dispute against Astra Asset Management UK Ltd, an alternative investment fund manager (AIFM). In this further judgment the Chancery Division considered whether costs that Musst incurred in earlier, related proceedings between the same parties can be claimed as a head of consequential loss (costs as damages) in the main action, rather than being governed exclusively by the costs regime of those earlier proceedings. The decision is relevant to funds-regulatory litigation where multi-stage disputes generate inter-related costs exposure.",
  c42: "Gordian Holdings Ltd brought further proceedings against Sofroniou in which it alleged transactions defrauding creditors within s.423 Insolvency Act 1986 and sought to enforce personal guarantee obligations. The dispute arises out of Bank of Cyprus lending and is connected to earlier Commercial Court proceedings at [2021] EWHC 235 (Comm). The judgment addresses the scope of s.423 claims and guarantee enforcement in the context of alleged creditor-defrauding transactions.",
  c43: "Songa Shipping SE (as lender) sought to enforce settlement-payment obligations under a bilateral loan agreement; Gardsea Ltd (as borrower) argued that the contractual requirement to pay on a 'Banking Day' fell to be computed by reference to both London and New York banking days, which on the facts of the payment instructions created a notional 37–38-hour window straddling time zones. Henshaw J held that 'Banking Day' in a multi-centre finance agreement is properly construed by reference to the time zone of the place of performance of the relevant payment obligation — here, London, where the payment was to be made — rather than by reference to the opening of the earliest financial centre and the closing of the latest. The decision aligns with LMA standard-form definitions and restores clarity to cross-border multi-currency facilities, confirming that settlement-day-count arithmetic should not introduce latency or ambiguity by spanning time zones.",
  c44: "Barclays Bank UK plc sought judicial review of a Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS) determination that the FOS had jurisdiction to consider a retail customer's complaint, notwithstanding that the complaint had been brought more than six months after Barclays' final response letter — outside the DISP 2.8.2 time-bar. The FOS had purported to exercise a discretion to accept the out-of-time complaint on grounds of exceptional circumstances. Lane J granted the judicial review application, quashing the FOS determination and holding that the six-month DISP 2.8.2 time-bar is a mandatory jurisdictional condition that circumscribes the FOS's compulsory jurisdiction; the FOS has no general discretion to waive it except in the circumstances expressly identified in the DISP sourcebook. The decision is significant for financial institutions facing high volumes of complaints lodged after DISP time-limits, particularly in the context of PPI and motor-finance redress exercises.",
  c45: "PrivatBank had obtained a judgment of roughly US$3 billion against Igor Kolomoisky and Gennadiy Bogolyubov, with a bespoke ring-fencing arrangement protecting designated funds from enforcement so the defendants could fund their appeal. When the Court of Appeal dismissed that appeal on 22 May 2026, a contractual trigger disapplied the ring-fencing, and the dispute concerned whether legal fees already incurred but not yet paid before that date could still be discharged from the protected accounts. Mr Justice Trower held the termination was complete and unqualified, so unpaid pre-existing invoices could not be paid from the ring-fenced funds after the disapplication event. The decision is significant for practitioners structuring litigation-funding ring-fence/carve-out provisions in major asset-recovery and enforcement litigation, showing courts will give full effect to plain disapplication triggers over arguments of unfairness to defendants' legal teams.",
  c46: "Claimants sought to continue interim proprietary injunctions over roughly £9 million that had been advanced by a finance company for mortgage loan origination, improperly redirected by a law firm to a bridging lender, and then distributed to several individual defendants as purported 'investment returns'. The court considered whether the claimants could still trace the funds and defeat a bona fide purchaser for value defence, given that most defendants had received the money as unsolicited windfalls rather than under any contract. Deputy Judge Charles Hollander KC held that defendants who retained early, uncontracted repayments without objection had given consideration through accord and satisfaction, and that notice only crystallised when injunctions were served in April 2026 — discharging the injunction against most defendants but maintaining it against one defendant closely connected to the lender. The decision illustrates the practical limits of proprietary tracing remedies against downstream recipients of misapplied loan finance where funds have been commingled and distributed as apparent investment proceeds.",
  c47: "Cross Transport Ltd had entered a statutory moratorium under the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020 before moving into administration, leaving HMRC as both a protected moratorium creditor and preferential creditor for a substantial sum, against modest remaining assets and a litigation claim that a funder had agreed to back. The issue was whether Paragraph 64A of Schedule B1 to the Insolvency Act 1986, which requires administrators to discharge protected moratorium debts, prevented them from first paying litigation funder costs and administration expenses needed to pursue value-generating litigation. ICC Judge Jones held that the paragraph 64A duty confers super-priority status on protected moratorium creditors rather than mandating literal pre-payment ahead of all other expenses, so administrators retain discretion to fund litigation pragmatically consistent with their fiduciary duties. The ruling gives helpful guidance to insolvency practitioners on funding litigation recoveries in moratorium-linked administrations, an increasingly important restructuring tool since CIGA 2020.",
  c48: "Institutional and retail shareholders brought claims exceeding £1.6 billion against gambling group Entain Plc under sections 90 and 90A of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, alleging that prospectuses and market disclosures over roughly twelve years concealed bribery payments connected to Turkish operations and that senior managers knowingly withheld this information. The key case-management question was whether to bifurcate the trial, and if so, how to allocate reliance, causation, limitation and quantum issues between phases. Mr Justice Trower, sitting in the Financial List, ordered a split trial: Trial One will determine defendant-side issues (misstatement, omission and director knowledge) and standing, while Trial Two will address claimant-side reliance, causation, limitation and quantum, with disclosure on Trial Two issues required before Trial One begins. As one of the largest FSMA section 90A securities-litigation claims progressing through the Financial List, the case-management approach adopted here is likely to be influential for the conduct of other large multi-claimant securities fraud actions against listed companies.",
  c50: "HMRC determined that certain members of BlueCrest, an investment-management LLP, should be treated as employees rather than self-employed partners under the 'salaried members' anti-avoidance regime (Finance Act 2014, ss 863A-863G ITTOIA), raising approximately £142m in PAYE and £55.3m in NIC assessments across five tax years (2014-2019). The core legal question was how 'significant influence over the affairs of the partnership' under Condition B should be assessed - whether influence flowing from informal, practical or personal-performance-based arrangements could count, or whether only legally enforceable contractual/statutory rights are relevant. The Supreme Court held that qualifying influence must derive exclusively from legally enforceable rights under the LLP agreement and statutory framework, generally requiring influence over strategic or managerial matters rather than operational decisions in a particular area, and remitted the matter to the tribunal below for reconsideration on that basis. The decision is significant for asset managers and other partnership/LLP structures using discretionary or informal governance arrangements, materially narrowing what counts as 'influence' for salaried-member tax purposes.",
  c51: "Part of the long-running 'cum-ex' litigation, this appeal concerned whether a 2022 fraud claim brought by SKAT (the Danish tax authority) against a financial brokerage (over tax vouchers used to support withholding-tax refund applications, 2012-2015) was barred by issue estoppel following the dismissal of an earlier 2018 negligence claim on Revenue Rule grounds (which bars English courts from enforcing foreign tax laws). The legal question was whether the dismissal of the negligence claim gave rise to issue estoppel preventing the later fraud claim from proceeding, given both concerned the same underlying vouchers. The Supreme Court held that issue estoppel only attaches to determinations 'necessary and fundamental' to the earlier decision, and since fraud and negligence involve distinct factual and legal bases, no estoppel arose from the 2018 judgment. The decision is significant for the wider cum-ex/withholding-tax fraud litigation ecosystem, confirming that an adverse ruling on one cause of action will not automatically shut down a parallel fraud claim arising from the same facts.",
  c52: "London Credit Limited had lent £1.881m to residential-property borrowers at a default interest rate of 4% per month following breach of the facility's loan-to-value covenant. The borrowers argued that their without-prejudice settlement offers amounted to a valid tender that should have stopped interest accruing, and separately that the 4%-per-month default rate was penal and unenforceable following Cavendish Square Holding v Talal El Makdessi. The Court of Appeal (Lewison, Newey and Arnold LJJ) held that an offer contingent on resolving a separate dispute over quantum, and unsupported by funds immediately available for payment, cannot constitute a valid tender, and that the elevated default rate was not a penalty because it protected the lender's legitimate interest in the increased refinancing risk created by the covenant breach. The decision reaffirms the post-Cavendish approach to penalty clauses in secured real-estate lending and tightens the law of tender for borrowers seeking to stop interest running by way of a conditional settlement offer, of particular interest to specialist and bridging lenders drafting default-interest provisions.",
  c49: "More than 5,000 individual claimants who financed vehicle purchases brought unfair-relationship claims under sections 140A-B of the Consumer Credit Act 1974 against eight motor finance companies, alleging undisclosed discretionary commission arrangements with dealers in breach of FCA CONC rules. The procedural question was whether such mass claims could properly be brought and managed via single multi-claimant claim forms per defendant under CPR 7.3's 'convenient disposal' test, or whether they needed to be split into individual actions. Lord Justice Coulson, applying and extending the Court of Appeal's approach in Morris v Williams, upheld the High Court's case-management decision permitting the consolidated claim forms, holding that convenient disposal can encompass lead-case and common-issue management even though ultimate unfairness findings remain fact-specific to each claimant. The decision is highly significant for the ongoing wave of motor finance discretionary commission litigation following the FCA's redress scheme, confirming that mass claims of this kind can proceed efficiently through consolidated case management rather than requiring thousands of separate claims.",
  c53: "Culina Group and related companies sued former chief executive Thomas van Mourik and other former directors and executives (including William Stobart and David Meir) for breach of duty in connection with their time running the group. At a case management conference, the claimants sought permission to amend their pleadings to add further bases of liability. Andrew Baker J refused an amendment that sought to found fiduciary duties on a service company merely because it supplied a professional to perform an executive role, holding that supplying personnel does not by itself create a fiduciary relationship. He permitted a separate amendment alleging liability to account for an increase in the value of a shareholding, treating whether such liability can arise as an unsettled point of law better resolved at trial than struck out summarily. Directions were given for a trial listed for 28 February 2028, with extended disclosure and evidence timetabled through 2027, keeping live substantial fiduciary-duty and accounting claims against the former Culina/Stobart group executives.",
  c54: "Gable Insurance AG, an Austrian insurer in liquidation, sued its former CEO and director William Dewsall for breach of duty over payments he authorised during his tenure, totalling £4,957,788.52 for which the trial judge found him liable. The trial judge distinguished between payments diverted from trust accounts, which were found dishonest, and other payments made directly by the company (including to a party called Hogarth), which the judge found were negligent breaches but not dishonest because Dewsall believed the payments were authorised by the relevant boards and disclosed to the auditor. On appeal, Gable Insurance argued this distinction was irrational and that all the payments should have been treated as dishonest. The Court of Appeal dismissed the appeal, holding the trial judge was entitled to draw a rational distinction between the two categories of payment based on Dewsall's state of mind and the surrounding governance context, so the finding of negligence rather than dishonesty for the non-trust payments stood.",
  c55: "The liquidators of The Wine Enterprises Investments Scheme Limited brought a professional-negligence claim against its former auditors, Crowe U.K. LLP, alleging Crowe failed over seven audit years (2012-2018) to detect fraud by the company's directors. The main judgment (27 March 2026) found Crowe liable but awarded only about £102,000 — roughly 1.6% of the £8.4m claimed. In this consequential costs judgment, the court held that Crowe was nonetheless the 'successful party' under the established costs test, awarding Crowe 85% of its costs up to the expiry of its Part 36 offer and the whole of its costs thereafter (plus interest), on the standard rather than indemnity basis. The decision is a reminder to liquidators and other claimants that a nominal or heavily discounted damages recovery can still translate into a substantial adverse costs order if it falls short of a defendant's settlement offer.",
  c56: "Philip Cole and Scott Masterton co-founded E-Technologies Global Limited, and in September 2021 executed a Share Purchase Agreement under which Masterton agreed to buy Cole's shares for £10m, contingent on anticipated third-party investment that never materialised. Masterton repeatedly failed to comply with court orders and engage with the litigation, leading to a debarring order preventing him from advancing a defence; he sought relief from sanctions under the Denton v White framework, citing dyslexia and unrelated criminal proceedings, which the court found unsupported by evidence and insufficient to excuse serious, repeated breaches. Applying CPR 3.9, Hancock J maintained the debarring order but still considered the merits on the available documentary record, since Masterton could not positively advance his sham-agreement defence. The court found the SPA was a genuine, binding agreement breached by Masterton's failure to pay the agreed £10m by the completion date.",
  c57: "Frontiers Capital I Limited Partnership (FCILP), a pre-2009 venture capital fund that ceased business in 2010, had sued Thomas Flohr for breach of a 2002 Subscription and Shareholders' Agreement and breach of fiduciary duty, alleging he improperly used competing 'Comprendium' companies to acquire subsidiaries of the bankrupt Comdisco Inc for his own profit. After FCILP and its dissolved Guernsey general partner (FCGPL) were restored, Timothy Horlick — a former indirect investor holding a 24% stake — became FCGPL's director/liquidator, funded the revived claim via his own company Milo Investments Limited, and stood to receive 90% of any recovery. Flohr applied for a non-party costs order against Horlick personally. The court found Horlick was the true party in interest, having pursued the litigation for personal gain without proper consultation of the limited partners and without candour about the funding arrangement, and ordered costs against him.",
  c58: "Stor RB One had obtained summary judgment in 2023 against Mr Kanthasamy for over £541,000 on a personal guarantee he gave in support of a receivables purchase facility extended to a company run by a relative. Kanthasamy later alleged the guarantee deed was forged and that the earlier proceedings were tainted by a fabricated defence admission and a false solicitor's certificate of independent legal advice, produced by the relative rather than by Stor itself, and sought to set the judgment aside for fraud. Judge Kramer KC refused to strike out the fraud case, finding a real triable issue on forgery, but declined to resolve whether the 'fraud unravels all' principle extends to a co-defendant's fraud (rather than the judgment creditor's), adjourning that novel point pending the Court of Appeal's decision in Lindsay's Estate v Outlook Finance Ltd. The case is of practical significance for lenders relying on personal guarantees where the alleged fraud is perpetrated by someone other than the borrower or creditor.",
  c59: "Twelve standby letters of credit, issued 2017-2020 by Sberbank and confirmed by UniCredit's London branch, secured lease obligations of Russian carriers to Irish lessors Celestial Aviation Services and two Aircastle-group entities. After UK Russia sanctions were imposed, UniCredit refused payment, and the lessors argued the leases and LCs predated the sanctions regime and so fell outside its reach. Lord Stephens, giving the judgment for a unanimous panel, held that Regulation 28(3) of the Russia (Sanctions) (EU Exit) Regulations 2019 requires only a factual connection between financial services or funds and an arrangement whose object or effect makes restricted goods available to a Russia-connected person, dismissing the lessors' appeals and allowing the bank's cross-appeal. The decision is a landmark for trade-finance and letter-of-credit practice under UK sanctions law, confirming that banks can lawfully decline payment under pre-existing instruments where a sufficient factual connection to sanctioned activity exists.",
  c60: "Zedra, a minority shareholder in THG plc, originally filed an unfair-prejudice petition under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006 in 2019 and in 2022 sought to amend it to add allegations concerning share distributions made in July 2016; THG argued the proposed amendment was time-barred. The Court of Appeal had agreed that the amendment was out of time. A five-Justice Supreme Court panel (Lord Hodge, Lord Lloyd-Jones, Lord Briggs, Lord Burrows and Lord Richards) reviewed the position and gave authoritative guidance on how, and whether, a statutory limitation period constrains amendments to section 994 unfair-prejudice petitions. The ruling is significant as one of the few Supreme Court decisions to address limitation in the context of shareholder unfair-prejudice claims, providing clarity for corporate and shareholder-dispute practitioners on when historic conduct can still be pleaded into an existing petition.",
  c61: "Stefensen received 2.5 million shares in Rezolve AI plc under a share purchase agreement but held them on trust pending payment; he sold all the shares without authorisation and then defied four separate court orders (a proprietary injunction, an information order, a debarring order and a post-judgment injunction) requiring him to disclose what had become of the proceeds. Mr Justice Mellor found the breaches deliberate, flagrant and continuing, rejected suspension, and sentenced Stefensen to two years' imprisonment, emphasising the serious prejudice to DBLP's ability to trace the misappropriated funds. The decision illustrates the court's willingness to impose immediate custodial sentences for sustained non-compliance with disclosure orders protecting trust property in share-sale disputes.",
  c62: "Kuwait Finance House PLC applied to set aside a contempt order and default judgment obtained against it in enforcement proceedings, arguing it had never been properly served with the relevant documents and pointing to evidence that the claimant, Hamad Al Haroun, had manipulated a delivery-confirmation record dated 23 June 2023. David Quest KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, found that document manipulation had indeed occurred but held that genuine service had nonetheless taken place on multiple occasions via courier and postal delivery, and that KFH's failure to properly process the documents internally did not undermine the validity of service. The bank's applications to set aside both the contempt order and the default judgment were accordingly dismissed. The decision is a reminder to regulated institutions that internal document-handling failures will not excuse non-compliance with validly served enforcement orders, even where a counterparty's evidence is shown to be partly unreliable.",
  c63: "IlliquidX, an advisory boutique specialising in illiquid and distressed investments, disclosed under NDA in 2019 its strategy to establish a sanctions-compliant fund to monetise undervalued, sanctions-restricted Venezuelan sovereign debt, in connection with a proposed joint venture with Altana Wealth and Brevent Advisory (the Altana IlliquidX Canaima Fund), which subsequently collapsed. Altana went on to launch its own competing fund (ACOF) in 2020, allegedly built on the same confidential strategy. On appeal, Altana and its principals argued the strategy was in the public domain and therefore not protectable as confidential information; the Court of Appeal (Arnold, Zacaroli and Miles LJJ) rejected this, holding that 'public domain' retains its established meaning of general accessibility rather than mere absence of a confidentiality restriction, and that the appellants — who bore the burden of proof — failed to show the information was genuinely public. The decision is a useful appellate restatement of the public-domain threshold in breach-of-confidence claims, directly relevant to fund managers and advisers handling proprietary, sanctions-driven investment strategies.",
  c64: "The claimants sought to lift legal privilege over documents and communications relating to the late Oleg Bourlakov's business interests, invoking the 'iniquity exception' which disapplies privilege where legal advice was sought or given for a fraudulent or otherwise iniquitous purpose. The dispute concerns more than $3bn in disputed business interests and involves complex, multi-jurisdictional corporate structures. The High Court's ruling on the scope of privilege in this asset-tracing context is significant for practitioners advising on fraud and asset-recovery claims involving corporate vehicles, with the substantive trial listed for October 2027.",
  c65: "Tether Investments sought anti-suit relief in the English Commercial Court to restrain a derivative action brought in the BVI courts against Electric Solidus (trading as Swan Bitcoin), relying on a litigation-restriction clause in the parties' shareholders' agreement. The Commercial Court refused the relief, holding that such contractual restrictions on litigation do not extend to bar derivative claims brought on behalf of the company itself, as opposed to direct claims by a shareholder. The decision is relevant to governance and minority-shareholder disputes in crypto and digital-asset joint ventures structured through offshore vehicles.",
  c66: "JJH Enterprises (trading as ValueLicensing) brought a Competition Appeal Tribunal follow-on damages claim against Microsoft over the secondary market for used software licences, alleging Microsoft's contractual and technical restrictions unlawfully blocked resale of bulk licences. Microsoft appealed on jurisdiction and copyright-exhaustion grounds, arguing the CAT could not determine embedded copyright questions and that the Software Directive's exhaustion principle does not cover non-program works such as icons and GUI elements bundled with software, nor the subdivision and resale of bulk licences. The Court of Appeal dismissed the appeal in full, confirming the CAT's jurisdiction over copyright issues arising within a competition claim and rejecting Microsoft's narrow reading of exhaustion, distinguishing the CJEU's UsedSoft ruling on client-server arrangements. The decision is a significant precedent for competition litigators and technology-sector practices tracking the CAT's jurisdictional scope and the limits of contractual restraints on software resale.",
  c67: "Joint liquidators of Eversholt Rail (365) Limited, an SPV holding railway rolling stock with no employees or documents of its own, sought sweeping disclosure of 'everything forever' relating to the company from sister company Eversholt Rail Limited (which held all the group's records without segregation) and its solicitors, Norton Rose Fulbright, under sections 235 and 236 of the Insolvency Act 1986. ICC Judge Burton had dismissed the application at first instance as too widely framed and unjustified, and Sir Anthony Mann rejected all ten grounds of the liquidators' appeal. The court held that while reconstituting a company's corporate knowledge is a legitimate purpose of sections 235-236, that purpose does not itself establish the 'reasonable requirement' the statute demands — officeholders must identify concrete categories of material and articulate why each is reasonably necessary, rather than requesting unlimited access as a matter of course. The decision confirms that sections 235-236 are not a 'blank cheque' and will be of significant practical importance to insolvency practitioners seeking information from affiliated companies and professional advisers.",
  c68: "The Financial Conduct Authority brought a rare contested winding-up petition against Equity for Growth (Securities) Limited, a firm facing approximately £2.7m of consumer redress claims, many already assessed by the Financial Ombudsman Service. Following a two-day trial in December 2025 at which the company's director was cross-examined, ICC Judge Agnello KC scrutinised every line of the company's balance sheet and the extent to which redress liabilities could be met from trading income, insurance or other resources. The judge found for the FCA on almost every point, holding the company both balance-sheet insolvent and, independently, that its combative approach to redress claims and failure to treat customers fairly justified winding up on the 'just and equitable' ground under section 367(3) of FSMA 2000. Following the order, the outstanding redress claims will be administered through the Financial Services Compensation Scheme rather than by the company itself.",
  c69: "BCNO Limited, which operates osteopathic colleges, sought a permanent 'no-access' order under section 117 of the Companies Act 2006 against Mr Cooke, a critic of its decision to close its London campus who had informally emailed requesting a copy of the register of members to lobby against the closure. Mr Cooke's email lacked the address and stated purpose required by section 116(4) CA 2006, and both parties agreed it was not a compliant request, yet the company pressed ahead with litigation seeking to bar him from making this or any future request. HHJ Paul Matthews (sitting as a High Court judge) dismissed the claim on the threshold jurisdictional point, holding that section 117 confers no power to grant relief where the underlying section 116 request was itself invalid — the company had no obligation to comply with, or to litigate against, a request that was not properly made in the first place. The judgment revisits and qualifies the judge's own earlier reasoning on section 117 in Sir Henry Royce Memorial Foundation v Hardy, and stands as a cautionary tale for companies overreacting to informal shareholder-communication requests.",
  c70: "The South African joint liquidators of Mirror Trading International (Pty) Ltd, one of the largest cryptocurrency Ponzi and pyramid schemes in South African history, brought transaction-avoidance claims in England under section 426 of the Insolvency Act 1986, seeking assistance from the English court in aid of the South African insolvency proceeding. A respondent applied to strike out the claims, arguing they were time-barred under the South African Prescription Act 1969 and that their pursuit was an abuse of process. In a 63-page judgment, Mr Justice Leech dismissed the strike-out application, holding that whether the claims were prescribed raised disputed questions of South African law and fact — including expert evidence on foreign limitation principles — that were unsuitable for summary determination at the pleading stage. The decision allows the liquidators' claims to proceed to a full hearing and illustrates the English courts' continued willingness to provide cross-border assistance to foreign liquidators under section 426, including on complex foreign-law limitation defences.",
  c71: "Bridging Finance Inc obtained a bankruptcy order against Anthony Lyons, a Bahamas-resident property investor, in respect of a CAD$39m debt (c.£26.6m) arising under an English-law guarantee. Lyons appealed, arguing the English court lacked jurisdiction because he was not 'carrying on business' in England within section 265 of the Insolvency Act 1986, and that even if jurisdiction existed the court should exercise its discretion against making an order against a foreign debtor. Mr Justice Rajah dismissed the appeal, holding that Lyons' development, letting and eventual sale of a substantial property at 100 Hamilton Terrace, London, through agents, was sufficient to constitute 'carrying on business' even though it was a single venture conducted in a personal capacity. Applying the three-step test from Re Latreefers Inc (No 2) and JSC Bank of Moscow v Kekhman, the judge accepted the court retained a discretion not to make an order against a foreign debtor but declined to exercise it in Lyons' favour, upholding the bankruptcy order.",
  c72: "Investors in the Devas satellite-spectrum venture sought to enforce €195m of arbitral awards against India in the English courts, arguing that India's ratification of the New York Convention 1958 amounted to a waiver of state immunity from enforcement proceedings. The Court of Appeal (Lewison, Newey and Phillips LJJ) disagreed, holding that Article III of the Convention — which requires contracting states to enforce awards 'in accordance with the rules of procedure of the territory where the award is relied upon' — expressly preserves local procedural rules, and that state immunity is such a rule. The court distinguished the New York Convention from the ICSID Convention, whose unqualified enforcement obligation has been held to waive immunity, and noted the decision aligns England with recent Australian and German rulings on the same question. The ruling is significant for investors seeking to enforce international arbitral awards against sovereign states in England, confirming that immunity must be separately and expressly waived rather than inferred from Convention ratification alone.",
  c73: "Members of a joint venture that built the London Array offshore windfarm sought to recover overcharges paid to Nexans Norway for submarine power cables supplied during a 2008–09 tender, following the European Commission's finding that Nexans participated in a worldwide cartel for high- and extra-high-voltage submarine and underground power cables between 2000 and 2009. The Competition Appeal Tribunal upheld the claim, and Nexans sought permission to appeal on four grounds relating to the CAT's construction of the Commission's binding infringement findings, its treatment of evidence about a rival bidder's non-genuine bid, its causation analysis, and its reliance on a 'group-to-group' econometric damages methodology. Lord Justice Green refused permission on all four grounds, holding the CAT had properly construed the Commission decision, that its factual findings were evidentially supported, that no counterfactual bidder needed to be identified to establish causation, and that the averaging methodology was a careful and justified approach to quantifying cartel overcharges. The decision reinforces appellate courts' deference to the CAT's fact-finding and damages methodology in follow-on competition claims.",
  c74: "Lender-side claimants including ICG Manager Limited sued valuer Colliers International Valuation UK LLP for negligence over a January 2018 valuation of a property securing a loan, alleging that the negligent valuation prevented them from identifying a breach of the loan's loan-to-value covenant and from taking steps to avert the losses that followed. On an application to amend the pleadings, the claimants sought to add allegations that Colliers negligently revised its valuation upward — from £22.41m to £23.75m — after communications with the borrower, and that a failure to obtain internal risk-management sign-off was a further breach of duty, while also seeking to plead that causation between the borrower contact and the valuation increase could be inferred. Sitting as a deputy High Court judge, David Halpern KC allowed most of the proposed amendments as arguable, including the inference-based causation case, but refused amendments that sought to recast the valuer's conduct as intentional wrongdoing rather than negligence. The ruling is a reminder that valuers owing duties to lenders can face amended, more specific negligence pleadings well into litigation where the amendments are properly arguable, even as courts continue to police the line between negligence and dishonesty at the pleading stage.",
  c75: "The joint liquidators of The Food Retailer Operations Limited — formerly Somerfield Stores Limited, once one of the UK's largest supermarket chains — brought a claim worth around £205m against Co-operative Group Limited and associated Co-op entities, alleging that a 2015 internal reorganisation known as 'Project Chicago' was a transaction at an undervalue or a preference under sections 238 and 239 of the Insolvency Act 1986. The liquidators argued that Co-op transferred Somerfield's most profitable stores to Co-op subsidiaries and then withdrew approximately £478m of Somerfield's share capital, leaving the company saddled with onerous leases on its remaining, less profitable stores before its 2017 administration. Following a seven-week trial that examined the valuation of pension and guarantee-covenant releases given as part of the restructuring, Mr Justice Cawson dismissed the claim, holding that the share capital withdrawal was supported by consideration — the capital originally subscribed for those shares — so no transaction at an undervalue or preference arose. The judgment is a significant, closely watched decision on the scope of sections 238 and 239 in the context of a pre-insolvency group reorganisation, and a substantial win for Co-op after nearly a decade of dispute over the collapse of Somerfield.",
  "c80": "This is the consequentials ruling following an April 2026 trial on claims by former Finalto executives concerning an Employment and Transfer Scheme and management equity entitlements following a corporate reorganisation/sale, plus a substantial counterclaim. The court rejected a request to reopen valuation findings based on late-disclosed financial statements, awarded interest for the full pre-judgment period, and split costs, giving claimants two-thirds of costs up to the date of a GBP3.5m Part 36 offer and no order thereafter. All six grounds for permission to appeal were refused. The judgment is a good example of post-trial costs/interest mechanics in a high-value corporate earn-out dispute arising from a group restructuring.",
  c81: "Francesco Costa, chairman of Spring Media Investments Ltd, was bound by a shareholders' agreement committing the board to pursue an exit sale of the company by the end of 2019; instead he covertly worked to delay and derail the sale while concealing this from, and misleading, his fellow directors and shareholder Saxon Woods Investments, ultimately causing the company to miss its exit window before Covid-19 destroyed its value. The High Court had dismissed Saxon Woods' unfair-prejudice petition on the footing that Costa subjectively believed his own approach served the company; the Court of Appeal reversed, holding that the section 172(1) Companies Act 2006 duty of good faith requires conduct that is honest by the standards of ordinary people, not merely genuine subjective belief. The Supreme Court (Lord Briggs giving the lead judgment) unanimously dismissed Costa's further appeal, holding that confining good faith to a director's private thought processes 'would be a recipe for chaos and paralysis in corporate governance' — concealment from the board is concealment from the company itself — and upheld the order requiring Costa to buy out Saxon Woods' shareholding at its undiscounted 31 December 2019 valuation. The decision is now the leading authority on the good-faith/loyalty limb of section 172, distinct from but reinforcing the separate honest-commercial-judgment principle under which courts will not otherwise second-guess a director's business decisions.",
  c82: "Anna Okroyan owned Dorchester House in Virginia Water, Surrey, refinanced in October 2022 with a bridging mortgage from West One Loan Ltd of roughly £4m. In December 2023 OFSI designated her a sanctioned person under the Russia (Sanctions) (EU Exit) Regulations 2019, making it unlawful for her to make — and for West One to accept — further mortgage payments. She argued that this unlawfulness suspended her repayment obligation, so no default capable of founding a possession claim ever arose. Edwin Johnson J found the argument intellectually coherent but declined to extend it to possession proceedings, holding sanctions designation does not disable a lender acting under an OFSI licence from enforcing its security, and granted West One possession of the property.",
  c83: "392 former Northern Rock mortgage customers, whose loans passed to TSB's 'Whistletree' book after a 2016 portfolio transfer, brought group litigation alleging TSB's Whistletree standard variable rate breached the express mortgage terms and engaged the unfair-relationship provisions of the Consumer Credit Act 1974. On two preliminary issues, the Court of Appeal (Newey, Baker and Arnold LJJ) upheld the High Court, holding that the mortgage contracts did not require a transferee lender to apply one of its own pre-existing SVRs rather than a new rate set for the acquired book, and that section 140A(5) CCA 1974 precluded relief for regulated mortgage contracts where the complaint was properly attributable to a separate, linked credit agreement. The ruling narrows the claimants' remaining routes to redress in the wider 'mortgage prisoners' litigation against transferee lenders.",
  c84: "IBP Markets Ltd, an FCA-regulated broker, has been in special administration since 2023 under the Investment Bank Special Administration Regulations 2011. A creditor, All Blue Investments North Star 1 Ltd (in liquidation), claims £21.2m as client money under the FCA's CASS client-money rules; the joint special administrators dispute this, contending the sums were instead transferred under a title transfer collateral arrangement (TTCA) that puts them outside the client-money pool. All Blue sought permission to rely on expert evidence analysing trading-data patterns to support its characterisation of the arrangement. Mr Justice Hildyard refused permission, holding the proposed expert evidence had not been shown to be 'reasonably necessary' under CPR 35.1 given the dispute turns on contractual construction and findings of fact rather than matters requiring specialist expertise, but left the door open to a renewed application if supported by a draft expert report, due by 15 September 2026, demonstrating that expert evidence could identify genuine alternative market explanations for the trading patterns relied on.",
  c85: "A procedural sequel to ICG Manager Ltd & Ors v Colliers International Valuation UK LLP [2026] EWHC 1749 (Ch) (c74), in which the court had permitted lender-claimant amendments alleging Colliers' valuation of secured property was influenced by borrower representations. This further ruling dealt with the consequential directions flowing from that permission: the timetable for the defendant to amend its Defence and Reply, and the allocation of costs of the amendment application. Mr David Halpern KC, sitting as a deputy High Court judge, directed that the amendment timetable be fixed at a forthcoming Pre-Trial Review rather than immediately, and — although the claimants had succeeded in obtaining most of the amendments sought — awarded them only half their costs because repeated drafting iterations had unnecessarily increased both sides' costs, with the defendant recovering its own costs of responding to the amendments.",
  c86: "A dispute over an 11.7% shareholding in Sporting Holdings, the owner of the Rajasthan Royals IPL cricket franchise. After India's Supreme Court found Ripu Sudan Kundra ('Raj Kundra') had committed IPL betting offences in 2015, he transferred his shareholding under a Share Transfer Agreement and later entered a 2019 Settlement Agreement receiving payments in exchange for undertakings never to assert franchise interests or sue the shareholders. The claimants terminated the settlement in July 2025, alleging Kundra breached its non-disparagement terms by making public claims to the franchise interest. Griffiths J granted summary judgment for the claimants, declaring both agreements valid and binding, ordering repayment of US$4,937,887 plus interest, and granting injunctions — including a permanent anti-suit injunction — restraining Kundra from pursuing related proceedings in India.",
  c87: "Lars Windhorst, a financier and controlling shareholder/CEO of Tennor Holding BV, was subject to a Part 71 order requiring him to attend court, answer questions and produce documents about Tennor's finances so that ADS Securities could enforce a roughly €27.2m judgment debt after Tennor entered Dutch insolvency proceedings. He repeatedly failed to attend the examination hearings, and the High Court found this a deliberate breach of both the order and his own undertaking to comply, sentencing him to 18 months' imprisonment for contempt, suspended for seven months on conditions including document production. The Court of Appeal (Fraser and Newey LJJ) dismissed all three grounds of his appeal, holding the sanction was proportionate and that a judge may lawfully impose a positive document-production condition as a term of a suspended committal order. The decision is a reminder of the courts' willingness to use contempt powers robustly against individuals who obstruct judgment-enforcement examinations tied to insolvent corporate structures.",
  c88: "Together Commercial Finance had lent £2.4m to a company directed by the defendant's father, secured against Matthew Slack's property under a legal charge. When the company defaulted, Together's receivers moved to sell the charged property to recover the debt. Slack obtained a mental-health crisis moratorium under the Debt Respite Scheme (Breathing Space) Regulations 2020, and argued this prevented enforcement action against his property. HHJ Klein, sitting as a High Court judge in the Business and Property Courts in Leeds, held that a Breathing Space moratorium protects only 'qualifying debts' owed personally by the moratorium debtor — it does not extend to a separate corporate debt merely because the debtor's own property secures that corporate debt via a third-party charge. The receivers were therefore free to proceed with the sale despite the moratorium. The judgment gives secured lenders useful clarity on the limited reach of Debt Respite Scheme protections where security is granted by a guarantor or third-party chargor rather than the primary corporate debtor.",
  c89: "Cedar Mundi (Holding) SAL, a Lebanese joint-stock company operating a venture-capital fund formed in 2015 by Mundi Ventures SL and the IFA Group and funded via a 2016 private placement, sued its former director Bassel Attieh and related parties over two transfers of substantially the fund's entire investment portfolio — first to IFA Capital Ltd and then, under a 2021 English-law sale-and-purchase agreement, to Cedar II Fund LP — alleging these disposals were unauthorised and effected without proper shareholder or governance approval. Mr Justice Bryan, following a multi-week trial in the Commercial Court, held that the transfers breached the director's fiduciary duties and did not comply with the governance requirements for disposing of substantially all of a fund's assets. The judgment is a reminder of the fiduciary and procedural constraints on those controlling fund vehicles when transferring a fund's entire asset base to affiliated entities.",
  c90: "Yieldpoint had invested US$5m in a one-year participation in a loan Kimura had advanced to a Chilean copper-mining borrower, Minera Tres Valles SPA; when the loan matured in 2022 the borrower had defaulted, and the parties disputed the redemption value Kimura owed under a fair-market-value formula whose methodology the contract left unspecified (a point the Court of Appeal had already confirmed governed the payment, rather than the full US$5m principal). Rosalind Phelps KC, sitting as a judge of the Commercial Court, valued the participation by reference to Kimura's own contemporaneous internal credit and recovery assessments, treating Kimura — realistically the only prospective purchaser of such an unusual distressed asset — as the appropriate benchmark, and fixed fair market value at US$3,115,000, holding Kimura had breached its obligation by failing to pay that sum at maturity. The judgment gives rare guidance on valuing distressed loan participations where the contract is silent on methodology, of particular relevance to funds holding illiquid credit participations.",
  c91: "Elevate Estates Management Ltd, a residential property management company, held 51 leases granted by landlord Fine Star Ltd between April 2025 and February 2026, under which it sublet the properties but fell into substantial rent arrears — £836,557.23 by the petition date of 23 June 2026, rising to £1,120,586.87 by 7 July 2026 as payments largely ceased from May 2026. Facing a winding-up petition from Fine Star, Elevate applied for a validation order under section 127 of the Insolvency Act 1986 and to restrain advertisement of the petition, asserting £1.76m of disputed uncredited payments and a £334,376.23 cross-claim for alleged repair costs. Chief Insolvency and Companies Court Judge Briggs dismissed both applications, holding that Elevate's claims were unsubstantiated by contemporaneous documentation and that there was no genuine or substantial dispute as to the underlying debt, and ordered Elevate to pay costs on the indemnity basis. The judgment is a reminder that unparticularised set-off or cross-claims, unsupported by contemporaneous evidence, will not defeat an otherwise undisputed winding-up petition.",
  c92: "GLAS, as trustee for €250m of exchangeable bonds issued by Luxembourg company European Topsoho (ETS, the former holding vehicle for SMCP shares), sued over the October 2021 transfer of ETS's remaining unpledged SMCP shares — worth roughly €85m — to Dynamic Treasure for a nominal €1, arranged by ETS's manager Ms Qiu shortly before ETS's insolvency. The defendants claimed the shares were already validly charged under a 2018 agreement with a Xinbo entity and a related trust arrangement. The Commercial Court found the 2018 agreement, the Qiu-Xinbo trust document and the 2021 transfer notices were not genuine documents created at their stated dates but were fabricated afterwards to justify the disposal, so the transfer was an unauthorised transaction at a substantial undervalue made to place ETS's remaining assets beyond its creditors' reach. A significant illustration of the English courts' willingness to draw adverse inferences and unpick backdated security documentation in cross-border insolvency-linked asset-recovery litigation.",
  c93: "Fraudsters using a stolen identity opened an account with electronic-money institution Moorwand and tricked the Hamblins into transferring £160,000 into it, believing they were making a genuine investment; the money passed through the account of RND Global Ltd, a shell company controlled by the fraudsters. The Hamblins brought a derivative claim on RND's behalf against Moorwand, arguing that under the Quincecare duty Moorwand should have questioned the payment instructions before acting on them and so should restore the funds. The trial judge agreed and ordered Moorwand to repay £160,000, but the Court of Appeal allowed Moorwand's appeal, holding the judge's underlying factual finding — that nothing about the instructions or account activity put Moorwand on inquiry — was one it was reasonably entitled to reach, and reversing the order on that basis. The decision reinforces how high the appellate bar is for disturbing a first-instance Quincecare assessment and underlines that payment institutions are not liable merely because their customer turns out to be a fraudster's vehicle.",
  c94: "Three R Tourism and Hospitality Limited and two individuals sued Orrick Herrington & Sutcliffe (UK) LLP for professional negligence, alleging the firm gave inadequate legal advice on a complex investment transaction under which Carlyle Group acquired shares in Wakanow.com Limited, a travel-booking business, causing the claimants economic loss. Orrick applied to strike out the claim and for summary judgment, arguing it was time-barred under the six-year primary limitation period in the Limitation Act 1980 because any actionable loss crystallised when the relevant agreements were signed in November 2018, well outside the limitation window by the time proceedings were issued. Mr Justice Cotter rejected the strike-out and summary-judgment application, agreeing that damage accrued on execution of the November 2018 agreements rather than on the later service of a conversion notice, but holding it was properly arguable that the claimants did not have constructive knowledge of their claim until later under section 14A of the Limitation Act 1980, so the limitation question requires a full trial. Herbert Smith Freehills Kramer acted for Orrick, the defendant firm.",
  c96: "Salinas Pliego and a co-claimant sued Astor Asset Management and others over stock-lending facilities of around US$115m secured against roughly US$415m of Grupo Elektra shares, alleging the defendants had defrauded them. During the litigation the claimants engaged private investigators, Black Cube, to run a covert sting operation on the defendants' solicitor at DWF Law LLP, deceiving them into disclosing privileged and confidential litigation-strategy information. The Court of Appeal upheld the first-instance finding that this was a serious abuse of process, dismissed the claimants' appeal against the striking-out of their summary judgment application, and allowed the defendants' cross-appeal discharging the freezing orders that had been in place, though the underlying fraud claim proceeds to trial. The decision is a significant marker on the limits of covert investigative tactics in commercial litigation and the consequences of obtaining privileged material by deception.",
  c95: "RockRose UKCS8 LLC (UKCS8), part of the TAQA Bratani/Spirit Energy group, paid a dividend of roughly US$84m to its parent shortly before UKCS8 itself was sold to Fujairah International Oil & Gas Corporation for a nominal US$1, as part of a wider group reorganisation. The purchaser's group later challenged the dividend under section 238 of the Insolvency Act 1986 as a transaction at an undervalue by an insolvent company, raising the question whether the relevant 'transaction' for section 238 purposes was the dividend alone or the broader arrangement of which the share sale formed part, and whether the section 238(5)(b) defence could be satisfied by pointing to benefits flowing to the wider group or shareholders rather than to UKCS8 itself. The Court of Appeal (Falk and Newey LJJ, Sir Geoffrey Vos MR) held that a company must itself be a party to the impugned dealing for it to be 'the transaction', so the dividend — not the surrounding sale — was the relevant transaction, and that the section 238(5)(b) defence must be judged from the paying company's own perspective, requiring particularly compelling justification once it is insolvent. The judgment tightens the undervalue-transaction defence available to corporate groups that extract value via dividends ahead of a distressed disposal.",
  c97: "Ciright, a US information-technology company, transferred roughly US$3.4m into the bank account of Centili, a UK information-technology company, in January 2026. Ciright says the money was held on bare trust for a specific purpose and seeks its return; Centili says the funds were instead an advance under a facility agreement and so are its own money to use. Ciright obtained a without-notice interim proprietary injunction freezing the disputed funds pending trial, applying the American Cyanamid test for interim relief, but Centili argued the injunction should be discharged because Ciright had failed to disclose material facts about the status of the facility agreement when it applied. Mr David Rees KC, sitting as a deputy High Court judge, found there had been material non-disclosure but exercised his discretion to continue the injunction anyway in the interests of justice, instead sanctioning Ciright's conduct through adverse and penal costs orders. The decision illustrates the court's willingness to preserve disputed funds pending trial even where the without-notice applicant has fallen short of its disclosure duties, provided a costs sanction adequately marks the breach.",
  c98: "China Triumph International Engineering Co Ltd (CTIEC), a Chinese engineering company, claims the WeLink Global group of companies assumed roughly £17.2m of debts under loan agreements entered into in April 2019 relating to three UK solar-farm projects. The WeLink defendants say a December 2021 memorandum of understanding fundamentally restructured the repayment obligations and that CTIEC itself breached commitments made under that later agreement, and sought to join two further companies, Project Green Town Limited and Orcus Solar Park 1 SL, as additional defendants so they could bring related counterclaims within the same proceedings. Paul Stanley KC, sitting as a deputy High Court judge in the Commercial Court, allowed most of the defendants' pleading amendments to run their MOU-based defence and counterclaim, but refused the application to join the two further companies, holding that widening the litigation in that way would unnecessarily complicate the proceedings and that any claims those companies wished to bring were better pursued independently. The ruling is a routine but useful illustration of the court's reluctance to expand loan-restructuring disputes to accommodate satellite claims by non-parties.",
  c100: "Amrik Singh Saini and the late Jagdeep Singh Pandher formed a joint venture through BS Enterprises Limited to acquire and operate 'The Thorns Inn' public house, each holding a 50% stake, but their relationship broke down in 2016 and Jagdeep died intestate before a planned buy-out of Saini's shares could be finalised. Jagdeep's widow, Inderjit Kaur Pandher, then took control of the company and let the pub premises to operating companies she and a cousin owned, at rents below market value and without formal lease agreements, while excluding Saini from management and withholding information about the restructuring. ICC Judge Greenwood upheld Saini's unfair-prejudice petition under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006, finding Pandher breached her fiduciary duties through undisclosed conflicts of interest, unauthorised dealings with connected companies and a failure to inform Saini of material changes, and ordered a buyout of his shares as the appropriate remedy. A useful illustration of how courts police self-dealing and information-withholding by a controlling director in small, deadlocked owner-managed companies.",
  c101: "Argo Blockchain plc, a Nasdaq-listed cryptocurrency-mining company, proposed a Part 26A restructuring plan to address US$40m of senior unsecured notes and its Growler secured facility, restructuring the debt via an equity-for-debt structure that transferred value to Growler Mining Tuscaloosa LLC while addressing the position of retail noteholders and existing shareholders. Hildyard J sanctioned the plan, applying cross-class cram-down against dissenting classes and giving one of the first post-Petrofac rulings on how any restructuring surplus should be allocated as between creditors and out-of-the-money shareholders. The case was notable as the first English restructuring plan used for a Nasdaq-listed company without loss of its listing and the first for a crypto-mining business, and the sanction order was subsequently recognised and enforced under US Chapter 15. South Square counsel (Matthew Abraham and Rabin Kok, instructed by Fladgate) appeared for the plan company.",
  c109: "Two claimants transferred roughly £10.5m into what they believed was a legitimate, London-run cryptoasset trading business operating under the names Raliplen and Servelius, but the operation was in fact a deceit-based fraud and their assets were misappropriated and dissipated through a chain of cryptoasset addresses controlled by unidentified 'persons unknown'. Having already obtained proprietary and freezing relief earlier in the proceedings, the claimants applied for summary judgment against the second category of defendants — the addresses that received the misappropriated assets — on the basis there was no real prospect of a defence given the unrepresented defendants' failure to engage with the claim. Mr Justice Bright granted summary judgment for deceit, ordering the return of the claimants' Bitcoin holdings as identifiable non-fungible property and monetary compensation for the fungible USD Coin and Ethereum that could no longer be traced to specific coins, and awarded indemnity costs of £230,000 given the defendants' conduct. The decision is a further example of the Commercial Court's willingness to grant final relief, including proprietary remedies over specific cryptoassets, against anonymous fraudsters identified only as categories of 'persons unknown'.",
  c110: "Kevin Lee Rice sold Core Management Group Limited, an umbrella payroll company, under a share purchase agreement that entitled him to further 'Stretch Value Payments' calculated by reference to a defined measure of 'New Revenue' generated after completion. The buyer, Napril Holding Ltd, argued that 'New Revenue' should be read narrowly — subject to a 25% quarterly growth threshold and excluding revenue from clients who merely maintained, rather than actively renewed, their existing business — which would have sharply reduced the earn-out due. Mr Justice Sweeting rejected that construction on the buyer's summary judgment/strike-out application, holding that 'New Revenue' as defined in Schedule 7 of the SPA covered all revenue generated through the company's ordinary business activity, including revenue from clients renewing existing arrangements, and that the buyer's narrower reading had no realistic prospect of succeeding at trial. Judgment was accordingly entered for Rice, illustrating the courts' willingness to give summary effect to the plain, purposive meaning of earn-out definitions in SPAs rather than allow a strained reading to defeat a seller's contractual entitlement.",
  c111: "Xiong Wei and a co-claimant sought to enforce a Chinese judgment for approximately US$2.13 million plus interest against Wang Junhong in the English Commercial Court, arising from a dispute over whether the defendant was entitled to acquire shares in a subsidiary, Syner Ltd, without payment or was instead owed compensation for a prior loan. The defendant resisted enforcement on the grounds that the Chinese judgment lacked finality because of a pending supervisory retrial application, and that the Chinese proceedings breached natural justice by failing to consider auditor evidence on share valuation. Mr Andrew Hochhauser KC, sitting as a deputy High Court judge, rejected both defences, holding the judgment was final and conclusive under English conflict-of-laws principles despite the pending supervisory review, and that no breach of natural justice arose since the defendant had strategically withheld the auditor evidence rather than being denied an opportunity to adduce it. The decision is a useful illustration of the English courts' narrow approach to finality and natural-justice challenges to enforcement of Chinese judgments in shareholder/corporate disputes.",
  c112: "E J W Builders Limited and its principal, Eamonn Wynne, appealed against the dismissal of their claim that a partnership or contractual joint venture existed between them and Audrey and Edward Marshall over the redevelopment of a site in Trowbridge previously run as the Hilbury Court Hotel. HHJ Paul Matthews had dismissed the claim in an ex tempore judgment at the close of a two-day trial, finding no such arrangement was ever formed. The Court of Appeal (Falk, Warby and Miles LJJ) held that the appeal amounted to a challenge to the trial judge's evaluation of the facts and evidence, not any error of legal principle, and applying the established appellate-deference principles for such challenges, found the appellants had not surmounted the resulting high hurdle. The appeal was accordingly dismissed, leaving the first-instance finding that no partnership or joint venture existed undisturbed.",
  c113: "Kession Capital Ltd was an FCA-authorised firm permitted to advise on and arrange deals in investments only for professional clients, not retail clients. Under a 2015 Appointed Representative Agreement, Kession appointed Jacob Hopkins McKenzie Ltd (JHM) as its appointed representative, expressly restricted to the same professional-clients-only scope, but JHM went on to deal with retail clients who later brought claims against Kession as principal under section 39(3) FSMA 2000. The Court of Appeal majority had held Kession liable regardless of the AR agreement's restriction, on the basis that JHM remained an appointed representative for all its activities. The Supreme Court (Lord Richards giving the unanimous judgment) allowed Kession's appeal, holding that dealing with retail clients is a distinct 'part' of a financial services business for section 39 purposes, and a principal who has not accepted responsibility for that part — because its AR was contractually barred from it — is not liable for the AR's unauthorised conduct within it. The decision gives principal firms a clearer, more reliable basis for limiting their section 39 exposure by contractually restricting the client categories or business lines their appointed representatives may serve.",
  c114: "Mr and Mrs Webster and Mr and Mrs Sood each controlled 47.6% of the shares in ESMS Global Ltd, with the remaining 4.8% held by a Guernsey-based trustee administering the company's employee benefit trust, leaving the board deadlocked. The Websters sought to break the deadlock by requiring the company, under section 292 of the Companies Act 2006, to circulate a written resolution proposing to appoint an additional director, but the Soods refused to approve circulation. HHJ Cadwallader, sitting as a judge of the High Court, held that the court has jurisdiction to grant injunctive relief compelling a company to circulate a member-requested written resolution where the board has failed to comply with a valid request under sections 292-293, confirming that the statutory right to require circulation is directly enforceable rather than merely aspirational. The decision gives minority and deadlocked shareholders in private companies a practical route to force resolutions to a vote without needing to convene a general meeting, and has been described by practitioners as opening a new option for resolving board deadlocks.",
  c116: "The Secretary of State for Business and Trade brought disqualification proceedings under section 6 of the Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 against Alexander David Greensill, a director connected with the insolvencies of Greensill Capital (UK) Limited and its subsidiary Greensill Limited, alleging (among other things) that between November and December 2020 he caused the companies to enter into transactions with SoftBank Group companies to the detriment of investors in construction firm Katerra, and that from August 2020 he made misrepresentations and non-disclosures to the boards about the status of trade credit insurance. Mr Greensill applied to strike out the proceedings, arguing the Secretary of State needed to show as a threshold matter that his conduct caused the companies' insolvency and that the process of preparing the case for trial had been unfair. Mr Justice Trower dismissed the strike-out application in full, holding that causation of insolvency is not a precondition under the 1986 Act and that no unfairness in case preparation had been established. The ruling clears the way for a six-week trial and is a reminder that disqualification claims connected to the high-profile collapse of Greensill Capital can proceed even without proof that the alleged misconduct itself caused the group's failure.",
  c117: "Gardner Aerospace Holdings Limited, whose subsidiaries manufacture aerospace parts, was acquired in 2017 by Ligeance Aerospace Technology (LAT), a Chinese company listed on the Shenzhen Stock Exchange, subject to a National Security Deed. Mr Upton joined as CFO in 2018, became a director, and in 2021 became interim CEO; in January 2022 LAT's owner agreed a board-approved debt-for-equity swap that would increase the shareholding of a Chinese state-owned investment fund from 14% to 32%. Gardner alleged that, while outwardly supporting the transaction, Mr Upton unilaterally and secretly lobbied Members of Parliament to subject it to close governmental scrutiny, aiming to extract investment and job guarantees, without board authority to do so. Mr Justice Richard Smith found Mr Upton in breach of his duties under sections 171 (acting within powers), 172 (promoting the success of the company) and 175 (avoiding conflicts of interest) of the Companies Act 2006 across all four heads of alleged misconduct, with a further trial to follow on whether the breaches caused Gardner any loss.",
  c149: "Sucden Financial brought a fraud and deceit claim alleging it was induced to forbear from enforcing a debt owed by TMT Metals over a fraudulent bill of lading purportedly covering a cargo of nickel cathodes, and sought to establish the English court's jurisdiction over defendants outside the jurisdiction. The defendants challenged jurisdiction, arguing the tort gateway under the Civil Procedure Rules was not satisfied because the relevant loss was not sustained in England. The Court of Appeal upheld jurisdiction under the Tort Damage Gateway, applying the reasoning in Dolphin Maritime v Swedish Club that where a debt induced to be forgone was itself payable in England, the resulting loss is sustained in England for jurisdictional purposes. Macfarlanes acted for Sucden as the successful appellant, and the decision reinforces the reach of the tort gateway for financial-institution claims arising from fraud connected to England-payable debts.",
  c153: "Clifford Stewart Lay and Jacqueline Suzanne Lay sold their shares in Easy Direct Debits Ltd (EDD) to Independent Vetcare Limited (IVL) under a share purchase agreement; IVL later counterclaimed that the Lays had breached warranties by operating EDD's veterinary-practice direct-debit business without the FCA authorisation it required. In concurrent employment tribunal proceedings brought by Mr Lay after his dismissal, however, IVL had taken the opposite position, asserting EDD's business operated entirely lawfully and needed no FCA authorisation. The Court of Appeal (Andrews and Cockerill LJJ, Bean LJ) allowed the Lays' appeal and struck out the warranty counterclaim, holding that IVL's inconsistent stances on the same regulatory issue across the two sets of proceedings amounted to impermissible 'approbation and reprobation' and an abuse of process. The decision is a reminder that a party cannot adopt contradictory positions on the same compliance question in parallel litigation arising from a single transaction merely for tactical advantage.",
  c154: "IPJSC United Company Rusal, one of the two largest shareholders in PJSC MMC Norilsk Nickel, applied for letters of request under CPR 34.13 to obtain witness evidence from four witnesses in Cyprus and one in Germany, for use in its Commercial Court dispute with Whiteleave Holdings, Vladimir Potanin, Crispian Investments and Roman Abramovich over the framework shareholders' agreement governing Norilsk Nickel — a dispute heading to a 14-week trial in 2027. Mr Justice Bright declined to approve the letters as drafted, objecting to proposals for English counsel to conduct the overseas examinations and to the absence of a Greek translation of the Cyprus letter. He indicated the requests could proceed only once English-counsel involvement was removed and the letters were properly translated so that local judicial authorities could manage execution under their own procedure. The ruling illustrates the practical friction English litigants face in obtaining cross-border evidence through the Hague Evidence Convention machinery in large shareholder disputes.",
  c155: "Paul Andrew Whittaker, former chair and a 25% shareholder of documentary-film company Dogwoof Ltd, was suspended, dismissed and removed as a director between 2021 and 2022 after alleging whistleblower retaliation, claims later dismissed as time-barred by the employment tribunal; fellow shareholder Bertha UK Limited then obtained charging orders over his 28,556 Dogwoof shares to recover about £1.22m of costs from that litigation, and Deputy Master Jefferis ordered the shares sold to satisfy them. On Whittaker's appeal, Mr Justice Leech granted permission to appeal on two of six grounds — the valuation methodology applied to the shares and the relevance of the whistleblowing allegations to that valuation — while dismissing the rest. The case shows the court's willingness to scrutinise the methodology used to value private-company shares for charging-order enforcement, especially where the underlying costs liability stems from unresolved whistleblowing allegations.",
  c156: "Crossbaron Ltd served a statutory demand on Providence Gate Group Holdings Ltd for a sum in excess of £7m on 23 September 2025, making clear the company would face a winding-up petition unless it took action; the company applied to restrain presentation of any such petition, arguing the underlying debt was genuinely disputed on substantial grounds and that presenting a petition would in any event be an abuse of process. An interim undertaking not to present a petition was given at a short-notice hearing on 24 October 2025 pending full argument. HHJ Stephen Davies, sitting in the Business and Property Courts' Insolvency and Companies List, reviewed the evidence on both the underlying dispute and the abuse-of-process ground and dismissed the company's application, declining to restrain Crossbaron from presenting a petition. The decision is a reminder that restraining a creditor from presenting a winding-up petition requires a genuine, substantial dispute over the debt itself — a bare assertion of dispute or complaint about the creditor's conduct will not suffice.",
  c157: "Kevin-Gerald Stanford, a co-founder of AllSaints, sold shares in All Saints Retail Ltd to Klotho Brands Ltd under a 2011 sale agreement and a 2012 settlement; in April 2022 Deputy Judge Ambrose declared that Klotho held good legal and beneficial title to those shares, expressly rejecting Stanford's allegation that he had been fraudulently induced into the agreements, and Stanford did not appeal. In November 2025 Stanford issued fresh proceedings seeking to reopen the question, framing his claim as challenging only the 'scope' of the 2022 declaration rather than its correctness and arguing the factual basis for Klotho's title had never been properly adjudicated. Klotho and its co-founder Lyndon Lea applied to strike out the new claim, and Amanda Hardy KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, held that in substance it remained a challenge to property rights in the shares — a cause of action that had vested automatically in the Official Receiver when Stanford was made bankrupt in May 2023 over unpaid costs from the earlier litigation, so Stanford had no standing to bring it. The judge struck out the claim and imposed an extended civil restraint order against Stanford.",
  c158: "Float Capital Limited's joint liquidators, Geoffrey Bouchier and Ben Woodthorpe, had their remuneration approved by Chief ICC Judge Briggs on 24 July 2026 in [2026] EWHC 1891 (Ch), fixing one liquidator's fees on a time-costs basis and the other's on an uplifted basis under rules 18.24/18.28 of the Insolvency Rules. The Court of Appeal's intervening decision in Frost v The Good Box Co Labs Limited [2025] EWCA Civ 252 then narrowly construed rule 18.24, so that it could not be used to increase remuneration already fixed on a time-costs basis — undermining part of the earlier order's legal basis. Because rule 18.30's creditor-approval machinery could not operate effectively here (the 98%-by-value creditor had a conflict of interest and remaining creditors were passive), ICC Judge Briggs held the court could instead rely on its general supervisory jurisdiction under section 112 of the Insolvency Act 1986 to fix the liquidators' remuneration, discharging the earlier order and re-confirming the same fees on that alternative legal footing. The decision illustrates how the court can fall back on its inherent section 112 jurisdiction to police office-holder remuneration where the Insolvency Rules' ordinary approval routes are unavailable or, as here, subsequently narrowed by appellate authority.",
  c178: "HSJ Consultancy Limited went into liquidation and its liquidators sued two former directors, Jackson and Hughes, over the company's 2010 participation in an employee benefit trust and an employer-financed retirement benefits scheme — arrangements the liquidators characterised as aggressive tax-avoidance planning that extracted roughly £436,500 as purported loans to the directors, which were never repaid. ICC Judge Mullen held the directors breached their duty under section 172 of the Companies Act 2006, finding the creditor duty was engaged because the schemes were entered into with considerable doubt over their effectiveness, no independent advice was taken, and no responsible reserve was maintained against the risk they would fail. The judge rejected the directors' reliance on the Duomatic principle (no informed shareholder ratification) and on section 1157 relief, and held the claim was not time-barred because section 21(1)(b) of the Limitation Act 1980 applied to the fraudulent breach of trust involved in the unrepaid loans. Neither side's solicitors (Wedlake Bell for the liquidators; Neil Davies & Partners for the respondents) is a tracked firm; the decision is a further instalment in a line of authority holding directors personally liable for facilitating EBT/EFRBS extraction schemes that leave a company unable to meet its creditors.",
  c179: "Three shipping companies advanced fraudulent-misrepresentation claims in England against the Export-Import Bank of China over ship-finance arrangements worth around US$61m, while the Chinese lender pressed parallel proceedings in Singapore; each side asked the Commercial Court to shut down the other's forum — the lender seeking a stay of the English claim, the borrowers an anti-suit injunction against Singapore. The outcome turned on an asymmetric exclusive jurisdiction clause in the parties' loan agreement, which confined the borrowers to suing in England while leaving the lender free to sue elsewhere, including concurrently. Mr Justice Bright held that construing the clause by its commercial purpose — facilitating the lender's enforcement against the borrowers' assets wherever located — meant it expressly contemplated parallel English and Singapore litigation, so neither a stay nor an anti-suit injunction was warranted; both applications were dismissed and the judge urged the parties to cooperate pragmatically across the two forums. The ruling is a useful illustration of how English courts will give full effect to deliberately asymmetric jurisdiction clauses common in cross-border ship- and trade-finance lending, rather than treating them as requiring single-forum litigation.",
  c187: "SQN Global Limited, a hire-purchase creditor of insolvent haulage operator Genesis (2014) UK Ltd, brought fraudulent-trading claims under section 213 of the Insolvency Act 1986 against the company's directors after obtaining a worldwide freezing order in May 2021, following which the defendants allegedly transferred around 112 trailers and made over £411,000 of cash payments to an associated entity, CRVS, in breach of the order. ICC Judge Mullen held that de facto/de jure directors Marcus Hughes, Tracy Greening and David Hughes deliberately dissipated the company's assets to defeat creditors, meeting the dishonesty threshold for section 213 and rendering each liable for the full loss, while a transport manager was liable only for the value channelled through CRVS from June 2021. Claims against an accountant and a director who had resigned before the relevant conduct were dismissed for want of evidence of knowing participation, none of the defendants having appeared or been represented at trial. The judgment is a further illustration of the courts' willingness to impose personal liability on directors and associates for asset-stripping carried out in defiance of a freezing order.",
  c189: "Steven Orton had already received partial redress from Barclays in 2019 over mis-sold PPI before bringing a further consumer-credit claim for undisclosed broker commissions, worth around £2,750 net of the earlier redress; twelve days before trial he discontinued after correspondence from Barclays made clear that instructing counsel would eliminate any net recovery given the small sum at stake, and the County Court at Middlesbrough ordered him to pay Barclays' costs as unreasonable conduct under CPR 27.14(2)(g). The Court of Appeal allowed Orton's appeal and set aside the costs order, holding that the small-claims track's 'costs neutral' regime must heavily inform any finding of unreasonableness, that discontinuing for legitimate commercial reasons is not itself unreasonable, and that Barclays' pre-trial correspondence amounted to ultimata rather than genuine settlement invitations reasonably open to acceptance. The judgment is a useful appellate reminder that lower courts should not import relief-from-sanctions-style reasoning into small-claims costs decisions. Simmons & Simmons acted for Barclays as respondent.",
  c190: "Glint Pay Ltd had granted Brahma Finance a debenture over its assets to secure a £1.65m loan in January 2019; after Niven Alpha acquired the loan and declared an event of default when Glint refused to provide requested financial information, Niven appointed administrators in September 2019, and Glint refinanced and repaid the loan within weeks. More than four years later Glint sued, alleging the administrators' appointment was invalid because it was made for the improper collateral purpose of enabling parties connected to the appointor to acquire the business rather than to achieve a proper administration purpose. The Court of Appeal allowed Glint's appeal against strike-out, holding that the debenture's information-provision covenant extended broadly to the financial data Niven had sought (so an arguable breach existed), that there was a realistic prospect the appointment was improperly motivated — which could render it void — and that estoppel and abuse-of-process doctrines could not be used to shut out the claim at the strike-out stage. The decision is a reminder that even years after the event, an administrators' appointment can be challenged where there is a real prospect the appointor's true purpose was collateral to the statutory scheme.",
  c195: "QuidPay Finance's earlier win on the central contractual point — that OpenPayd's reserve-account clause did not survive termination of their agreement — left outstanding questions of costs and consequential relief, which Lance Ashworth KC resolved in this further judgment. He ordered OpenPayd to pay over the disputed reserve funds of €2,453,717.89 and £7,000,000 with interest at 1% above the Bank of England base rate (4.75%) running from 27 May 2026, and dealt with costs from two earlier stages of the litigation: QuidPay recovered its costs of the preliminary-issue trial in full, but only 65% of the costs of its earlier injunction application, the reduction reflecting unsustainable allegations QuidPay had advanced about the beneficial ownership of the reserved funds. The ruling illustrates how English courts apportion costs issue-by-issue in commercial litigation where a party succeeds on the central point but pursued some unsustainable subsidiary arguments along the way.",
  c197: "Alliance Petrochemical Investment (Singapore) Pte Ltd (API) alleged that Francesco Mazzagatti and a related entity, Viaro Energy Limited, misappropriated roughly €143.8m from it by diverting customer payments, siphoning profits through intermediaries and making unauthorised bank withdrawals, and API sought to bolster its case with worldwide freezing and proprietary injunctions plus third-party disclosure from API's own former financial adviser, H&P Advisory Limited, covering due-diligence materials from Viaro's RockRose acquisition. Lionel Persey KC, sitting as a Deputy High Court Judge, dismissed the defendants' application to strike out API's allegations concerning an allegedly forged Abu Dhabi loan facility and related Italian proceedings, holding they remained properly pleaded and relevant to the wider fraud case, and ordered H&P Advisory to give third-party disclosure of the first eight categories of RockRose-related financial due-diligence documents sought. However, the judge refused API's application for a worldwide freezing order — citing delay in bringing it and insufficient fresh evidence of a real risk of asset dissipation — and dismissed the proprietary injunction application outright. The judgment is a reminder that even well-pleaded fraud allegations will not automatically unlock freezing relief where the applicant has delayed and cannot point to new evidence of dissipation risk.",
  c198: "The applicants — investors in a failed investment in Drecford Holdings Limited — sought pre-action disclosure from Craig Coughlan under CPR 31.16 to help formulate an anticipated claim for fraudulent misrepresentation and unlawful means conspiracy over how the investment was procured. Coughlan resisted disclosure on several grounds: that the Abu Dhabi Global Market (ADGM) was a more appropriate forum for any dispute, that the applicants' claims were too speculative to justify pre-action disclosure, and that at least some of the requested documents were not within his control. Master Clark rejected each objection, finding the applicants' case was sufficiently articulated to meet the CPR 31.16 threshold, that forum arguments did not defeat an application confined to disclosure rather than substantive proceedings, and that disclosure was desirable both to help the parties fairly dispose of anticipated proceedings without full litigation and to save costs — so the order was made, subject to some limitations on scope. The decision illustrates how readily the English courts will use pre-action disclosure to test the strength of a prospective cross-border investment-fraud claim before proceedings are issued.",
  c204: "Garden House Software Ltd's insolvent estate had assigned valuable intellectual property to connected individuals in 2017 for no adequate consideration; the court had already found this to be both a transaction at an undervalue and a transaction defrauding creditors under sections 238 and 423 of the Insolvency Act 1986. In this quantum judgment, Mr Justice Fancourt fixed the sum recoverable from the former directors at just over £2.03m, reflecting the value improperly stripped from the company, alongside associated findings on breach of directors' duties. The case is a straightforward but useful illustration of how English courts quantify recoveries once liability for undervalue transactions and creditor-defrauding dispositions has been established, rather than breaking new legal ground.",
  c206: "A company facing a threatened winding-up petition, founded on an unsatisfied statutory demand, applied to restrain its presentation on the basis that the underlying debt was genuinely disputed. HHJ Kelly, sitting in the Business and Property Courts in Leeds, granted the injunction restraining presentation of the petition but declined to make any order as to costs, reflecting a mixed outcome on the wider dispute. The case reaffirms the well-established principle that winding-up procedure is not to be used as a means of debt collection where the debt is bona fide disputed on substantial grounds.",
};

